window.EPPP_TESTS["practice-exam-6"] = {"name": "Practice Exam 6", "slug": "practice-exam-6", "category": "Full Tests", "questionCount": 225, "questions": [{"question": "Longitudinal studies indicate that individuals who receive a diagnosis of Borderline Personality Disorder in adolescence or early adulthood often exhibit a reduction or remission in symptoms over time. However, recovery is dependent on symptom type, with _________ symptoms showing the least amount of age-related improvement.", "options": {"A": "impulsive", "B": "interpersonal", "C": "affective", "D": "cognitive"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Of the Personality Disorders, Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD) has the best prognosis, with most patients showing a signi\u0000cant\nimprovement in—or remission of—symptoms over time. Longitudinal studies have found that by middle age or earlier, most individuals with\nBPD no longer meet the diagnostic criteria for the disorder. The resolution of symptoms, however, varies over time, with affective symptoms\nshowing the least amount of age-related improvement.\nAnswers A, B, and D: A study by Zanarini et al. indicated that affective symptoms were the most chronic, impulsive symptoms resolved most\nquickly, and cognitive and interpersonal symptoms were intermediate in terms of resolution (Zanarini, M.C., Frankenburg, F.R, Hennen, J. and\nSilk, K.R. The longitudinal course of borderline pathology: 6-year prospective follow-up of the phenomenology of borderline personality disorder,\nAmerican Journal of Psychiatry, 160, 274-283, 2003)."}, {"question": "Studies investigating anxiety over the lifespan indicate that, when compared to younger adults, older adults are:", "options": {"A": "more likely to be misdiagnosed as having an anxiety disorder.", "B": "much less likely to bene\u0000t from pharmacotherapy or cognitive-behavioral therapy.", "C": "less likely to have comorbid symptoms of depression.", "D": "more likely to believe their symptoms are due to physical health problems."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Studies indicate that anxiety is the most common psychiatric disorder in older adults, with Generalized Anxiety Disorder being the most\nprevalent anxiety disorder. Older adults are more likely than younger adults to attribute their anxiety symptoms to physical health problems and,\nas a result, to seek help from a medical (versus mental health) professional (Stanley, M. A., & Beck, J. G. (2000). Anxiety disorders. Clinical\nPsychology Review, 20(6), 731754).\nAnswer A: In fact, under-diagnosis and under-treatment of anxiety disorders are more common among older (versus younger) adults.\nAnswer B: Research has generally demonstrated that older adults with anxiety bene\u0000t to about the same degree as younger adults from\npharmacotherapy and CBT.\nAnswer C: Younger and older adults with anxiety frequently have comorbid symptoms of depression, and this may actually occur more often in\nolder adults."}, {"question": "Hypnagogic hallucinations are:", "options": {"A": "misperceptions of real stimuli.", "B": "sensations perceived in the wrong sensory modality.", "C": "false perceptions that occur as you're falling asleep.", "D": "false perceptions associated with hallucinogenic use."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Hypnagogic hallucinations are vivid experiences that occur as a person falls asleep. These hallucinations are not necessarily indicative of a\nmental disorder, but are often associated with narcolepsy.\nAnswer A: This description sounds more like an illusion.\nAnswer B: This description refers to synesthesia.\nAnswer D: False perceptions associated with hallucinogenic use are simply hallucinations."}, {"question": "During the \u0000rst few sessions with a male client, you learn that he has trouble maintaining friendships, and he is disturbed by this since he does not like being alone. Additionally, he has frequent mood shifts, gets very angry about even minor irritations, is not sure what he wants to do with his life, and has had three different jobs in the past two years. Based on this information, the best diagnosis is:", "options": {"A": "Borderline Personality Disorder.", "B": "Narcissistic Personality Disorder.", "C": "Histrionic Personality Disorder.", "D": "Schizoid Personality Disorder."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "To distinguish between different Personality Disorders, you need to be familiar with their essential features; this information is summarized in\nthe Abnormal Psychology chapter of the written study materials. A pattern of instability in mood, relationships, and occupation is common in\nindividuals diagnosed with Borderline Personality Disorder.\nAnswer B: The man does not exhibit the grandiosity that characterizes Narcissistic Personality Disorder.\nAnswer C: Although shifts in mood are typical of Histrionic Personality Disorder, the man does not demonstrate attention-seeking behavior.\nAnswer D: Schizoid Personality Disorder involves restricted emotions and detachment from interpersonal relationships."}, {"question": "The most effective intervention for cigarette smoking combines nicotine replacement therapy with:", "options": {"A": "antidepressants and bibliotherapy.", "B": "support from a clinician and skills training.", "C": "covert sensitization and relapse prevention.", "D": "stimulus control and habit reversal training."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Multimodal smoking cessation interventions are most effective for treating nicotine use disorders. Speci\u0000cally, interventions are most\nsuccessful when they combine therapy with advice and support from medical or mental health professionals (APA, 2006).\nAnswers A, C, and D: The most effective intervention for cigarette smoking combines nicotine replacement therapy with support from a\nclinician and skills training."}, {"question": "Your new client, Dave D., age 28, says that, for as long as he can remember, he has experienced trouble \u0000nishing projects because of his tendency to repeatedly check for mistakes and his desire to achieve perfection in whatever he does. He says that, because of these tendencies, he received several \"incompletes\" when he was in college and was \u0000red from his last job. Dave also reports that he has constant thoughts and impulses that he knows are inappropriate and that he cannot control, which leads him to feel very anxious. He says he's afraid he's going to hurt one of his family members by forgetting to turn off the stove or by accidentally leaving a door or window unlocked at night, and that for this reason, he spends a great deal of time checking and rechecking the stove, doors, and windows. Based on these symptoms, the most likely diagnosis or diagnoses for Dave is/are:", "options": {"A": "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder and Generalized Anxiety Disorder.", "B": "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder and Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder.", "C": "Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Impulse Control Disorder NOS.", "D": "Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder only."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Dave is exhibiting symptoms that are consistent with the criteria of two disorders. Dave's preoccupation with perfection and orderliness are\nsymptoms of Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder. Dave also reports obsessions and compulsions, which are not criteria of Obsessive-\nCompulsive Personality Disorder but are symptoms of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder.\nAnswer A: A diagnosis of Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) requires the presence of excessive anxiety and worry about several events.\nFurthermore, Dave's \"constant thoughts\" and safety concerns appear to re\u0000ect obsessions, and his behaviors of checking and rechecking the\nstove, doors, and windows would be classi\u0000ed as compulsions. In addition, his other symptoms are consistent with Obsessive-Compulsive\nPersonality Disorder.\nAnswer C: Dave's symptoms do not meet the diagnostic criteria for an impulse control disorder.\nAnswer D: See explanation for response B."}, {"question": "Childhood-onset Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder:", "options": {"A": "is about equally common in boys and girls.", "B": "is four times more common in girls than boys.", "C": "is two times more common in girls than boys.", "D": "is more common in boys than girls."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with prevalence rates and gender ratios for several disorders, including OCD. The rates and ratios you are\nmost likely to encounter on exam questions are in the Abnormal Psychology chapter of the written study materials. While OCD is about equally\ncommon in male and female adults, because of its earlier onset in males, it is more common in male children than in female children.\nAnswer A: See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer C: See explanation for answer D."}, {"question": "A female client says she frequently misperceives things, and this has made her anxious about leaving home. For example, she often thinks she sees small animals at work when, in fact, the \"animals\" are inanimate objects, such as books, coffee mugs, and o\u0000ce supplies. The woman's misperceptions are best described as:", "options": {"A": "illusions.", "B": "delusions.", "C": "hallucinations.", "D": "depersonalization."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the de\u0000nitions of the terms listed in the responses to this question. An illusion is a misperception of\nreality (e.g., misperceiving a coffee mug as a rodent).\nAnswer B: A delusion is a false belief about reality that is \u0000rmly held regardless of evidence to the contrary.\nAnswer C: While an illusion is elicited by an actual stimulus, a hallucination is a sensory perception in the absence of an external stimulus.\nSince this woman is \"misperceiving things,\" this suggests she is experiencing illusions rather than hallucinations.\nAnswer D: Depersonalization is an alteration in the sense of self (i.e., the sense that one is detached from one's mind or body)."}, {"question": "High expressed emotion is associated with multiple diagnoses, including:", "options": {"A": "Antisocial Personality Disorder, Borderline Personality Disorder, Mood Disorders, and Conduct Disorder.", "B": "Mood Disorders, Conduct Disorder, Schizophrenia, and Borderline Personality Disorder.", "C": "Major Depressive Disorder, Substance Use Disorders, Conduct Disorder, and Schizophrenia.", "D": "Schizophrenia, Bipolar Disorder, Major Depressive Disorder, and Eating Disorders."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "High expressed emotion involves open criticism and hostility by family members toward the individual with a mental health diagnosis.\nAlternatively, this phenomenon may entail overprotectiveness and emotional overinvolvement. High expressed emotion is associated with\nSchizophrenia, Bipolar Disorder, Major Depressive Disorder, and Eating Disorders. High levels of expressed emotion among family members\nhave been linked to high relapse and rehospitalization rates for individuals who have been diagnosed with Schizophrenia.\nAnswers A, B, C, and D: Research indicates that high expressed emotion is most often associated with Schizophrenia, Bipolar Disorder, Major\nDepressive Disorder, and Eating Disorders."}, {"question": "Alcohol-Induced Sleep Disorder most often involves which of the following symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Increased wakefulness, restless sleep, and vivid dreams", "B": "Abnormal physiological and behavioral symptoms during sleep", "C": "Excessively long nocturnal sleep and excessive sleepiness during the day", "D": "Di\u0000culty falling and staying asleep with a reduction in REM (dream) sleep"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "According to the DSM-5, Alcohol-Induced Sleep Disorder is usually of the insomnia type and can be the result of either Intoxication or\nWithdrawal. When produced by Intoxication, it involves immediate sedation accompanied by increased Stages 3 and 4 sleep and reduced REM\nsleep, followed by increased wakefulness. When resulting from Withdrawal, the disorder involves a severe disruption in sleep continuity with\nvivid dreams.\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Electroconvulsive shock therapy (ECT) is considered an effective intervention for severe depression that has not been responsive to other treatments or is accompanied by a high suicide risk. However, ECT also often produces adverse side effects including:", "options": {"A": "patchy anterograde amnesia.", "B": "temporary retrograde amnesia.", "C": "patchy anterograde amnesia and temporary retrograde amnesia.", "D": "permanent retrograde amnesia."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The side effects of ECT vary from patient to patient but usually include some degree of memory loss. Memory loss most often involves patchy\nanterograde amnesia for three to six months post-ECT and retrograde amnesia for events that occurred within several months prior to ECT. In\nmost cases, memories of past events eventually return.\nAnswer A: See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer D: Permanent retrograde amnesia is relatively uncommon, so this is not the best response."}, {"question": "Of the 6 to 10% of adults over 65 who are diagnosed with dementia, what percent have Alzheimer's disease?", "options": {"A": "85 to 95", "B": "60 to 90", "C": "40 to 60", "D": "20 to 35"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Estimates of the prevalence rates for Alzheimer's disease and other forms of dementia vary, with rates increasing as age increases. The\nstudies indicate that, of adults over 65 with dementia, between 60 to 90% have a diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease.\nAnswer A: See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer C: See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer D: See explanation for answer B."}, {"question": "Research on the impact of cultural identi\u0000cation on substance abuse among Native American youth has found that the risk for abuse is:", "options": {"A": "lowest for those who are acculturated into the non-Native American (mainstream) culture.", "B": "lowest for those who can adapt to both Native American and non-Native American cultures.", "C": "highest for those who strongly identify with the Native American culture and reject the non-Native American culture.", "D": "highest for those who do not strongly identify with either the Native American or non-Native American culture."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Relevant literature has established a link between risk for substance abuse and cultural identity for Native American youth. Speci\u0000cally, studies\nhave shown that either strong identi\u0000cation with one's Native American tribe or a bicultural identity is associated with a lower risk for\nsubstance use, compared to a strong identi\u0000cation with the mainstream culture only (Oetting, E.R., Beauvais, F., and Velarde, J. Marijuana use\nby reservation Native American youth, Listening Post (Indian Health Service), 4 25-28, 1982).\nAnswer A: Strong identi\u0000cation with non-Native American (mainstream) culture only is associated with a greater risk for substance abuse,\ncompared to identi\u0000cation with one's own tribe or a bicultural identity.\nAnswer C: Strong identi\u0000cation with the Native American culture is associated with a lower risk for substance abuse.\nAnswer D: The highest risk seems to be for those who reject the Native American culture and are highly acculturated into the mainstream."}, {"question": "The psychoanalyst Adolph Stern considered the di\u0000culties experienced by individuals diagnosed with Borderline Personality Disorder to be secondary to:", "options": {"A": "inhibited aggression.", "B": "\u0000xation.", "C": "emotional dysregulation.", "D": "narcissism."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Stern considered the di\u0000culties experienced by the borderline patient to be secondary to narcissism and viewed narcissism as arising from a\nserious disturbance in the early mother-child relationship. Note that other psychoanalytically oriented theorists have identi\u0000ed other factors as\nbeing primary. Kernberg, for example, emphasized the role of excessive aggression.\nAnswer A: See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer C: See explanation for answer D."}, {"question": "The presence of which of the following symptoms would suggest a diagnosis of Conduct Disorder rather than a diagnosis of Oppositional De\u0000ant Disorder?", "options": {"A": "Frequent lying and running away from home", "B": "Low frustration tolerance and temper outbursts", "C": "Drug use", "D": "Onset of symptoms after age 12"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Conduct Disorder involves a pattern of behavior in which the basic rights of others, and major age-appropriate social norms or rules, are\nviolated. Lying and running away are characteristic of Conduct Disorder. Behaviors associated with Oppositional De\u0000ant Disorder are less\nsevere and include negativism, de\u0000ance, and hostility.\nAnswer B: Temper outbursts (and irritability) are more explicitly associated with Oppositional De\u0000ant Disorder and are not directly included in\nthe criteria for Conduct Disorder.\nAnswer C: The use of drugs does not help distinguish between the two disorders.\nAnswer D: This information would not assist with differentiating the diagnoses, as the onset of both disorders may occur before age 12."}, {"question": "Research on the comorbidity of the eating disorders suggests that the rates of Social Phobia and Speci\u0000c Phobia are:", "options": {"A": "higher in individuals with Anorexia Nervosa and Bulimia Nervosa than in other individuals and that the onset of the anxiety disorder is most often prior to the onset of the eating disorder.", "B": "higher in individuals with Anorexia Nervosa or Bulimia Nervosa than in other individuals and the onset of the anxiety disorder is most often simultaneous with the onset of the eating disorder.", "C": "higher in individuals with Anorexia Nervosa or Bulimia Nervosa than in other individuals and the onset of the anxiety disorder is most often after the onset of the eating disorder.", "D": "similar in individuals with Anorexia Nervosa or Bulimia Nervosa as the rates for other individuals and the onset is most often simultaneous with the onset of the eating disorder."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The literature has generally demonstrated that the rates of several anxiety disorders are higher among individuals with an eating disorder than\namong individuals in the general population. Furthermore, most studies have revealed that the onset of the anxiety disorder tends to precede\nthe onset of the eating disorder (Kaye WH, Bulik CM, Thornton L, Barbarich N, Masters K. Comorbidity of anxiety disorders with anorexia and\nbulimia nervosa. Am J Psychiatry. 2004 Dec;161(12):2215-21).\nAnswers B, C, and D: See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Fairburn's (2008) CBT-E (cognitive-behavioral therapy - enhanced) adds which of the following to traditional cognitive- behavioral therapy for individuals with Bulimia Nervosa?", "options": {"A": "Interventions aimed at boundary disturbances", "B": "Interventions aimed at interpersonal relationships", "C": "Interventions that foster insight into the cause of the disorder", "D": "Interventions derived from Rogerian therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "According to several evidence-based studies, cognitive-behavioral therapy is the most effective treatment for Bulimia Nervosa. However,\nseveral experts have suggested that the effectiveness of a traditional approach to CBT is improved if it includes a greater focus on other\nissues, such as emotional responding and interpersonal relationships. C. G. Fairburn's CBT-E incorporates interventions that target\nperfectionism, low self-esteem, interpersonal factors, and emotional factors (Cognitive behavior therapy and eating disorders, New York,\nGuilford Publ., 2008).\nAnswers A, C, and D: C. G. Fairburn's CBT-E incorporates interventions that target perfectionism, low self-esteem, interpersonal factors, and\nemotional factors."}, {"question": "Within the DSM-5, level of severity of Intellectual Disability is based on:", "options": {"A": "the individual's score on a standardized intelligence test.", "B": "the individual's adaptive functioning in conceptual, social, and practical domains.", "C": "the degree of discrepancy between the individual's cognitive and adaptive functioning.", "D": "the degree of discrepancy between the individual's IQ and academic achievement."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The DSM-5 distinguishes between four levels of severity for Intellectual Disability - mild, moderate, severe, and profound. These severity\nspeci\u0000ers are based on level of adaptive functioning in conceptual, social, and practical domains.\nAnswer A: See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer C: See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer D: See explanation for answer B."}, {"question": "Augustine, age 5, is in foster care and enrolled in a therapeutic nursery school. Children's Services removed Augustine and his older sister from their home after a stranger found them wandering in the park asking people for food. Additionally, their parents both have substance use disorders. While observing Augustine at school, you notice that he impulsively approaches strangers, tends to be overly familiar with people he has just met, and quickly becomes attached to any adult who pays attention to him. Before assigning a DSM-5 diagnosis of _____________ you will want to con\u0000rm that his symptoms are attributable to ______________.", "options": {"A": "Reactive Attachment Disorder, disinhibited type; developmental delays", "B": "Disinhibited Social Engagement Disorder; developmental delays", "C": "Reactive Attachment Disorder, disinhibited type; early neglect or deprivation", "D": "Disinhibited Social Engagement Disorder; early neglect or deprivation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Disinhibited Social Engagement Disorder involves a pattern of culturally inappropriate and overly familiar behavior with unfamiliar people, with\nevidence that the behavior is related to the experience of extreme insu\u0000cient care.\nAnswer A: The presence or absence of developmental delays is not a diagnostic criterion for a diagnosis of Disinhibited Social Engagement\nDisorder or Reactive Attachment Disorder and, therefore, would not be useful for con\u0000rming either diagnosis.\nAnswer B: See explanations for answers A and D.\nAnswer C: See explanations for responses A and D."}, {"question": "When using the DSM-5, a clinician would code which of the following to indicate that a client has symptoms that do not meet the diagnostic criteria for a speci\u0000c disorder and does not want to specify the reason why?", "options": {"A": "Disorder not otherwise speci\u0000ed", "B": "Provisional disorder", "C": "Other speci\u0000ed disorder", "D": "Unspeci\u0000ed disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "When using the DSM-5, a clinician would code unspeci\u0000ed disorder when he or she does not want to indicate the reason that the client's\nsymptoms do not meet the criteria for a speci\u0000c diagnosis (e.g., unspeci\u0000ed depressive disorder).\nAnswer A: Disorder not otherwise speci\u0000ed is not a DSM-5 code.\nAnswer B: When using the DSM-5, the provisional speci\u0000er applies when \"there is a strong presumption that the full criteria will ultimately be\nmet for a disorder but not enough information is available to make a \u0000rm diagnosis.\"\nAnswer C: A clinician would code other speci\u0000ed disorder when he or she wants to indicate the reason that the client's symptoms do not meet\nthe criteria for a speci\u0000c diagnosis (e.g., \"other speci\u0000ed depressive disorder, recurrent brief depression\")."}, {"question": "Which of the following is true about sleep terrors and sleepwalking?", "options": {"A": "Both occur during REM sleep.", "B": "Both are usually followed by amnesia for the event.", "C": "Both are associated with an impaired homeostatic sleep drive.", "D": "Both are accompanied by di\u0000culty awakening in the morning with morning confusion."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "In the DSM-5, sleep terrors and sleepwalking are types of Non-Rapid Eye Movement Sleep Arousal Disorder. Both types occur during non-REM\nsleep and involve amnesia for the episode.\nAnswer A: Sleep terrors and sleepwalking both occur during non-REM sleep.\nAnswer C: An impaired homeostatic sleep drive has been linked to Circadian Rhythm Sleep-Wake Disorder.\nAnswer D: Di\u0000culty awakening and morning confusion are associated with Circadian Rhythm Sleep-Wake Disorder."}, {"question": "Which of the following is indicative of transvestic disorder?", "options": {"A": "You experience repeated arousal from looking at or feeling clothing typically worn by someone of a different sex.", "B": "You may want to dress up in items or clothing of another gender because it’s relaxing.", "C": "You persistently use inanimate objects to reach sexual arousal.", "D": "You experience feelings of distress that your birth-assigned sex does not match your personal identity."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "you experience repeated arousal from looking at or feeling clothing, fabrics, or undergarments typically worn by someone of a different sex, you\nmay have symptoms of transvestic disorder. This repeated arousal is a key component of diagnosis.\nAnswer B: Cross-dressing is when you dress in clothing typically made for a different gender. Cross-dressing by itself is not a disorder. It\ndoesn’t mean you have transvestic disorder or gender dysphoria.\nAnswer C: Fetishistic disorder also involves intense sexual arousal, urges, or behaviors relating to any nonliving objects. However, unlike in\ntransvestic disorder, the objects of these urges are not limited to the clothing of different genders.\nAnswer D: Gender dysphoria is a feeling of distress that your birth-assigned sex does not match your personal identity."}, {"question": "Dissociative Identity Disorder involves the existence in one individual of:", "options": {"A": "two or more distinct personality states.", "B": "two or more distinct personality states or the experience of possession.", "C": "two or more distinct personality states, the experience of possession, and the experience of detachment with respect to one's identity.", "D": "experiences of unreality, detachment, or being an outside observer with respect to one's identity."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Dissociative Identity Disorder is characterized by the existence in one individual of two or more distinct personality states or the experience of\npossession, with recurrent gaps in the recall of ordinary events, personal information, or traumatic events that is not consistent with ordinary\nforgetfulness. In some cultures, dissociative states are an acceptable expression of religious or cultural beliefs and do not necessarily\nconstitute a mental disorder.\nAnswers A, C, and D: These responses are incorrect as the DSM-5 description of this disorder accounts for cultural differences, in which\ndistinct personality states may be described as an experience of possession."}, {"question": "Antidepressants are effective for reducing binge eating and purging and improving dysphoria. However, compared to antidepressants, _________ is generally associated with lower relapse and treatment dropout rates.", "options": {"A": "acceptance and commitment therapy", "B": "keeping a food journal", "C": "cognitive-behavioral therapy", "D": "exposure with response prevention"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Comparisons of cognitive-behavioral therapy and antidepressants have generally demonstrated that cognitive-behavioral therapy is associated\nwith lower relapse and treatment dropout rates.\nAnswer A: See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer D: See explanation for answer C."}, {"question": "Research by Kaye, Gendall, and Strober (1998) suggests that food restriction associated with Anorexia Nervosa reduces __________ levels.", "options": {"A": "serotonin", "B": "acetylcholine", "C": "dopamine", "D": "glutamate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Kaye et al. found that anorexia is related to higher-than-normal levels of serotonin in the brain. The researchers proposed that high levels of\nserotonin cause anxiety and that starvation reduces tryptophan, which then reduces brain levels of serotonin and temporarily relieves anxiety\n(Kaye W, Gendall K, Strober M. Serotonin neuronal function and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor treatment in anorexia and bulimia\nnervosa. Biol Psychiatry. 1998 Nov 1;44(9):825-38).\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Your 32-year old female client reports experiencing \"panic attacks,\" including elevated heart rate and palpitations. She says that before this happens, she has hot \u0000ashes, and sometimes she \"blacks out\" and loses control. Which of the following should you rule out in order to assign a diagnosis of Panic Disorder?", "options": {"A": "Cardiac arrhythmia, general medical conditions, and Substance-Induced Panic Disorder", "B": "Substance-Induced Panic Disorder or other cardiac-related medical conditions", "C": "Hypothyroidism, hyperglycemia, cardiac arrhythmia, and other medical conditions that include symptoms of Panic Disorder", "D": "Hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, cardiac arrhythmia, and other medical conditions that include symptoms of Panic Disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Your client's symptoms are consistent with Panic Disorder. However, before assigning this diagnosis, you must rule out medical conditions that\nmay include symptoms of Panic Disorder. The most common examples of these conditions include hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, and\ncardiac arrhythmia.\nAnswers A and B: Substance-Induced Panic Disorder is not a DSM-5 diagnosis, so these responses can be eliminated.\nAnswer C: Panic symptoms are associated with hyperthyroidism and hypoglycemia (not hypothyroidism and hyperglycemia)."}, {"question": "Neurocognitive Disorder Due to HIV infection involves six stages. A person with AIDS who has a clear functional impairment, but can perform all but the most demanding aspects of activities of daily living and can walk without assistance, is in which of the following stages?", "options": {"A": "Stage 0.5 (equivocal/subclinical)", "B": "Stage 1 (mild)", "C": "Stage 2 (moderate)", "D": "Stage 3 (severe)"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Neurocognitive Disorder Due to HIV infection is also known as AIDS Dementia Complex (ADC) and is categorized into six stages that re\u0000ect\nprogressive de\u0000cits in functioning. The symptoms listed in the question are characteristic of Stage 1.\nAnswer A: Stage 0.5 involves minimal or equivocal signs of impairment with no de\u0000cits in work or activities of daily living.\nAnswer C: A person in Stage 2 cannot work or perform demanding activities of daily living and may require assistance when walking.\nAnswer D: A person in Stage 3 has signi\u0000cant intellectual impairments and cannot walk unassisted. Additional information about the stages of\nNeurocognitive Disorder Due to HIV Infection is included in the Abnormal Psychology chapter of the written study materials."}, {"question": "For a diagnosis of Bulimia Nervosa, an individual must exhibit which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Purging following binge eating for at least one month", "B": "Binge eating for at least two months", "C": "Lack of control over eating plus either purging or excessive exercise for at least two months", "D": "Binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behavior for at least three months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The key features of Bulimia Nervosa are binge eating and recurrent inappropriate compensatory behavior (e.g., purging, diuretic use, excessive\nexercising) for at least three months.\nAnswer A: See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer C: See explanation for answer D."}, {"question": "Which of the following is true about Separation Anxiety Disorder?", "options": {"A": "It can only be diagnosed in children.", "B": "It can only be diagnosed in children and adolescents.", "C": "It can only be diagnosed in adolescents when it manifests as school refusal.", "D": "It can be diagnosed in children, adolescents, and adults."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Separation Anxiety Disorder involves developmentally inappropriate and excessive fear or anxiety related to separation from home or\nattachment \u0000gures. The disturbance must last for at least four weeks in children and adolescents or six months in adults.\nAnswers A, B, and C: These responses are incorrect as Separation Anxiety Disorder may be diagnosed in adults as well as children and\nadolescents."}, {"question": "An electrolyte imbalance is a possible complication of Bulimia Nervosa. The danger of this complication lies in the fact that it can lead to:", "options": {"A": "cardiac arrhythmia and arrest.", "B": "blood dyscrasias.", "C": "permanent memory loss.", "D": "dehydration."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Only one of the listed conditions is a consequence of an electrolyte imbalance. Electrolyte imbalances caused by vomiting and the use of\nlaxatives and diuretics can have serious consequences, including, in extreme cases, cardiac arrhythmia and arrest.\nAnswer B: Blood dyscrasias are not caused by electrolyte imbalances.\nAnswer C: Permanent memory loss has not been linked to electrolyte imbalances.\nAnswer D: Dehydration is one of the causes (not consequences) of an electrolyte imbalance."}, {"question": "Research on Helms' White Racial Identity Development Model has shown that White therapists are most effective in cross-cultural counseling situations when they are in which stage of identity development?", "options": {"A": "The stage that matches the client's stage of identity development", "B": "The stage that complements the client's stage of identity development", "C": "The reintegration stage", "D": "The autonomy stage"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Helms' (1995) model distinguishes between six stages of identity development: contact, disintegration, reintegration, pseudo-independence,\nimmersion-emersion, and autonomy. The research on Helms's model has found that, the more advanced the White therapist's identity\ndevelopment, the greater his/her effectiveness when working with clients from culturally diverse groups.\nAnswers A and B: White therapists who are at a less advanced identity stage of development are likely to be less effective when working with\nclients who are not part of the racial majority regardless of what stage the client is at.\nAnswer C: White therapists in this stage idealize White society and denigrate members of minority groups."}, {"question": "With regard to power in the client-therapist relationship, a feminist therapist would most likely:", "options": {"A": "recognize that her power stems primarily from her ability to serve as a role-model.", "B": "acknowledge the power differential inherent in the relationship but view it as a temporary condition.", "C": "initially foster her position of power so as to maximize her in\u0000uence.", "D": "regard power a \"non-issue\" because feminist therapists consider the therapeutic relationship to be egalitarian."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A key feature of feminist therapy is its promotion of an egalitarian approach. This issue is addressed by M. A. Douglas in her chapter entitled\n\"The Role of Power in Feminist Therapy: A Reformulation\" [in L. B. Rosewater and L. E. A. Walker (Eds.), Handbook of feminist therapy: Women's\nissues in psychotherapy, New York: Springer, 1985]. According to Douglas, the power differential inherent in the therapeutic relationship should,\n\u0000rst of all, be acknowledged. It should also be viewed as temporary since the goal of therapy will be to terminate the relationship and/or to\nreadjust the relationship so that it is more egalitarian. Douglas stresses the importance of minimizing the power differential inherent in the\ntherapeutic relationship.\nAnswer A: Although the therapist is viewed as a role model by many feminist therapists (and their clients), feminist therapists attempt to\nminimize the power differential inherent in the therapeutic relationship.\nAnswer C: Feminist therapist attempt to minimize the power differential.\nAnswer D: Feminist therapists acknowledge the power differential inherent in the therapeutic relationship and view it as an issue that must be\ndealt with."}, {"question": "A woman who previously drank two full bottles of wine each night has been sober for over a year and plans to stop attending AA meetings as she is 100% con\u0000dent in her ability to resist relapse. In the context of the transtheoretical model, she is in which stage of change?", "options": {"A": "Action", "B": "Maintenance", "C": "Preparation", "D": "Termination"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Prochaska and DiClemente's (1992) transtheoretical model distinguishes between six stages in the change process. During the \u0000nal stage,\ntermination, the person feels that s/he can resist temptation and is con\u0000dent that there is no risk for relapse. Given that she has maintained\nsobriety for a year, it is safe to assume she has worked through the \u0000rst \u0000ve stages of change and is currently in the termination stage as she is\ncon\u0000dent that there is no risk of relapse.\nAnswer A: In the action stage, the person actually takes steps to bring about change.\nAnswer B: In the maintenance stage, s/he consolidates the change and takes steps to prevent relapse.)\nAnswer C: In the preparation stage, the person has a clear intent to take action in the near future."}, {"question": "Members of an outpatient therapy group are likely to rank which of Yalom's therapeutic factors as most important?", "options": {"A": "Interpersonal input, catharsis, cohesiveness, and self-understanding", "B": "Self-understanding, universality, identi\u0000cation, and instillation of hope", "C": "Family re-enactment, catharsis, altruism, and interpersonal input", "D": "Interpersonal input, interpersonal output, universality, and identi\u0000cation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In his book, The Theory and Practice of Group Therapy, Yalom (1970) distinguishes between 12 therapeutic (curative) factors and discusses the\nresults of research examining the importance of these factors for group therapists and for group therapy members. Although the results vary\nsomewhat from study to study, these four factors are usually the most highly rated ones by group therapy members.\nAnswer B: Identi\u0000cation is usually among the factors that are rated least important.\nAnswer C: Family re-enactment is also rated as less important by group members.\nAnswer B: Identi\u0000cation is typically not rated as being particularly important by group members."}, {"question": "Research on the use of cognitive-behavioral therapy with older adults has found that it is:", "options": {"A": "less effective than cognitive-behavioral therapy for younger adults and, consequently, is generally contraindicated.", "B": "usually most effective when it is conducted at a slower pace and at higher levels of abstraction.", "C": "usually most effective when it is short- (versus long) term and conducted at lower levels of abstraction.", "D": "usually most effective when it focuses on replacing negative cognitions with positive ones and on the present rather than on the past."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The research has generally found that older adults bene\u0000t from cognitive-behavioral therapies and other forms of therapy to the same degree\nas do younger adults. However, the effectiveness of therapy with older adults may be enhanced when certain modi\u0000cations to therapy are\nmade. See, e.g., B. G. Knight and D. Satre, Cognitive behavioral therapy with older adults, Clinical Psychology: Science & Practice, 62(2), 188-\n203, 1999. Knight and Satre note that older adults generally bene\u0000t most from cognitive-behavioral therapies when therapy is presented at a\nslower pace and is conducted at an abstract level (i.e., at a level that focuses on schemas and relationship scripts rather than on simple\ncognitions).\nAnswer A: Cognitive-behavioral therapy has been found to be an effective treatment for older adults for a variety of disorders including\ndepression, anxiety, and insomnia and for coping with physical disability, chronic pain, and grief.\nAnswer C: This is the opposite of what is true. Because many of the problems faced by older adults are chronic, they are more likely to bene\u0000t\nfrom long-term interventions.\nAnswer D: This is also the opposite of what is true. The emotions of older adults are generally more complex than those of younger adults, and\nemotional reactions to events often consist of a mixture of positive and negative emotions. Consequently, it is usually best to focus on both the\npositive and negative connotations of emotions in therapy. In addition, allotting some time in therapy for older adults to reminisce about the\npast (rather than focusing only on the present) is bene\u0000cial."}, {"question": "Which of the following information-processing strategies is characteristic of the immersion-emmersion status of Helms's (1995) White Racial Identity Development Model?", "options": {"A": "Hypervigilance and reshaping", "B": "Obliviousness and denial", "C": "Flexibility and complexity", "D": "Selective perception and negative out-group distortion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Helms's (1995) White Racial Identity Development Model distinguishes between six stages or statuses: contact, disintegration, reintegration,\npseudo-independence, immersion-emersion, and autonomy. Each status is characterized by a different information-processing strategy (IPS),\nwhich are the methods the individual uses to reduce discomfort related to racial issues. The IPS for the immersion-emersion status is\ncharacterized by hypervigilance and reshaping.\nAnswer B: Obliviousness and denial are characteristic of the IPS of the contact status.\nAnswer C: Flexibility and complexity are characteristic of the IPS of the autonomy status.\nAnswer D: This is the IPS for the reintegration status."}, {"question": "A therapist is most likely to say that which of the following is the most stressful type of client behavior?", "options": {"A": "Threats of a malpractice suit", "B": "Demands for time and attention", "C": "Suicidal statements", "D": "A lack of gratitude"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Therapists experience stress and burnout for several reasons. Not surprisingly, the most di\u0000cult situation--i.e., the one requiring an immediate\nresponse--is the one that is associated with the greatest stress. Surveys of therapists have revealed that suicidal ideation and threats are\nconsidered the most stress-producing client behaviors.\nAnswers A, B, and D: While each of these responses certainly impact therapist stress level, research has shown that suicidal statements\nreportedly cause therapists the most stress."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Alfred Adler, __________ refers to a person's goals and the ways in which he or she attempts to achieve those goals.", "options": {"A": "homeostasis", "B": "success identity", "C": "self-actualization", "D": "style of life"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Adler believed that each person has a unique set of goals and ways for achieving goals, which he referred to as the person's style of life.\nAccording to Adler, a person's style of life is fairly well established by 4 or 5 years of age. When preparing for exam questions from the area of\nClinical Psychology, a good strategy is to associate key concepts and terms with each of the major theories and theorists.\nAnswer A: In systems theory, homeostasis refers to the tendency for a family system to maintain its current level of functioning.\nAnswer B: In reality therapy, a person has adopted a success identity when he or she ful\u0000lls his or her needs in a responsible way.\nAnswer C: The term \"self-actualization\" is used by several theorists. As de\u0000ned by Rogers, it refers to an internal biological force that serves as\na major source of motivation and guides the individual toward positive, healthy growth."}, {"question": "An existential therapist is most likely to describe \"existential anxiety\" as:", "options": {"A": "the result of unresolved intrapsychic con\u0000icts.", "B": "a potential stimulus for growth.", "C": "the result of a boundary disturbance.", "D": "a manifestation of incongruence between self and experience."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Existential therapists view anxiety as an inevitable condition of life and distinguish between normal and neurotic anxiety. For existential\ntherapists, neurotic anxiety is out of proportion to the situation and can be immobilizing. In contrast, normal (existential) anxiety can serve as a\nsource of motivation to change.\nAnswer A: This is more consistent with the Freudian view of anxiety.\nAnswer C: According to Gestalt psychologists, boundary disturbances are a source of neurotic behavior.\nAnswer D: This answer is consistent with Carl Rogers's view of anxiety."}, {"question": "The traditional Hawaiian practice of ho'oponopono is best described as a:", "options": {"A": "primary prevention.", "B": "family intervention.", "C": "type of individual psychotherapy.", "D": "method for promoting relaxation."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Being a culturally competent practitioner requires being familiar with indigenous healing practices that might be appropriate for culturally\ndiverse clients. Ho'oponopono (\"setting it right\") is a traditional Hawaiian spiritual healing ritual for restoring harmony among family members\nby resolving a current con\u0000ict or other interpersonal problem. It is a structured process that is led by a senior family member or other respected\nelder and incorporates prayer, discussions aimed at identifying and resolving the problem, and a closing ceremony.\nAnswer A: This intervention would not be considered a primary intervention. Primary interventions are designed to prevent the development of\na disorder or problem.\nAnswer C: This is a group intervention.\nAnswer D: This intervention is designed to resolve con\u0000ict rather than simply promoting relaxation."}, {"question": "A therapist relying on a solution-focused family therapy approach would be most interested in which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Miracle and scaling questions", "B": "Boundary disturbances", "C": "Joining and mimesis", "D": "Schism and skew"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Solution-focused therapists use various types of questions to identify solutions to a client's problems. The miracle question involves asking\nwhat the individual would notice \u0000rst if, as the result of a miracle, his/her problem is suddenly gone. Scaling questions are used to determine\nhow a client currently feels.\nAnswer B: Boundary disturbances are of concern to Gestalt psychologists.\nAnswer C: Joining and mimesis are techniques used by structural family therapists.\nAnswer D: Marital schism and skew are terms used by Theodore Lidz to describe dysfunctional marital relationships."}, {"question": "According to Cross (2001), an African American adult in which of the following stages of identity development is likely to say that racial discrimination is not a contributor to his/her problems and that s/he prefers to see a White therapist?", "options": {"A": "Disintegration", "B": "Pre-encounter", "C": "Emersion", "D": "Pseudo-independence"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Cross's Black Racial (Nigrescence) Identity Development Model distinguishes between four stages: pre-encounter, encounter,\nimmersion/emersion, and internalization. According to Cross, race has low salience for individuals in the pre-encounter phase. People in this\nstage are likely to deny the impact of racial oppression and prefer a White therapist.\nAnswer A: This is the second status of the White Racial Identity Development Model.\nAnswer C: Immersion-Emersion is the third stage of Cross's model. Racial identity has high salience during this stage.\nAnswer D: This is not one of Cross's stages."}, {"question": "According to the Health Belief Model, health behavior is related to:", "options": {"A": "a combination of information, motivation, and behavioral skills.", "B": "perceived severity, perceived susceptibility, and perceived bene\u0000ts.", "C": "habit.", "D": "intentions and perceived norms."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Several models have been developed to predict when people will act in ways that bene\u0000t their health. The Health Belief Model is based on the\npremise that perceived severity of consequences, perceived susceptibility to consequences, and perceived bene\u0000ts of preventive behavior all\ncontribute to health behavior.\nAnswer A: These are components of the Information-Motivation-Behavioral Skills model of AIDS prevention.\nAnswer C: Habit is not a component of the Health Belief Model.\nAnswer D: This sounds more like the Theory of Reasoned Action."}, {"question": "The resistance and immersion stage of Atkinson, Morten, and Sue's (1993) Racial/Cultural Identity Development Model is characterized by:", "options": {"A": "a preference for the dominant group and resistance to being considered a member of a minority group.", "B": "con\u0000icting attitudes towards both the dominant group and one's own group.", "C": "rejection of the dominant group and preference for one's own group.", "D": "active participation in efforts to promote social change that will bene\u0000t one's group."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Racial/Cultural Identity Development Model consists of \u0000ve stages of identity development: conformity, dissonance, resistance and\nimmersion, introspection, and integrative awareness. During the resistance and immersion stage, the individual actively resists the dominant\ncultural group and becomes immersed in his/her own culture.\nAnswer A: Preference for the dominant group is characteristic of the conformity stage.\nAnswer B: This best describes the dissonance stage.\nAnswer D: This describes the internalization stage of Cross's Nigrescence model."}, {"question": "When conducting Seligman's Positive Psychotherapy (PPT), a client is asked to keep a daily journal in which he/she lists:", "options": {"A": "three good things that happened each day.", "B": "three good and three bad things that happened each day.", "C": "his/her thoughts about the good and bad things that happened each day.", "D": "at least one positive activity that he/she will engage in the following day."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Knowing that the name of the daily journal is the blessings (gratitude) journal may have helped you identify the correct answer to this question.\nSee, e.g., M. E. P. Seligman, T. Rashid, and A. C. Parks, Positive psychotherapy, American Psychologist, 61, 774-788, 2006. Although Positive\nPsychotherapy does not entirely ignore negative events, as its name implies, it focuses primarily on positive events.\nAnswer B: Listing negative events is not part of this assignment.\nAnswer C: The assignment does not involve listing one's thoughts but simply making a list of three blessings.\nAnswer D: Seligman's assignment does not involve planning, making this answer incorrect."}, {"question": "From the perspective of structural family therapy, triangulation, parent-child coalition, and detouring are:", "options": {"A": "methods for establishing clearer boundaries.", "B": "methods for maintaining homeostasis.", "C": "types of positive feedback.", "D": "attempts to increase intimacy."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "As its name implies, structural family therapy focuses on the family structure, especially its boundaries. Also, like many other forms of family\ntherapy, it is based on systems theory. The function of these three processes is to reduce con\u0000ict or stress and thereby maintain the status\nquo.\nAnswer A: Although boundaries are important in structural family therapy, the phenomena listed in the question do not always create \"clearer\nboundaries.\"\nAnswer C: These phenomena are probably better conceptualized as sources of negative feedback since their function is to maintain a state of\nhomeostasis.\nAnswer D: This is not the function of these processes as de\u0000ned by Minuchin."}, {"question": "_____________ is a brief manualized therapy that was originally designed for the treatment of depression.", "options": {"A": "Cognitive processing therapy", "B": "Interpersonal therapy", "C": "Dialectical behavior therapy", "D": "Cognitive behavioral therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Interpersonal therapy (IPT) is a brief manual-based therapy that de\u0000nes four key problem areas presented by depressed patients: grief,\ninterpersonal role disputes, role transition, and interpersonal de\u0000cits. These areas are the primary targets of therapy. While IPT was originally\ndeveloped as a treatment for depression, its use has since been applied to a number of other mental health maladies.\nAnswer A: CPT was developed in the 1980s by Patricia Resick, Ph.D. for the treatment of PTSD among sexual assault survivors. It has since\nbeen applied to other traumatized populations.\nAnswer C: DBT was originally developed by Marsha Linehan for the treatment of borderline personality disorder. The use of DBT has since been\nwidely diversi\u0000ed.\nAnswer D: While CBT was originally developed by Beck for the treatment of depression; it is not inherently a manual based therapy. However,\nsince CBT's inception, many manualized versions of CBT have been created. Though CBT is generally regarded as a time-sensitive therapy, it is\nnot always brief. As a result some manualized adaptations of CPT, such as brief cognitive behavioral therapy, have condensed the general\nformat from 12-20 sessions to between 4-8 sessions."}, {"question": "Freud argued that the \"work of the mental apparatus is directed toward keeping the quantity of excitation low.\" If the mental apparatus is unsuccessful in doing so, the result is likely to be which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Anxiety", "B": "Ego decompensation", "C": "Psychosis", "D": "Object-cathexis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Freud postulated that excessive excitation by the id's impulses leads to anxiety and that the ego's defense mechanisms act to keep anxiety\nproducing con\u0000icts out of consciousness.\nAnswer B: This is not an actual term.\nAnswer C: Freud described anxiety, not psychosis, as an unpleasant feeling linked with excitement of the autonomic nervous system.\nAnswer D: Object cathexis is a psychoanalytic term that refers to the id's investment of energy into an object that will satisfy an instinctual\nneed."}, {"question": "A practitioner of Rogers' client-centered therapy would use the Q-sort technique to:", "options": {"A": "facilitate identifying a client's diagnosis.", "B": "help the client identify speci\u0000c therapy goals.", "C": "help identify solutions to a client's presenting problems.", "D": "evaluate a client's progress in therapy."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The Q-sort technique involves rank-ordering descriptive statements that are printed on separate cards. It requires the sorter to distribute the\ncards into a \u0000xed number of piles along a continuum (for example, a continuum ranging from \"not at all characteristic of me\" to \"extremely\ncharacteristic of me\") re\u0000ecting a normal distribution. In therapy, this technique is often used to evaluate a client's self-perceptions.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Rogers used the Q-sort technique to evaluate therapy progress by evaluating a client's degree of congruence between\nhis/her self and ideal self. This involved having the client \u0000rst sort the cards in terms of how well they describe the client's current self and then\nin terms of his/her ideal self. The more similar the two sorts, the greater the congruence between the self and ideal self."}, {"question": "Sue and his colleagues (2007) distinguish between three forms of racial microaggression. As described by these investigators, __________ is occurring when a White person says to an African-American individual, \"When I look at you, I don't see color\" or \"There's only one race, the human race.\"", "options": {"A": "microassault", "B": "microinsult", "C": "microinvalidation", "D": "microinequity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Microassault, microinsult, and microinvalidation are the three forms of microaggression described by D. W. Sue et al. (Racial microaggressions\nin everyday life: Implications for clinical practice, American Psychologist, 62, 271-286, 2007). Microinvalidation refers to \"verbal comments or\nbehaviors that exclude, negate, or nullify the psychological thoughts, feelings, or experiential reality of a person of color\" (p. 278). \"Color\nblindness\" is a type of microinvalidation.\nAnswer A: Microassaults are \"explicit racial derogations characterized primarily by a violent verbal or nonverbal attack meant to hurt the\nintended victim through name-calling, avoidant behavior or purposeful discriminatory actions\" (p. 278).\nAnswer B: Microinsults are \"remarks or comments that convey rudeness, insensitivity and demean a person's racial heritage or identity\" (p.\n278).\nAnswer D: Microinequity is not one of the three forms of microaggression described by Sue et al."}, {"question": "When working with a client belonging to an ethnic minority group, a White therapist interprets the client's reluctance to disclose personal information as a sign of paranoia. According to Ridley (2005), this therapist is exhibiting which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Marginalization", "B": "Overidenti\u0000cation", "C": "Color blindness", "D": "Cultural ambivalence"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Ridley distinguishes between ten race-related defense mechanisms: color blindness, color consciousness, cultural transference, cultural\ncountertransference, cultural ambivalence, pseudotransference, overidenti\u0000cation, identi\u0000cation with the oppressor, race-based misdiagnosis,\nand avoidance of race (Overcoming unintentional racism in counseling and therapy: A practitioner's guide to intentional intervention, Thousand\nOaks, CA, Sage Publications, 2005). As de\u0000ned by Ridley, color blindness refers to a therapist's \"illusion that minority clients are no different\nthan non-minority clients\" (p.67). He notes that \"color blind\" therapists tend to overlook the effects of racism and discrimination and,\nconsequently, view deviations from White middle-class norms as pathological.\nAnswer A: Marginalization is not one of the race-related defense mechanisms identi\u0000ed by Ridley.\nAnswer B: Overidenti\u0000cation occurs when a minority therapist over identi\u0000es with the experiences of a minority client and assumes that the\nclient's presenting problems are race-related.\nAnswer D: According to Ridley, cultural ambivalence refers to a therapist's ambivalent motivations for treating members of ethnic minority\ngroups (e.g., needs for power and dominance versus needs for acceptance and approval)."}, {"question": "From the perspective of Prochaska and DiClemente's (1984) transtheoretical model of change, the failures of treatment programs for cigarette smoking are often due to the fact that:", "options": {"A": "the majority of smokers are not ready to change.", "B": "cigarette-smoking is self-reinforcing and di\u0000cult to extinguish.", "C": "nicotine addiction has a genetic component.", "D": "interventions do not address the environmental cues that trigger relapse."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The Prochaska and DiClemente model describes six stages of change: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance, and\ntermination. Research by Prochaska and colleagues has shown that a majority of individuals with addictions are in the precontemplation or\ncontemplation stage and, therefore, are ambivalent about change. For these individuals, most interventions will be unsuccessful.\nAnswer B: While this may be true, Prochaska and DiClemente do not cite this as the reason treatment programs are not successful.\nAnswer C: Research has established that there is a genetic component to addiction; however, the transtheoretical model does not substantiate\nthis as a reason that treatment programs fail.\nAnswer D: This is not true. The model identi\u0000es one's stage of change as a primary factor that contributes to an individual's success, or lack\nthereof, in treatment."}, {"question": "Past studies with African American therapy clients suggest that African American clients may be most likely to prefer an African American therapist when the client is in which stage of identity development?", "options": {"A": "Pre-encounter", "B": "Immersion/Emersion", "C": "Internalization", "D": "Autonomy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Cross's Black Racial Identity Development Model includes four stages. The \u0000rst half of the \u0000nal stage, immersion-emersion, is characterized by\nimmersion in Black culture. People in this phase usually prefer a therapist of their own ethnicity.\nAnswer A: During the pre-encounter stage, race has low salience.\nAnswer C: Internalization-commitment is the \u0000nal stage of identity development. During this phase, other factors in addition to ethnicity are\nconsidered when choosing a therapist.\nAnswer D: Autonomy is not a stage in Cross's model and, in other models, is most similar to Cross's internalization phase."}, {"question": "____________ has frequently been cited as a primary problem for gay and lesbian adolescents?", "options": {"A": "Isolation", "B": "Academic di\u0000culties", "C": "Social anxiety", "D": "Dysphoria"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Adolescents who are gay or lesbian face similar problems as their heterosexual peers, however, their identity development is also complicated\nby stigmatization which may lead to social withdrawal. Thus, isolation is frequently cited as a primary problem for these adolescents, and\nMartin and Hetrick (1988) found it to be the single-most frequent presenting problem (A. D. Martin and E. S. Hetrick, The stigmatization of the\ngay and lesbian adolescent, Journal of Homosexuality, 15(1-2), 163-183, 1988).\nAnswer B: While adolescents who are gay or lesbian may experience academic di\u0000culties as a result of the societal stressors they face, this is\ntypically not a primary presenting problem. Answers C and D: While anxiety and dysphoria are common among adolescents who are gay and\nlesbian, the above mentioned study found that isolation was the primary presenting problem for these youth."}, {"question": "Which of the following approaches in family therapy encourages the therapist to develop a \"therapeutic triangle\" with family members?", "options": {"A": "Extended family systems therapy", "B": "Communication/interaction family therapy", "C": "Adaptive family therapy", "D": "Strategic family therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In extended family systems theory, one therapeutic technique involves creating a therapeutic triangle in which the therapist actively interacts\nwith two family members during the treatment process. The goal is to shed light on and reduce the fusion that exists between family members.\nBowen's extended family systems therapy encourages the therapist to become a member in a therapeutic triangle with two family members\n(usually the spouses/partners).\nAnswer B: In communication/interaction family therapy, the therapist serves as a facilitator and teacher.\nAnswer C: Adaptive family therapy is a \"made up\" response.\nAnswer D: Although strategic family therapy involves an active therapist, the role of the therapist is to assess the problems, not to join a\nfamily's triangle."}, {"question": "In treating a client, a practitioner of Reality therapy would most likely be interested in which of the following?", "options": {"A": "The client's sense of responsibility and needs for power, freedom, and belongingness", "B": "The client's cognitive distortions (e.g., selective inattention, misperceptions)", "C": "The nature of the client's ego state pathology", "D": "The client's level of \"response-ability\""}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Reality therapy emphasizes ful\u0000lling one's needs in a responsible way. For practitioners of Reality therapy, behavior is motivated primarily by\nbiological and psychological needs (e.g., needs for belongingness, power, and freedom). The ability to ful\u0000ll one's needs in a way that doesn't\ndeprive others of the ability to do the same (i.e., in a responsible way) is the goal of therapy.\nAnswer B: This sounds more like cognitive therapy.\nAnswer C: This sounds more like Transactional Analysis.\nAnswer D: This is a goal of Gestalt therapy. It refers to the ability to act, decide, choose, and otherwise respond."}, {"question": "A client you have been seeing for eight months says he wants to quit therapy. He feels that the original problems he came to therapy for have all been resolved. You disagree and feel that there is good reason for the man to continue seeing you. You should _________________________.", "options": {"A": "get the client to agree to a few more sessions", "B": "discuss his reasons for wanting to terminate", "C": "discuss his reasons for wanting to terminate and your reasons for thinking he should continue", "D": "let him terminate but let him know he can come back if he desires"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Obviously you can't force a client to continue seeing you, but you should discuss your reservations about his termination. If you believe it would\nnot be good for the client to quit at this time, you should discuss this with him. If he still wants to leave, then you'd want to assure him that he\ncan come back and/or give him appropriate referrals.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. You would not want to coerce the client into seeing you for additional sessions.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect, as it is incomplete. It would be important for you to share your perspective as his therapist.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect, as it fails to account for the importance of discussing the clients thoughts and the therapist's perspective."}, {"question": "A psychologist is hired by an industrial development corporation to administer tests to help select key personnel. After being hired, the psychologist learns that the corporation is interested in screening potential employees who are left-handed. He is asked by company management to administer a projective test for the purpose of assessing handedness. The psychologist should ___________________.", "options": {"A": "administer the test to applicants only after obtaining signed informed consents from job applicants", "B": "administer the test and make recommendations but do not give management the actual test results", "C": "administer the test and give the results to management since it is not his responsibility to decide what information should be used in making employment decisions", "D": "refuse to administer the test and explain to management that it is inappropriate to utilize projective tests for this purpose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Ethical guidelines require psychologists to use tests only for purposes for which the tests have been validated. For example, Standard 9.02(a)\nof the APA's Ethics Code requires psychologists to use tests only for purposes that are \"appropriate in light of the research on or evidence of\nthe usefulness and proper application of the techniques.\" Use of projective tests for this purpose is unethical because (1) projective tests have\nnot been validated as job selection tests and (2) handedness has not been shown to be related to job performance. Therefore, using a\nprojective test in the selection process to identify handedness violates ethical guidelines. Answer A. This answer is incorrect. Consent is not\nthe issue in this situation. Obtaining an informed consent would not resolve the unethical use of a test.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. Use of a projective test to make employment decisions is not considered ethical due to a lack of validity\nevidence, and the psychologist, therefore, should not administer the test. Answer C. This answer is incorrect. It is the psychologist's\nresponsibility to ensure that tests and test results are not misused."}, {"question": "You agree to see an adolescent in therapy. Before you begin therapy, the parents tell you they will want information about how their son is progressing. As an ethical psychologist, you should ______________________.", "options": {"A": "tell the boy you'll have to tell his parents everything", "B": "tell the boy's parents that you can't tell them anything", "C": "agree in advance with all parties what, if anything, will be revealed to the parents", "D": "proceed with caution"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Ethically, a psychologist has the obligation to protect the con\u0000dentiality of his/her client, but legally, in most situations, parents have the right to\ninformation obtained in therapy about their minor child. This dilemma is acknowledged by the Ethics Code, which requires that psychologists\nconsider the preferences and best interests of individuals who are legally incapable of giving informed consent. This course of action best\nmeets the psychologist's ethical and legal obligations. An advance agreement safeguards the interests of the adolescent, who would be made\naware of the types of information which may not be kept con\u0000dential.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. In most circumstances, this course of action will not be consistent with the adolescent's best interest.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. If you do this, you will be acting in con\u0000ict with the law. Legally, in most situations, parents have the right to\ninformation obtained in therapy by a psychologist about their minor children. Ethically, however, in order to safeguard the child's interests,\npsychologists should try to encourage parents to respect con\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. Certainly, you should proceed with caution in this situation, as you should in any situation in which you are\nfaced with a possible ethical dilemma. However, this is a very general response to a situation that is more speci\u0000c. When answering these\ntypes of questions, you should choose responses with a level of speci\u0000city that matches the question."}, {"question": "Dr. Bascom, a newly-licensed psychologist, has been hired by a community mental health center. Several months after beginning her job, Dr. Bascom starts treating a college student who experiences severe test anxiety. Although Dr. Bascom took a graduate course in behavior therapy and has had some clinical experience with behavioral techniques, she has never worked with anyone with test anxiety. As an ethical psychologist, Dr. Bascom should ___________________________.", "options": {"A": "review the literature on test anxiety before beginning treatment", "B": "seek consultation from an experienced colleague and continue working with the client", "C": "refer the student to another psychologist who has experience in treating test anxiety", "D": "continue to see the student since she has adequate experience in behavioral techniques"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Standard 2.01(a) of the APA's Ethics Code states that \"Psychologists provide services, teach, and conduct research with populations and in\nareas only within the boundaries of their competence, based on their education, training, supervised experience, consultation, study, or\nprofessional experience.\" This principle suggests that, in deciding whether to seek supervision or make a referral, psychologists must consider\ntheir training and experience. Seeking consultation is probably the best course of action in this situation since Dr. Bascom does have\nexperience and training with behavioral techniques. Such a course of action is consistent with Standard 2.01(a), which states that\npsychologists provide services involving new techniques \"based on their education, training, supervised experience, consultation, study, or\nprofessional experience.\"\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. While the psychologist does need further training in treating test anxiety, a literature review undertaken on\none's own is not su\u0000cient.\nAnswer C. This answer is incorrect, because it is not the best choice in this situation. A referral, while acceptable, is not necessary given the\npsychologist has the training and experience necessary to use behavioral techniques. If the psychologist did not have such training and\nexperience, a referral would be a better course of action.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. Since the psychologist has not used behavioral techniques speci\u0000cally to treat test anxiety before, it would\nbe better to obtain supervision."}, {"question": "A client says she is upset because her doctor has suggested she undergo tests for a possible hyperactive thyroid. She says she believes that doctors are always looking for excuses to perform unnecessary procedures in order to make more money. You should ________________________.", "options": {"A": "read up on hyperthyroidism so that you can give her an informed opinion", "B": "have her sign a release of information and contact her physician", "C": "refer her to an endocrinologist to discuss the purpose of the tests", "D": "explore the source of her hostility toward doctors"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Of the choices offered, a referral to an endocrinologist most directly meets the woman's needs and addresses the issue of the psychologist's\ncompetence, which is the ethical issue that underlies this question. A psychologist cannot ethically render a professional opinion on this\nmatter. An endocrinologist is quali\u0000ed to discuss the tests with the woman. This woman needs to \u0000nd out if tests for hyperthyroidism are\nnecessary.\nAnswer A: No matter how much you read on hyperthyroidism, you will not, as a psychologist, be quali\u0000ed to offer a professional opinion. Thus,\nthis course of action would be a direct violation of the ethics code.\nAnswer B: While this is not an unethical or incorrect course of action, it does not address the woman's needs. The woman needs to know if the\nphysician's recommendation was warranted if she is questioning the physician's opinion on this matter.\nAnswer D: There is no indication in this question that the woman has general feelings of hostility toward doctors."}, {"question": "For a claim of malpractice ___________________.", "options": {"A": "the therapist must have had malevolent intentions and there must be a causal connection between the therapist's acts and the harm suffered by the client", "B": "the therapist must have had malevolent intentions and/or there must be some harm to the client as a result of the therapist's acts", "C": "the therapist may or may not have had malevolent intentions but there must be a causal connection between the therapist's acts and the harm suffered by the client", "D": "the therapist must have violated normal \"standards of practice\" or must have had malevolent intentions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "A complaint of malpractice requires that (1) the psychologist had a professional relationship with the client that established a legal duty of\ncare; (2) there must be a demonstrable standard of care that the psychologist breached; (3) the client must have suffered harm or injury; and\n(4) the psychologist's breach of duty must be the proximate cause of that harm or injury.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. The psychologist's actions do not have to be malevolent but there must be evidence that the psychologist's\nactions are the cause of the harm suffered by the client.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. The psychologist's actions do not have to be malevolent but there must be evidence that the client has\nsuffered harm.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. The psychologist's actions do not have to be malevolent but there must be evidence that the psychologist's\nhas violated a standard of care."}, {"question": "The court asks a psychologist working for the criminal justice system to evaluate a young man accused of committing a felony. The young man's court-appointed attorney tells the psychologist he has attempted to interview the man, but the defendant has di\u0000culty following instructions and that his perceptions of time, place, and people appear to be confused. The psychologist will be required to testify in court regarding her assessment of the young man. In terms of con\u0000dentiality, the psychologist is _________________________.", "options": {"A": "legally bound to maintain con\u0000dentiality unless she obtains a written release from the young man's attorney", "B": "legally bound to maintain con\u0000dentiality unless she obtains a written release from the young man", "C": "not legally bound to maintain con\u0000dentiality but ethically required to inform the young man of the limits of con\u0000dentiality", "D": "not legally bound to maintain con\u0000dentiality or ethically required to discuss the limits of con\u0000dentiality due to the nature of the case"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Standard 4.02(a) of the Ethics Code mandates psychologists to discuss \"the relevant limits of con\u0000dentiality.\" When working within the criminal\njustice system, a psychologist is serving both the offender and the criminal justice system. In such situations, a psychologist is not legally\nbound to maintain con\u0000dentiality; however, in accordance with Standard 4.02, the psychologist should inform the offender of the limits of\ncon\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. A psychologist who is asked by the court to evaluate a defendant is not required legally or ethically to\nobtain the attorney's permission to conduct the evaluation.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. When the court requests a psychologist to conduct an evaluation, the psychologist is not required to obtain\na written release from the individual being evaluated.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect To be consistent with the requirements of the Ethics Code, psychologists should always discuss the limits\nof con\u0000dentiality."}, {"question": "A client you have been seeing in therapy for three months tells you that her former therapist made repeated sexual advances toward her. She tells you that she does not want you to say anything about it to anyone and that she just wants to \"get over it.\" As an ethical psychologist, you should _______________.", "options": {"A": "inform the client that you are ethically obligated to make a report to the state licensing board", "B": "convince the client that she should make a report to the appropriate authorities", "C": "tell the client that you will maintain con\u0000dentiality", "D": "tell the client that you must \u0000le a complaint with APA but that you will not reveal her name"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "It is important to keep in mind that client con\u0000dentiality nearly always takes priority over other concerns. You would not want to make a report\nunless you had permission from the client.\nAnswer A: In this situation, you are not required to make a report since the client has not waived con\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer B: \"Convincing\" the client would not be the best course of action.\nAnswer D: Although you could \u0000le a complaint without naming the client, the Ethics Committee would be unable to take action in most cases."}, {"question": "Dr. Yang, a psychologist, is hired by a company to administer tests for the purpose of evaluating current employees to determine if they should be considered for promotion. In this situation ____________________.", "options": {"A": "an informed consent from examinees does not need to be obtained", "B": "an informed consent is not required since the employer is Dr. Yang's \"client\"", "C": "an informed consent from examinees is required, and they should be reminded about the limits of con\u0000dentiality", "D": "it is up to Dr. Yang to decide whether any information about the test should be given to examinees"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "This issue is addressed in the APA's Code of Ethics. According to Standard 9.03 (a)2, one exception to informed consent in testing is \"when....\ninformed consent is implied because testing is conducted as a routine educational, institutional, or organizational activity (e.g., when\nparticipants voluntarily agree to assessment when applying for a job)...\"\nAnswer B. The fact that the employer is the client does not automatically eliminate the need for an informed consent.\nAnswer C. While reminding the examinees about limits to con\u0000dentiality is best practice, an informed consent is not required based on the\nAPA's code of ethics.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. Ethical responsibilities are not left up to psychologists' discretion."}, {"question": "To encourage college students to participate in her research study, Dr. LaPlace offers students free admittance to a popular four-hour workshop, \"Finding a Meaningful Relationship and Keeping It,\" that is offered by one of her colleagues. This policy _________.", "options": {"A": "is unacceptable since it is coercive and, therefore, violates the provisions of the Ethics Code", "B": "is unacceptable since it represents a multiple relationship and, therefore, violates the provisions of the Ethics Code", "C": "is unacceptable since it represents a \"con\u0000ict of interest\" and, therefore, violates the provisions of the Ethics Code", "D": "may be acceptable as long as Dr. LaPlace is careful to clarify any risks, obligations, and so on with the students"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The Ethics Code does not prohibit the use of inducements for encouraging people to participate in a research study but does place some\nlimitations on their use. Standard 8.06(b) of the Code states that in offering professional services as an inducement to potential research\nparticipants, \"psychologists clarify the nature of the services, as well as the risks, obligations, and limitations.\nAnswer A. The situation described in this question cannot really be considered coercive. It would be coercive if the research was being\nconducted in a prison and prisoners were led to believe that they would receive special privileges if they participate in the research study.\nAnswer B. There may be cases when this would be true, but there is not any information in the question to assume that a multiple (dual)\nrelationship is relevant to this situation.\nAnswer C. A con\u0000ict of interest is not relevant to this situation."}, {"question": "Dr. Abad, a psychologist, has been asked to serve as a fact witness by the plaintiff's attorney. The plaintiff was in a car accident three months ago and is claiming that he has suffered from persistent emotional problems as a result of the accident. He has been seeing Dr. Abad in therapy for these problems for several weeks. During her testimony in court, Dr. Abad is asked by the plaintiff's attorney whether, in her opinion, the patient's emotional di\u0000culties are directly related to the accident. Dr. Abad should ___________________.", "options": {"A": "comply with the request as long as she also presents any information regarding possible limitations of her opinion", "B": "comply with request only if her evaluation of the plaintiff has provided her with su\u0000cient information for an informed opinion", "C": "comply with the request only if the plaintiff has waived his right to con\u0000dentiality", "D": "not comply with the request unless she is ordered to do so by the court"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The distinction between a fact witness and an expert witness is an important one. As their names imply, a fact witness testi\u0000es only to the\nfacts, while an expert witness can offer an opinion. Note that, in this question, Dr. Abad has been asked to serve as a fact witness. This issue is\naddressed by T. P. Remley in \"Preparing for court appearances,\" American Counseling Association, Alexandria, VA, 1991. Remley notes that a\nfact witness should not render an opinion at a hearing or trial and, if asked to give an opinion, should note that he/she is not in a position to do\nso because of potential bias, insu\u0000cient information on which to base an opinion, etc. Note that a psychologist may be required to give an\nopinion when ordered to do so by the court. However, even in this situation, the psychologist should qualify the opinion with a statement about\nits potential limitations.\nAnswer A. The psychologist in this case should not give an opinion unless ordered to do so by the court. In that case, she would also present\ninformation regarding the limits of that opinion.\nAnswer B. As a fact witness, Dr. Abad should not provide an opinion.\nAnswer C. This answer is incorrect as it fails to address the more central issue in this question regarding the difference between a fact witness\nand an expert witness."}, {"question": "A psychologist is hired by the court to evaluate the two young children of a couple who are seeking a divorce. The psychologist's job will be to evaluate both children and report his \u0000ndings to the court along with any recommendations regarding custody he can make on the basis of his \u0000ndings. Through her attorney, the mother learns that the children have both revealed information to the psychologist that presents her in an unfavorable light. The mother contacts the psychologist and says that, since her children are both minors, she is the holder of the privilege and is, therefore, instructing the psychologist not to report the unfavorable information to the court. The psychologist should __________________________.", "options": {"A": "comply with the mother's request to not reveal the unfavorable information since she is the holder of the privilege", "B": "provide the court with information about the results of the evaluation as long as he believes his evaluation is nonbiased", "C": "provide the court with the information he feels is in the best interests of the children", "D": "refuse to testify unless court-ordered to do so"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "In custody cases, a psychologist must always put the best interests of the child \u0000rst. Therefore, he should provide the court with the\ninformation he feels is in the best interests of the children.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. As noted by E. J. Kermani (\"Handbook of Psychiatry and the Law,\" Chicago, Year Book Medical Publ., Inc.,\n1989), the concept of privilege \"speci\u0000es that the parents' right must yield to the best interest of the child\" regardless of who requested the\nevaluation in a custody case. In other words, parents cannot claim privilege or refrain from waiving privilege in legal matters pertaining to\ncustody.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. The psychologist was hired by the court to conduct an objective evaluation. Throughout the process, the\npsychologist should strive to assure an unbiased assessment.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect, because the psychologist was hired by the court, so he does not need a court order to testify."}, {"question": "The court offers a defendant the choice of jail or an in-patient alcohol treatment program after he is found guilty of driving while intoxicated. As the clinician who will be working with the man at the treatment center, you should keep in mind that _______________________.", "options": {"A": "a waiver of con\u0000dentiality is not required because treatment has been court-ordered", "B": "a waiver of con\u0000dentiality is not required because the man gave up his right to con\u0000dentiality by committing the crime", "C": "a waiver of con\u0000dentiality is not required because the man gave up his right to con\u0000dentiality by choosing the treatment program instead of jail", "D": "a waiver of con\u0000dentiality is required and should be obtained before you release any information about the man's treatment to the court"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The usual procedure is for the individual to sign a waiver of con\u0000dentiality when he/she chooses the treatment option. However, you would\nwant to verify this has occurred and if not, you would want to obtain a signed waiver. Even though the treatment is court-ordered, a waiver of\ncon\u0000dentiality is required before any information about the man's treatment is released to the court.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. A waiver is required before con\u0000dential information can be released.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. The patient maintains his rights, including his right to privacy. Without a waiver, records should not be\nreleased to the court.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. The treatment center cannot release any records without the patient's consent."}, {"question": "If a health care provider is faced with a con\u0000ict between state law and the HIPAA requirements _________.", "options": {"A": "state law would preempt the HIPAA requirements", "B": "the health care provider should respond based on the best interests of the client", "C": "the HIPAA requirement would preempt state law when it provides the client with greater privacy protection or control over access to his/her records", "D": "the health care provider has the discretion to resolve the issue in any appropriate manner"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The HIPAA Privacy Rule provides a Federal \u0000oor of privacy protections for individuals' individually identi\u0000able health information where that\ninformation is held by a covered entity or by a business associate of the covered entity. State laws that are contrary to the Privacy Rule are\npreempted by the Federal requirements, unless a speci\u0000c exception applies. These exceptions include if the State law (1) relates to the privacy\nof individually identi\u0000able health information and provides greater privacy protections or privacy rights with respect to such information, (2)\nprovides for the reporting of disease or injury, child abuse, birth, or death, or for public health surveillance, investigation, or intervention, or (3)\nrequires certain health plan reporting, such as for management or \u0000nancial audits. In these circumstances, a covered entity is not required to\ncomply with a contrary provision of the Privacy Rule [See California O\u0000ce Of HIPAA Implementation Frequently Asked Questions at\nhttp://www.ohi.ca.gov/calohi/#].\nAnswer A. Federal laws preempt state laws in this case.\nAnswer B. This answer does not re\u0000ect the requirements of HIPAA.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect, because it is vague and does not re\u0000ect the language of HIPAA."}, {"question": "Employee X is thinking about \u0000ling a sexual harassment suit against her boss, Supervisor Y. He makes a habit of complimenting Employee X on how she looks, and this makes Employee X feel very uncomfortable. Employee X decides to tell Supervisor Y that she \u0000nds his behavior offensive. Supervisor Y says he \"means no harm\" but that he'll stop if that's what she wants. This situation ________.", "options": {"A": "represents a sexual harassment suit because Supervisor Y is in a position of authority", "B": "represents a sexual harassment suit because Supervisor Y's comments have created a hostile work environment for Employee X", "C": "represents a sexual harassment suit only if it is determined that a \"reasonable woman\" would \u0000nd Supervisor Y's behavior offensive", "D": "does not represent a sexual harassment suit as long as Supervisor Y actually stops making comments about Employee X's appearance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Sexual harassment laws and policies continue to evolve and be reinterpreted. If the harassment is not severe and the person stops when\nasked, it is unlikely that the act would be found to constitute sexual harassment. In this case, complimenting the employee does not seem to\nbe grounds for a sexual harassment suit. For additional information on this issue, see the discussion of Standard 3.02 of the Ethics Code in the\nchapter on ethics and professional issues in the written study materials.\nAnswer A. When the harasser is in a position of power, this increases the likelihood that a claimant will win a suit. However, the harasser's\nstatus is not all that is taken into consideration.\nAnswer B. If Supervisor Y continues with his comments after being asked to stop, then this might be the case. Answer C. The \"reasonable\nwoman\" standard is used to determine if a person's actions constitute sexual harassment but, as noted above, if the supervisor stops his\nbehavior, it is unlikely that the employee would have grounds for a suit against him."}, {"question": "Dr. Green sets his clients' fees on the basis of a \"sliding scale\" that is based on their current income. This practice is _______________________________.", "options": {"A": "acceptable but not explicitly mentioned in the Ethics Code", "B": "unacceptable but not explicitly mentioned in the Ethics Code", "C": "explicitly recommended in the Ethics Code", "D": "explicitly prohibited in the Ethics Code"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Sliding scale fees are not explicitly addressed in the Ethics Code. Sliding scale fees are generally considered acceptable as long as they are fair\nand serve the best interests of the client.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Sliding scale fees are generally considered acceptable.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. The codes do not speci\u0000cally address sliding scale fees.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The codes do not prohibit the practice of using a sliding scale."}, {"question": "Shortly after a school psychologist begins working for a small rural school district, she \u0000nds that there are a number of children in a class for students with an Intellectual Disability who are emotionally disturbed but who appear to have an average or above average level of intelligence. The county has no class for emotionally disturbed children. The psychologist should _____________________________.", "options": {"A": "report the situation immediately to the psychology ethics committee in her state", "B": "report the situation immediately to the teacher's standards and practices commission in her state", "C": "threaten to quit if the children are not placed in a regular classroom", "D": "evaluate each student and recommend an appropriate action regardless of available options"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This question presents a rather complex ethical dilemma. Before responding too quickly, consider the psychologist's responsibilities and the\noptions that are available. The psychologist's \u0000rst intervention should be to assess each child and make appropriate recommendations. If\nappropriate actions are not taken by the school, the psychologist will then want to consider other interventions (e.g., talking to the school\nboard, consulting with the ethics committee). For ethics questions, the most conservative course of action is often the most appropriate one.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. At \u0000rst glance, reporting may seem appropriate. However, the psychology ethics committee does not\nrespond to complaints about the unethical activities of teachers and schools. Answer B. This answer is incorrect. There is no indication that\nthe inappropriateness of this situation is the responsibility of a particular teacher.\nAnswer C. This answer is incorrect. A psychologist should always try to correct a situation before quitting. Insisting that a situation be resolved\nand threatening to quit is not necessarily the most ethical or useful response."}, {"question": "During the \u0000rst session with a client, you learn that he has a history of offenses that you \u0000nd di\u0000cult to deal with and feel, therefore, that you would not like working with him. As an ethical psychologist you should _________________________.", "options": {"A": "disregard your personal concerns and accept the young man into therapy", "B": "refer the young man to another professional", "C": "accept the young man into therapy but seek supervision", "D": "tell the young man that you cannot accept him into therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The therapeutic alliance is considered a crucial factor in therapy outcome. If a therapist believes she or he cannot develop an alliance, it would\nnot bene\u0000t the client to continue treatment. It is not a violation of the Ethics Code to fail to accept a client into treatment when the psychologist\nfeels that he/she would be unable to form a therapeutic relationship with the client. Such a decision would be consistent with Ethics Code\nStandard 3.04 (Avoiding Harm). Therefore, the best, and most ethical, course of action in this situation is to refer the client to another\nprofessional (see Standard 2.06).\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. It is unethical for a psychologist to accept a client into therapy if the psychologist believes a therapeutic\nrelationship cannot be established.\nAnswer C. This answer is incorrect. Supervision may not be adequate to overcome the concerns of the psychologist.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. Although it is acceptable to refer a client to another therapist, referrals must be handled in a professional\nmanner. Abrupt termination is unethical."}, {"question": "You receive a voicemail from a well-respected licensed psychologist in your community who is currently seeing one of your former clients. She says that she has obtained the client's consent and wants you to forward the client's record to her. You should ___________________________.", "options": {"A": "forward a photocopy (not the original) of the record to her", "B": "forward only information that you feel is relevant and not obsolete", "C": "contact the client to obtain a release directly from him", "D": "wait until you hear directly from the client before taking any action"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This is the best option of those given. You would want to verify that the client has signed a release and discuss the release and its potential\nconsequences with him before providing the psychologist with the requested information.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Even though the psychologist is licensed and well-respected, you would not want to breach con\u0000dentiality\nby forwarding client information without knowing speci\u0000cally to what the client has consented (or that, in fact, he gave his consent).\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect, because it misses the larger issue, which is that you need to con\u0000rm the client has signed a release and\nwhat the release includes.\nAnswer: D: This answer is incorrect. Waiting to hear from a former client may take more time than is reasonable, especially if the client is in\ncrisis and your records would be helpful."}, {"question": "With regard to the termination of professional services, the Ethics Code requires psychologists to _____________________________________.", "options": {"A": "offer to help the client locate alternative services", "B": "take actions that protect the best interests of the client", "C": "provide pretermination counseling and provide referrals or alternative services, except where precluded by the actions of clients/patients", "D": "take action that is consistent with the reason(s) for the termination"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Standard 10.10(c) of the Ethics Code applies in this situation. It advises that certain steps be taken, regardless of the reason for the\ntermination. Speci\u0000cally, it reads, \"Except where precluded by the actions of clients/patients or third-party payors, prior to termination\npsychologists provide pretermination counseling and suggest alternative service providers as appropriate.\" This answer comes closest to the\nactual language of the Ethics Code and, therefore, is the best answer.\nAnswer A: The language in this answer does not align as well with that of the Ethics Code. Psychologists should \"suggest alternative service\nproviders,\" but are not instructed to \"help the client locate an alternative provider.\"\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect as it is vague and does not address the speci\u0000c ethical obligations inherent in termination.\nAnswer D. This answer fails to address the ethical responsibilities in this situation."}, {"question": "The primary function of the psychology licensing board is best described by which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Establishment of minimal standards of competence", "B": "Limitation of access to the profession", "C": "Protection of the public through the collection of fees for licensure", "D": "The establishment, monitoring, and enforcement of ethical principles"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Although the various state and provincial boards have different requirements for licensure, they all set the minimum requirements for licensure.\nLicensing boards establish and monitor entry-level quali\u0000cations required to offer services to the public for a fee under the title \"psychologist\";\nby doing so, the boards helps ensure competence.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. The licensing boards do not function to limit access to the profession. Answer C. This answer is incorrect.\nAlthough licensing is designed to protect the public, this protection is not accomplished through the collection of fees.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. The APA, not the licensing boards, establishes, monitors, and enforces the ethical principles."}, {"question": "You are working as a school psychologist. A teacher asks you to evaluate one of her students. After talking with the teacher and the student, you begin to suspect that the teacher is a signi\u0000cant part of the problem. As an ethical psychologist, you would ______________________________.", "options": {"A": "\u0000le a formal report with the School Board", "B": "discuss the problem with the child's parents immediately", "C": "talk to the teacher about your suspicions", "D": "report the teacher to the local ethics committee"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This is the most \"conservative\" of the responses given and most consistent with the \"spirit\" of the Ethics Code, which requires psychologists to\nattempt to informally resolve problems \u0000rst rather than immediately contacting the ethics committee or other authority.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. Initial steps toward resolution should involve parties closer to the problem. Answer B. This answer is\nincorrect. Although this problem is not dealt with directly by the Ethics Code, it does recommend an informal resolution with other\npsychologists whenever appropriate. Presumably, this would apply to other professionals as well.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. Trying to resolve the issue directly is the preferred approach."}, {"question": "The function of the state licensing boards is probably best described as ___________________________.", "options": {"A": "ensuring that psychologists provide clients with effective services", "B": "ensuring that only competent psychologists become and remain licensed", "C": "setting entry-level quali\u0000cations for licensure and monitoring the conduct of licensed psychologists", "D": "de\u0000ning the services that can be legitimately provided by licensed psychologists"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This is the most accurate description of the function of the licensing boards. As you are likely aware, the state licensing boards set the\nminimum requirements for licensure.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. Although this may be a desired outcome of the licensing requirements, it can't be assumed that licensure\nwill result in the provision of effective services.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. Licensing boards cannot guarantee competence.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect, because it fails to describe the role of state licensing boards."}, {"question": "With regard to information revealed by a client in the context of the client-therapist relationship, who is the \"holder of the privilege\"?", "options": {"A": "The client", "B": "The therapist", "C": "The court", "D": "Both the client and the therapist"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The psychotherapist-patient privilege refers to the legal right of the client to have his/her psychotherapy records and disclosure withheld in a\nlegal proceeding. This privilege is state law in all 50 states, though the laws related to privilege and exceptions to privilege vary somewhat from\nstate to state.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. The therapist does not hold the privilege.\nAnswer C. This answer is incorrect. The client is ordinarily the holder of the privilege.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. In all states, the client is ordinarily the holder of the privilege, which means that it is up to the client to\ndecide whether to exercise or waive the privilege."}, {"question": "A psychology intern administers tests to clients of a mental health clinic, but her supervisor scores the tests and interprets their results. When preparing the psychological report, the supervisor does not indicate that the intern administered the tests. This is __________.", "options": {"A": "ethical as long as the client was informed that the examiner is a psychology intern and was given the supervisor's name", "B": "ethical since the supervisor is ultimately responsible for the accuracy of the psychological report", "C": "unethical since the intern should not be administering psychological tests", "D": "unethical since the intern should be listed as the examiner in the report"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This issue is not directly addressed in the Ethics Code or the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing. However, this response is\nmost consistent with the provisions of the APA's guidelines. See, for example, Standard 5.01(b) of the Ethics Code, which prohibits\npsychologists from making misleading statements, and Standard 6.06, which requires psychologists to provide accurate information to payors\nand funding sources. Answer A. This answer is Incorrect. Informing the client the examiner is an intern and providing the supervisor's name are\nnecessary, but not su\u0000cient in reference to this question.\nAnswer B. To adhere to the standards noted in the explanation for Answer D, the psychologist should include the intern's name as the person\nwho delivered the assessment.\nAnswer C. Provided the intern had the proper training and was supervised by the psychologist, it is not unethical for the intern to deliver the\nassessment."}, {"question": "A licensed psychologist who is working in an isolated rural community \u0000nds that some of his clients have problems that are beyond his training and expertise. The psychologist should ______________________.", "options": {"A": "continue seeing the clients only if there is no other psychologist available", "B": "use only those interventions he feels competent to use in treating the clients", "C": "obtain appropriate consultation by phone", "D": "refuse to see the clients until he receives adequate training"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Ethics Code requires psychologists to provide only services that are within \"the boundaries of their competence.\" However, it is acceptable\nto provide new services when certain conditions are met (Standard 2.01). It is perfectly acceptable for psychologists to acquire new skills\nduring the course of their practice as long as they obtain adequate training, supervision, or consultation. Of course, consultation might not be\nsu\u0000cient in certain situations, but, of the responses given, this is the best choice. Note that nothing in the Code suggests that consultation\nmust be in-person. None of the responses to this question is optimal, so you have to pick the best one of those given.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. The lack of another psychologist is certainly a consideration in this situation, but, even in the absence of\nanother psychologist, seeing the clients without getting necessary supervision or consultation would not be acceptable.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. This would not be acceptable if more effective treatments for the clients' problems were available.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. Not providing services at all would not be the best course of action in most situations if the clients had no\nother professional they could see."}, {"question": "A psychologist is asked to evaluate a prisoner for the purpose of making a recommendation about the prisoner's readiness for parole. To be consistent with ethical guidelines, the psychologist should ______________________.", "options": {"A": "refuse to do the evaluation since making a recommendation in this situation is prohibited", "B": "conduct the evaluation but base his recommendation on the results of standardized tests only", "C": "conduct the evaluation as long as the prisoner has been told the purpose of the evaluation and has been warned about the limits on con\u0000dentiality", "D": "conduct the evaluation after obtaining a consent from the prisoner's legal representative and report the results without making a recommendation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This response is most consistent with the requirements of Standard 9.03(a) of the Ethics Code and Paragraph 6.03 of the Specialty Guidelines\nfor Forensic Psychology (APA, 2012). Special precaution is required when conducting evaluations and making recommendations in forensic\nsettings.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. Making a recommendation is not prohibited by APA guidelines.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. The techniques used by psychologists must be valid, but there is nothing that says a psychologist can only\nuse standardized tests.\nAnswer D. This answer is incorrect. Again, a recommendation is not prohibited, so this response is less desirable."}, {"question": "According to the APA's General Guidelines for Providers of Psychological Services, the term \"psychological services\" applies to all of the following except _________________________________.", "options": {"A": "administration and scoring of psychological tests", "B": "teaching psychology at a university", "C": "supervising delivery of psychological services", "D": "conducting psychotherapy in private practice"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "As noted in the ethics and professional issues chapter of the written study materials, teaching psychology at a university is not listed as a\npsychological service in the General Guidelines for Providers of Psychological Services.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect, as the administration and scoring of psychological tests is a psychological service.\nAnswer C. This answer is incorrect. Supervision of psychological services is considered a psychological service. Answer D. This answer is\nincorrect. Psychotherapy is considered a psychological service."}, {"question": "An intern at a large mental health clinic decides to work at another agency and makes appointments to see several of the clinic's clients at her new place of employment. Her actions are ______________________.", "options": {"A": "commendable since it makes it possible for the clients to have continuity in therapy", "B": "ethical as long as adequate supervision is available at the agency", "C": "unethical because she has previously worked with the clients", "D": "unethical because the clients are already receiving services from the clinic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Standard 10.04 of the APA's Ethics Code states that, \"in deciding whether to offer or provide services to those already receiving mental health\nservices elsewhere, psychologists carefully consider the treatment issues and the potential client's/patient's welfare, psychologists discuss\nthese issues with the client/patient in order to minimize the risk of confusion and con\u0000ict, consult with the other service providers when\nappropriate, and proceed with caution and sensitivity to the therapeutic issues.\" This response is most in line with Standard 10.04. By going\nahead and making the appointments, the intern is not taking steps to minimize the risk of confusion and con\u0000ict and is not consulting with the\nother service providers.\nAnswer A. This answer is incorrect. As suggested by this Standard, interns should not take clients from a previous place of employment\nwithout considering the professional relationships involved, the therapeutic issues, and the welfare of the clients.\nAnswer B. This answer is incorrect. Interns should not see clients without supervision. However, even if appropriate supervision is available, an\nintern should not offer his/her services to clients of a previous place of employment.\nAnswer C. This answer is incorrect. This response really does not make sense."}, {"question": "In terms of incremental validity, which of the following situations most supports the use of a new predictor?", "options": {"A": "Moderate base rate with many applicants and few job openings", "B": "Low base rate with many applicants and few job openings", "C": "Moderate base rate with few applicants and many job openings", "D": "Low base rate with few applicants and many job openings"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The degree to which a new selection technique will increase decision-making accuracy depends on several factors including the base rate\n(proportion of correct decisions without the new technique) and the selection ratio (ratio of applicants to job openings). A moderate base rate\nsuggests that there's room for improvement and, therefore, that a new predictor is likely to increase decision-making accuracy. Also, the\nsituation is optimal when there are many applicants to choose from (a low selection ratio).\nAnswer B: A low base rate suggests that something else besides selection is the problem - e.g., the company's standards of success are too\nhigh or employees need training in order to do the job satisfactorily.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"moderate base rate with few applicants and many job openings\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"low base rate with few applicants and many job openings\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Equity theory predicts that a husband will feel best about his marriage if:", "options": {"A": "he and his wife give and receive the same amount in their relationship.", "B": "in terms of their relationship, his give/receive ratio is the same as or lesser than the give/receive ratio of his wife.", "C": "in terms of their relationship, his give/receive ratio is the same as or greater than the give/receive ratio of his wife.", "D": "in terms of their relationship, he perceives that his give/receive ratio is the same as the give/receive ratio of his wife."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Equity theory is a cognitive theory of motivation that emphasizes the impact of the individual's beliefs about the equity of the situation in terms\nof inputs and outcomes. When a person perceives the situation to be equitable, he/she will be satis\u0000ed and motivated. According to equity\ntheory, a person compares his/her input/outcome ratio to that of others. If he/she perceives the ratios to be similar, this has positive effects on\nsatisfaction and motivation.\nAnswers A, B, and C: These responses provide incorrect statements. See explanation for response D."}, {"question": "According to Super's lifespan model of career development, a major task of the ________ stage is to update one's skills through training.", "options": {"A": "growth", "B": "disengagement", "C": "exploration", "D": "maintenance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Super's (1980) model of career development distinguishes between \u0000ve stages: growth (ages 4-13), exploration (14-24), establishment (ages\n25-44), maintenance (ages 45-65), and disengagement (ages 65+). During the maintenance stage, the primary tasks are staying competitive\nwith younger workers by obtaining necessary training and developing plans for retirement.\nAnswer A: A primary task for the initial growth stage is to develop an understanding of the meaning and purpose of work.\nAnswer B: A primary task of the \u0000nal disengagement phase is to gradually separate from paid employment.\nAnswer C: The tasks of the exploration phase include becoming aware of one's abilities and interests and learning about the different types of\nwork."}, {"question": "An evaluator wants to assess the bene\u0000ts received by client participation in a program. Which approach to program evaluation should they use?", "options": {"A": "Goals-based", "B": "Process-based", "C": "Outcomes-based", "D": "Assistance-based"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Outcomes-based program evaluation focuses on identifying and evaluating the bene\u0000ts gained by participation in a program, and is typically\nused for evaluation of non-pro\u0000t programs.\nGoals-based evaluations focus on how well the program is achieving set objectives, so answer A is incorrect. Process-based evaluations\nregard the effectiveness of the programs systems and structures, so answer B is incorrect.\nAnswer D, assistance-based, is not an approach to program evaluation and thus is incorrect."}, {"question": "A supervisor administers a test on the material covered in a training program to employees who have just completed the program in order to assess its effectiveness. Six months later, the same supervisor rates the employees on their job performance and correlates the two sets of scores. She obtains a correlation coe\u0000cient of .65. Most likely, the magnitude of the coe\u0000cient has been biased by which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Shrinkage", "B": "Criterion contamination", "C": "A contrast effect", "D": "Demand characteristics"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "In the situation described in this question, the same person is rating employees on the predictor and criterion. When knowledge of a person's\nperformance on the predictor affects how he/she is rated on the criterion, criterion contamination has occurred and is likely to in\u0000ate the\ncriterion-related validity coe\u0000cient. A coe\u0000cient of .65 is relatively high and may re\u0000ect criterion contamination.\nAnswer A: Shrinkage is associated with cross-validation.\nAnswer C: The contrast effect occurs when the ability of previous interviewees or applicants affects how a subsequent interviewee or applicant\nis evaluated.\nAnswer D: Demand characteristics are cues in the environment that inform research participants what behaviors are expected from them."}, {"question": "You would be concerned about a selection test's differential validity for males and females when you discover that:", "options": {"A": "the Y-intercept for the regression line is higher for males than for females.", "B": "the X-intercept for the regression line is higher for males than for females.", "C": "the slope of the regression lines for males and females are different.", "D": "females consistently obtain lower scores than males on the predictor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Differential validity refers to different validity coe\u0000cients for different groups of people. The slope of the regression line is directly related to the\nmagnitude of the validity coe\u0000cient. If the slopes differ for males and females, this indicates that the validity coe\u0000cients are different for\nmales and females.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"the Y-intercept for the regression line is higher for males than for females\", is incorrect. See explanation for response C.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"the X-intercept for the regression line is higher for males than for females\", is incorrect. See explanation for response C.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"females consistently obtain lower scores than males on the predictor\", is incorrect. See explanation for response C."}, {"question": "When applying Bandura's social learning theory to training in the workplace, you would use which of the following techniques?", "options": {"A": "Vestibule training", "B": "Job rotation", "C": "Behavioral modeling", "D": "Miniature job training"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Knowing that Bandura's research linked learning to observation would have enabled you to select the correct answer to this question. Bandura's\nsocial learning theory suggests that behavioral modeling would be an effective job training technique.\nAnswer A: Vestibule training involves the use of a simulation of the actual work environment.\nAnswer B: Job rotation involves \"rotating\" through several different jobs.\nAnswer D: Miniature job training involves providing a job applicant with some training to determine if he/she will bene\u0000t from it."}, {"question": "According to Donald Super, \"career maturity\" refers to:", "options": {"A": "the \u0000nal stage of career development.", "B": "the ability to accomplish the tasks of each stage of career development.", "C": "the ability to make realistic career choices.", "D": "the acquisition of skills needed to successfully perform one's chosen job."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Super proposes that career development involves several stages. Career maturity refers to successful accomplishment of the tasks of each\nstage of career development.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"the \u0000nal stage of career development\", is incorrect. See explanation for response B.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"the ability to make realistic career choices\", is incorrect. See explanation for response B.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"the acquisition of skills needed to successfully perform one's chosen job\", is incorrect. See explanation for response B."}, {"question": "Kurt Lewin (1951) described the process of organizational change as involving three stages. These stages are:", "options": {"A": "plan, act, and evaluate.", "B": "unfreezing, changing, and refreezing.", "C": "entry, planning, and action.", "D": "collecting data, providing feedback, and implementing/developing action plans."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Lewin's (1951) model of organizational change was among the \u0000rst to be proposed and involves three stages. This answer correctly identi\u0000es\nLewin's three stages.\nAnswer A: Plan, act, and evaluate are not the stages identi\u0000ed by Lewin.\nAnswer C: Huse (1980) subsequently modi\u0000ed Lewin's model of organizational change by adding steps to the three stages. This answer\nidenti\u0000es three of the steps (entry and planning are steps in the unfreezing stage, while action is the primary step in the changing stage).\nAnswer D: These are the three steps of survey feedback."}, {"question": "In his theory of career development, Donald Super uses which of the following to pictorially depict the various roles that a person participates in during the course of his/her life?", "options": {"A": "Rainbow", "B": "Arch", "C": "Ladder", "D": "Web"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Super uses several \u0000gures to illustrate and integrate aspects of career development. Super's Life Career Rainbow illustrates the various roles\npeople play (e.g., student, parent, worker) and how these roles correspond to a person's life stages.\nAnswer B: Super's arch illustrates the personal and environmental factors that determine a person's career path.\nAnswer C: The ladder depicts the stages in Super's career development model.\nAnswer D: The web illustrates the bases of career maturity."}, {"question": "The \"risky shift phenomenon\" is a potential problem when:", "options": {"A": "employees are low in both motivation and ability.", "B": "employees work together to derive a work-related decision.", "C": "a subjective rating scale is used to evaluate employee performance.", "D": "a controversial message is delivered by a highly credible communicator."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The research has shown that group decision-making is associated with a number of potential liabilities including the risky shift phenomenon.\nThe risky shift is the tendency for decisions made by a group to be less conservative (more risky) than the decisions made by individuals\nworking alone. For additional information on the risky shift and related phenomena, see the Industrial-Organizational Psychology chapter of the\nAATBS written study materials.\nAnswer A: This sounds more like Hersy and Blanchard's (1974) situational leadership model.\nAnswer C: This sounds more like a type of criterion measure of employee performance.\nAnswer D: This sounds more like a concept regarding types of communication and their effects on perception."}, {"question": "In organizations, quality circles (QCs):", "options": {"A": "consist of workers who make work-related decisions that were previously made by management.", "B": "consist of workers who make suggestions to management about solutions to work-related problems.", "C": "include a designated internal change agent who is responsible for initiating, guiding, and evaluating a change effort.", "D": "include an external change agent who helps employees adapt to change."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "QCs consist of a small team of employees who work together on a particular job and meet voluntarily to discuss solutions to job productivity\nproblems. A feature that distinguishes QCs from other types of work teams is that members of QCs do not make \u0000nal decisions about\nsolutions to problems but, instead, recommend their solutions to management.\nAnswer A: This is not associated with quality circles (QCs).\nAnswer C: This does not necessarily have to be the case but is another method of organizational change and development.\nAnswer D: This is not possible as external change agents are \"external\" and QCs are made up of employees from within the company. However,\nthis is yet another method of organizational change and development."}, {"question": "Lawler's (1973) model of facet satisfaction:", "options": {"A": "is similar to equity theory and assumes that job satisfaction is affected by comparisons of one's own inputs and outcomes to the inputs and outcomes of others.", "B": "is similar to need hierarchy theory and assumes that job satisfaction is related to the ful\u0000llment of one's most prepotent needs.", "C": "is similar to two factor theory and views satisfaction and dissatisfaction as independent factors.", "D": "is similar to goal setting theory and proposes that participation in the setting of work objectives is crucial for job satisfaction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Lawler's model of facet satisfaction is based on the premise that job satisfaction is a product of comparisons of one's job inputs, the inputs of\nothers, and the job's characteristics to one's outcomes and the outcomes of others. Like equity theory, Lawler's model predicts that workers\ncompare their own input/outcome ratios to the input/outcome ratios of comparable others. When the ratios are the same, the worker is\nsatis\u0000ed; when the worker's own ratio is larger than that of comparable others, the worker may feel guilt or other discomfort; and when the\nworker's ratio is less than that of comparable others, he/she is dissatis\u0000ed.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"is similar to need hierarchy theory and assumes that job satisfaction is related to the ful\u0000llment of one's most\nprepotent needs\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"is similar to two factor theory and views satisfaction and dissatisfaction as independent factors\", is incorrect. See\nexplanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"is similar to goal setting theory and proposes that participation in the setting of work objectives is crucial for job\nsatisfaction\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Calculation of the utility of a training program in terms of dollar value includes which of the following?", "options": {"A": "T x d x C", "B": "T - d x C", "C": "T x C - d", "D": "T + C + d"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Utility refers to the cost effectiveness of training and can be calculated using the following formula: $U = T x N x d x SD - N x C where: $U =\nutility measured in terms of dollar value, T = number of years duration of the training program's effects on performance, N = number of people\ntrained, d = effect size of the training program, SD = standard deviation of job performance in dollars, and C = per person cost of training. As\nshown above, T x d x C is part of the formula for calculating utility.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"T - d x C\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"T x C - d\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"T + C + d\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Transformational leaders:", "options": {"A": "are likely to have a \"dark side.\"", "B": "use their legitimate status to gain compliance.", "C": "appeal to the self-interest of their followers.", "D": "seek to empower their followers."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Transformational leaders recognize the need for change (transformation) and are able to communicate that vision to their followers and gain\ntheir commitment to it. One characteristic of transformational leaders is that they attempt to raise the level of consciousness, awareness, and\ncommitment of their followers and to strengthen their ability to control their own destinies.\nAnswer A: Some experts describe a charismatic leader as having a \"dark side,\" but it's not a characteristic ordinarily associated with\ntransformational leaders.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"use their legitimate status to gain compliance\", sounds more like transactional leaders (who are contrasted with\ntransformational leaders).\nAnswer C: The answer, \"appeal to the self-interest of their followers\", also sounds more like a transactional leader."}, {"question": "Social loa\u0000ng is most likely to occur when members of a team are less productive than individual members would have been working alone because:", "options": {"A": "they believe their lack of effort will not be recognized by others.", "B": "they experience a diminished sense of self-awareness while in the presence of others.", "C": "the mere presence of others produces a level of arousal that interferes with productivity.", "D": "they experience an enhancement of pre-existing tendencies."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Social loa\u0000ng is one of the possible negative outcomes of group participation. Social loa\u0000ng is the tendency of people to exert less effort when\nworking as a member of a group than they would exert when working alone. It has been attributed to the belief that one's effort (or lack of\neffort) will not be recognized or that group outcomes do not depend on one's effort.\nAnswer B: The \"experience of a diminished sense of self-awareness while in the presence of others\" is incorrect as it is not an \"experience\" but\na belief, and the belief is that their lack of effort with not be recognized by others. Answer C: This sounds more like social inhibition.\nAnswer D: This sounds more related to selective exposure theory."}, {"question": "Which of the following is speci\u0000cally excluded as a \"disability\" in the Americans with Disabilities Act?", "options": {"A": "HIV/AIDS", "B": "Learning disabilities", "C": "Transvestism", "D": "Diabetes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Disability is de\u0000ned in the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) as \"a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits one or more of\nthe major life activities\" of an individual. The ADA lists several conditions that do NOT qualify as disabilities. These include transvestism,\nexhibitionism, gender identity disorder not resulting from a physical impairment, compulsive gambling, kleptomania, and use of illegal drugs.\nAnswer A: HIV/AIDS is included as a disability.\nAnswer B: Dyslexia and other learning disabilities are included in the de\u0000nition of disability.\nAnswer D: Diabetes is included as a disability."}, {"question": "When using the survey feedback method in an organization, the purpose of the feedback is to:", "options": {"A": "inform individual employees about their individual areas of strength and weakness.", "B": "inform employees about their progress toward meeting organizational goals.", "C": "provide employees with information about the organization's strengths and weaknesses.", "D": "provide managers with information about employees' strengths and weaknesses."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Survey feedback is an organizational development technique. The rationale underlying the use of survey feedback is that, for organizational\nchange to be effective, employees must be aware of the organization's current strengths and weaknesses. The technique entails collecting\ninformation about the organization (especially its problems) and providing that information to employees who then work together to identify\nsolutions to the problems.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"inform individual employees about their individual areas of strength and weakness\", is incorrect. See explanation for\nanswer C.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"inform employees about their progress toward meeting organizational goals\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer\nC.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"provide managers with information about employees' strengths and weaknesses\", is incorrect. See explanation for\nanswer C."}, {"question": "The \"paired comparison\" technique is:", "options": {"A": "a method of job analysis.", "B": "a method of job evaluation.", "C": "a relative measure of job performance.", "D": "an absolute measure of job performance."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The paired comparison technique is a performance appraisal technique. When using the paired comparison technique, all employees are\ncompared to each other in pairs for each dimension of job performance.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"a method of job analysis\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"a method of job evaluation\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"an absolute measure of job performance\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer C."}, {"question": "Brousseau and Driver's (1994) notion of \"career concept\" refers to an individual's:", "options": {"A": "work-related values.", "B": "career-related identity.", "C": "work-related personality characteristics.", "D": "career decisions."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Career concept is a key concept in Brousseau and Driver's model of career development. Career concept refers to an individual's career\ndecisions, which vary along three dimensions: frequency of job change; direction of change; and type of change in job content. Brousseau and\nDriver distinguish between four career concepts - steady state, linear, spiral, and transitory. See, e.g., K. R. Brousseau and M. J. Driver,\nEnhancing informed choice: A career-concepts approach to career advisement, Selections Spring, 24-31, 1994.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"work-related values\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"career-related identity\", describes differential validity. See explanation for answer D.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"work-related personality characteristics\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer D."}, {"question": "Transformational leaders:", "options": {"A": "appeal to the self-interest of their followers.", "B": "help followers recognize that their goals are compatible with the organization's goals.", "C": "induce followers to transcend self-interest for the sake of the organization.", "D": "rede\u0000ne the organization's goals to better coincide with the self-interest of followers."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Transformational leaders motivate followers to accept and support organizational change that is in the best interests of the organization.\nTransformational leaders motivate workers to achieve transcendental goals that go beyond each worker's immediate self-interests.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"appeal to the self-interest of their followers\", is more characteristic of transactional leaders.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"help followers recognize that their goals are compatible with the organization's goals\", is less consistent with\ndescriptions of transformational leaders than answer C.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"rede\u0000ne the organization's goals to better coincide with the self-interest of followers\", is not true about\ntransformational leaders."}, {"question": "Which of the following individuals has proposed that organizational culture acts, at least in part, as a defense mechanism that helps organizational members avoid uncertainty and anxiety?", "options": {"A": "Herbert Simon", "B": "Edgar Schein", "C": "Leon Festinger", "D": "Geert Hofstede"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Knowing that Edgar Schein has written extensively on organizational culture would have helped you choose the correct answer to this question.\nIn his book, Organizational Culture and Leadership(1992), Schein describes the basic assumptions that underlie an organization's culture as\ncognitive defense mechanisms. Additional information about Schein's work on organizational culture is provided in the Industrial-\nOrganizational Psychology chapter of the written study materials.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"Herbert Simon\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"Leon Festinger\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"Geert Hofstede\", is incorrect. See explanation for answer B."}, {"question": "An aerospace corporation uses the procedure known as multiple cutoff in its employee selection process. As a result, which of the following is most likely to occur?", "options": {"A": "An applicant who obtains very high scores on tests of mathematical and mechanical intelligence will not be hired because he fails a test of verbal intelligence by several points.", "B": "An applicant who scores low on a test of verbal intelligence obtains very high scores on tests of mechanical and mathematical intelligence, so he is hired as an airline mechanic.", "C": "An applicant's score on tests of verbal, mathematical, and mechanical intelligence place him in the lowest of \u0000fteen criterion groups, so he is not hired.", "D": "An applicant's score on a test of mechanical intelligence compares favorably to the scores of successful employees, so he is hired as an airline mechanic."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Multiple cutoff is a method for combining predictor scores that involves establishing minimum cut-off scores for each predictor. If an examinee\nfalls below the cut-off for even one predictor, he or she will be rejected. The applicant in this case was not hired because he did not pass all of\nthe tests used for prediction. As noted above, when multiple cutoff is used, all of the predictor tests must be passed.\nAnswer B: The multiple cutoff technique is noncompensatory; that is, a high score on one measure does not compensate for a low score on\nanother measure.\nAnswer C: In this case, scores on two or more predictors are combined to predict membership in three or more criterion groups. This procedure\nis known as multiple discriminant analysis.\nAnswer D: In this case, a score on a test is interpreted in terms of a comparison to the scores of successful employees and is not a description\nof the multiple-cutoff technique."}, {"question": "Vestibule training would be most useful:", "options": {"A": "for teaching basic skills to unskilled workers.", "B": "when on-the-job training would be too dangerous.", "C": "when training costs must be kept to a minimum.", "D": "for teaching complex cognitive skills."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Vestibule training is a type of off-the-job training that makes use of a physical replication or simulation of the actual job environment. A\nvestibule is especially useful when on-the-job training would be too dangerous.\nAnswer A: Teaching basic skills to unskilled workers is incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer C: Setting up a vestibule can be very costly, so the need to minimize costs would not be an indicator for this type of training.\nAnswer D: Teaching complex cognitive skills to unskilled workers is incorrect. See explanation for answer B."}, {"question": "When using the technique known as \"covert sensitization,\" the client:", "options": {"A": "is consistently reinforced for engaging in behaviors other than the target (undesirable) behavior during a prespeci\u0000ed period of time.", "B": "imagines a person other than him/herself engaging in a variety of alternative (desirable) behaviors.", "C": "repeatedly engages in the target (undesirable) behavior while reinforcement is consistently withheld.", "D": "imagines he/she is engaging in the target (undesirable) behavior and then imagines an aversive consequence for doing so."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Covert sensitization is a type of aversion therapy that uses imagery. Covert sensitization uses counterconditioning in imagination (versus in\nvivo) in order to reduce or eliminate an undesirable behavior.\nAnswer A: This procedure describes the technique known as overcorrection.\nAnswer B: This answer describes covert modeling.\nAnswer C: This answer describes operant extinction."}, {"question": "When using in vivo exposure with response prevention:", "options": {"A": "the CS is presented repeatedly without the US.", "B": "the US is presented repeatedly without the CS.", "C": "the CS and US are alternately presented.", "D": "the CS and US are presented simultaneously."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In vivo exposure with response prevention (also known as \u0000ooding) involves exposing an individual to an anxiety-arousing stimulus while\npreventing the person from making his/her usual avoidance response. The assumption underlying the use of in vivo exposure as a treatment\nfor anxiety is that anxiety is a classically-conditioned response in which the anxiety-arousing stimulus (the conditioned stimulus) attained its\nanxiety-arousing properties by being paired in the past with a stimulus that naturally produced anxiety (the unconditioned stimulus). In vivo\nexposure involves extinguishing the classically conditioned anxiety response by repeatedly exposing the person to the conditioned stimulus\n(CS) without the unconditioned stimulus (US).\nAnswer B: Presentation of the US without the CS will produce an unconditioned response (UR). In this example, the unconditioned stimulus is\nsomething that naturally produces anxiety.\nAnswer C: Extinction relies on the repeated presentation of the CS without the US. If the CS and US are alternatively presented, the association\nbetween the CS and the conditioned anxiety response will be maintained.\nAnswer D: When the CS and US are presented simultaneously, the CR (anxiety) will be elicited. This process is conditioning, not extinction."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a type of conditioning that entails presenting and terminating the conditioned stimulus (CS) prior to presenting the unconditioned stimulus (US)?", "options": {"A": "Delay conditioning", "B": "Simultaneous conditioning", "C": "Trace conditioning", "D": "Backward conditioning"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Trace conditioning is a type of forward conditioning in which the CS is presented and terminated before presenting the US. It is not as effective\nas delay conditioning.\nAnswer A: Delay conditioning is a type of forward conditioning that involves presenting the CS so that it precedes and overlaps presentation of\nthe US. It is the most effective method for establishing a conditioned response.\nAnswer B: As its name implies, simultaneous conditioning involves presenting the US and CS simultaneously. It is not as effective as trace and\ndelay conditioning.\nAnswer D: Backward conditioning involves presenting the US before the CS and is rarely effective."}, {"question": "The aspect of memory that is capable of storing a very large amount of information for a very brief period of time is referred to as ________ memory.", "options": {"A": "short-term", "B": "sensory", "C": "primary", "D": "secondary"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "According to the multistore model of memory, memory has three components -- sensory, short-term, and long-term. Sensory memory contains\ninformation collected from all senses but retains that information for a very brief period (probably .5 second).\nAnswer A: Short-term memory has a limited capacity and a limited duration.\nAnswer C: Some authors use the term primary memory as a name for short-term memory.\nAnswer D: The term secondary memory refers to long-term memory which has a very large capacity and a very long duration."}, {"question": "For the treatment of migraine headaches:", "options": {"A": "autogenic training is more effective than thermal biofeedback.", "B": "thermal biofeedback is more effective than autogenic training.", "C": "thermal biofeedback plus autogenic training is more effective than either treatment alone.", "D": "thermal biofeedback plus autogenic training is no more effective than either treatment alone."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the research on the use of biofeedback for the disorders addressed in the Learning Theory chapter\nof the written study materials. Several studies have found that the combination of thermal feedback and autogenic training is the best\napproach for migraine headaches.\nAnswers A, B, and D are incorrect."}, {"question": "Which of the following is best conceptualized as a type of aversive counterconditioning?", "options": {"A": "Implosive therapy", "B": "Overcorrection", "C": "Response cost", "D": "Covert sensitization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Covert sensitization is an intervention based on aversive counterconditioning. Aversive counterconditioning is used to eliminate an undesirable\nbehavior that produces a pleasant or positive response (e.g., sexual arousal) by pairing stimuli associated with that behavior with stimuli that\nproduce an unpleasant response. By doing so, the pleasant response is replaced by the unpleasant response and the behavior is reduced or\neliminated. When using covert sensitization to eliminate an undesirable behavior, the individual imagines an unpleasant (aversive)\nconsequence while imagining that he/she is engaging in the behavior.\nAnswer A: Implosive therapy is used to eliminate anxiety and involves exposing the individual to the anxiety-arousing stimulus in imagination\nfor an extended period of time. Images used during implosive therapy are embellished with psychodynamic themes.\nAnswer B: Overcorrection is usually classi\u0000ed as a type of punishment and involves having the individual engage in certain behaviors as a\npenalty for having performed the target behavior - e.g., \"\u0000xing\" the consequences of the target behavior and/or engaging in alternative desirable\nbehaviors.\nAnswer C: Response cost is a type of negative punishment that involves removing a stimulus (e.g., a privilege) following a behavior whenever it\noccurs in order to reduce the frequency of that behavior."}, {"question": "Implicit memory is involved in:", "options": {"A": "remembering where you were on your last birthday.", "B": "buttoning your shirt when you get dressed in the morning.", "C": "solving a simple arithmetic problem \"in your head.\"", "D": "studying for the licensing exam."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Implicit memory is memory that does not require conscious effort, while explicit memory requires conscious recollection. Of the memories\nlisted in the responses, buttoning a shirt is most likely to involve implicit memory. In fact, most procedural memories are implicit and some\ninvestigators use the terms interchangeably.\nAnswer A: Remembering where you were on your last birthday is an example of episodic memory. Episodic memory consists of information\nabout events that have been personally experienced.\nAnswer C: Solving a math problem in your head is an example of semantic memory. Semantic memory includes general knowledge and stores\nfacts, rules, and concepts.\nAnswer D: Studying for the licensing exam is another example of semantic memory."}, {"question": "Baddeley and Hitch's (1974) multi-component model of working memory includes all of the following components except:", "options": {"A": "a phonological loop.", "B": "a visuospatial sketchpad.", "C": "a central executive.", "D": "a semantic-abstract processor."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "A semantic-abstract processor was not identi\u0000ed by Baddeley and Hitch as a component of working memory. Working memory is the aspect of\nshort-term memory that is involved in the manipulation and storage of information. Baddeley and Hitch's (1974) multi-component model of\nworking memory describes it as consisting of a central executive and three subsystems: the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad, and\nthe episodic buffer.\nAnswer A: The phonological loop is one of the components of working memory identi\u0000ed by Baddeley and Hitch. Its role is to rehearse verbal\ninformation in order to retain it in short-term memory.\nAnswer B: The visuospatial sketchpad is also one of the components of working memory and is involved in the processing and short-term\nstorage of visuospatial information.\nAnswer C: As described by Baddeley and Hitch, the central executive component of working memory controls the transmission of information\nto other parts of the cognitive system and coordinates the activities of the three subsystems."}, {"question": "A researcher conducting a study to investigate the phenomenon known as \"stimulus generalization\" would most likely do which of the following after establishing a conditioned response in her participants?", "options": {"A": "Present new stimuli that are similar in varying degrees to the CS", "B": "Repeatedly present the CS after a neutral stimulus", "C": "Repeatedly present the CS without the US", "D": "Pair presentation of the CS with a second US"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Stimulus generalization occurs when stimuli similar to the conditioned stimulus (CS) elicit a response that is similar to the conditioned\nresponse (CR). The presentation of new stimuli that are similar, in varying degrees, to the CS would be used to determine the extent of stimulus\ngeneralization following classical conditioning.\nAnswer B: This answer describes higher-order conditioning.\nAnswer C: This answer describes extinction.\nAnswer D: The procedure described in this answer would not be useful for investigating stimulus generalization."}, {"question": "The best way to keep two sets of six digits in working memory is to:", "options": {"A": "visualize the digits in a string.", "B": "convert the 12 digits to \"chunks.\"", "C": "connect each digit with a visual image.", "D": "repeat the digits \u0000rst forward and then backward several times."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Working memory is another name for short-term memory. Two processes are useful for maintaining information in short-term memory:\nchunking and rehearsal (especially elaborative rehearsal). The number of digits exceeds the number of units that can be held in short-term\nmemory, as the average capacity for short-term memory is 7 +/- 2 distinct units of information. Therefore, it would be necessary to \"chunk\" the\ndigits into a smaller number of units; e.g., four chunks of three digits each.\nAnswer A: Visualizing the digits in a string would not effectively keep the digits in working memory.\nAnswer C: This might be useful after the digits have been chunked to transfer information from short-term to long-term memory.\nAnswer D: Rote repetition (e.g., repeating a phone number) is useful for maintaining information in working memory. When utilizing repetition\none would repeat the numbers in order (and not reverse them); however, the number of digits in the question (12) exceeds the number of units\nthat can be held in short-term memory, and chunking would be a more effective strategy."}, {"question": "The gradual decline in the intensity, frequency, or duration of a response to the repeated presentation of the same punishment or other stimulus is referred to as:", "options": {"A": "satiation.", "B": "inhibition.", "C": "fading.", "D": "habituation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This question describes a situation in which an individual is no longer responding to a punishment or other stimulus because of repeated\nexposure to it. This is the de\u0000nition of habituation.\nAnswer A: Satiation occurs when a reinforcer has lost its reinforcing value.\nAnswer B: Inhibition, in conditioning, is the active blocking or delay of a response to a stimulus.\nAnswer C: Fading refers to the gradual removal of a prompt. A prompt is provided before the target behavior as a cue to facilitate the\nacquisition of the behavior."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Beck, \"schemas\" are:", "options": {"A": "cognitive structures.", "B": "logical errors.", "C": "innate predispositions.", "D": "automatic interpretations."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "For Beck and others, schemas are cognitive structures that represent knowledge about events, objects, or other phenomena. Schemas\nstructure the way we think about the stimuli we encounter. Beck believes that cognitive schemas are the product of biological and\nenvironmental phenomena and develop early in life.\nAnswer B: Logical errors are important to Beck's theory but are not synonymous with cognitive schemas.\nAnswer C: Cognitive schemas are predispositions and do have a biological component, but this response does not re\u0000ect the structures, rules,\nand core beliefs that are part of cognitive schemas. Therefore, this is not the best response.\nAnswer D: This sounds more like automatic thoughts, which are also part of Beck's theory but are not synonymous with cognitive schemas."}, {"question": "Tolman's research with rats in mazes con\u0000rmed that:", "options": {"A": "noncontingent reinforcement can lead to superstitious behavior.", "B": "reinforcement is not necessary for learning to occur.", "C": "observational learning occurs in non-human animals.", "D": "learning is constrained by biological predispositions."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For the licensing exam, you want to have Tolman associated with latent learning. Tolman's research indicated that learning of a new behavior\n(but not necessarily performance of that behavior) occurs without reinforcement. Speci\u0000cally, his studies of rats in mazes demonstrated that\nrats formed cognitive maps of the maze even without reinforcement.\nAnswer A: The effect of noncontingent (accidental) reinforcement on behavior was identi\u0000ed by Skinner.\nAnswer C: Bandura's (1986) theory of observational learning proposes that most complex human behaviors are learned by observing another\nperson perform those behaviors.\nAnswer D: Biological predispositions were not addressed by Tolman's research."}, {"question": "Secondary reinforcers are:", "options": {"A": "stimuli that increase a behavior through their accidental pairing with that behavior.", "B": "stimuli that gain their reinforcing value through their association with an unconditioned reinforcer.", "C": "high-frequency behaviors that are used to reinforce low-frequency behaviors.", "D": "reinforcers that are removed (rather than applied) following a behavior."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Reinforcers are classi\u0000ed as primary or secondary. Primary (unconditioned) reinforcers are inherently reinforcing, while secondary\n(conditioned) reinforcers acquire their reinforcing value through their association with one or more primary reinforcers. Money is an example of\na secondary reinforcer.\nAnswer A: Superstitious behavior occurs due to stimuli that increase a behavior through their accidental pairing with that behavior.\nAnswer C: When using the Premack Principle, a high-frequency behavior is used as a reinforcer for a low-frequency behavior to increase the\nlow-frequency behavior.\nAnswer D: This answer describes negative reinforcement."}, {"question": "Which of the following proposes that responses that produce a satisfying effect in a particular situation become more likely to occur again?", "options": {"A": "Matching law", "B": "Levels-of-processing model", "C": "Yerkes-Dodson law", "D": "Law of effect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The law of effect states that when behaviors are followed by \"satisfying consequences,\" they are more likely to increase or occur again.\nAnswer A: The matching law suggests that when different schedules of reinforcement are available at the same time for different behaviors,\nindividuals will allocate their behavior according to the relative rates of reinforcement available for each option.\nAnswer B: The levels-of-processing model assumes that memory for information is affected by the depth of processing that information. The\nmodel distinguishes between three levels: structural, phonemic, and semantic. The deepest and most effective level is the semantic\n(\"meaning\") level.\nAnswer C: The Yerkes-Dodson law predicts that moderate levels of arousal are associated with optimal learning and performance so that the\nrelationship between arousal and learning takes the shape of an inverted \"U.\""}, {"question": "Self-monitoring is used in cognitive-behavioral therapies:", "options": {"A": "to facilitate diagnosis.", "B": "as both an assessment tool and an intervention.", "C": "to foster feelings of self-e\u0000cacy.", "D": "to explore the underlying meaning of events."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Self-monitoring is what it sounds like -- it involves monitoring one's own behavior. Client may record information about the frequency and\nconditions surrounding their behaviors or they may monitor their self-statements. Self-monitoring has been found useful not only as an\nassessment technique but also as an intervention because it tends to alter the target behavior in the desired direction.\nAnswer A: Self-monitoring is not used to facilitate diagnosis.\nAnswer C: Self-e\u0000cacy refers to a person's beliefs about his or her ability to perform a behavior or task or to achieve certain goals. Although\nself-monitoring may lead to increased self-e\u0000cacy throughout the course of therapy, the intended purpose of self-monitoring is not to foster\nfeelings of self-e\u0000cacy.\nAnswer D: Cognitive-behavioral therapies do not explore the underlying meaning of events; this sounds more like psychodynamic therapies."}, {"question": "A psychologist investigating short-term memory shows the letter \"T\" to each research subject for a brief period and, after removing the letter, asks the subject what he or she has seen. The psychologist \u0000nds that many subjects report seeing the wrong letter, with the most common error of recall being which of the following letters?", "options": {"A": "L", "B": "D", "C": "S", "D": "F"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "This is a di\u0000cult question that requires you to know that information in short-term memory is believed to be primarily coded acoustically.\nStudies similar to the one described in this question have found that errors in short-term memory usually involve a confusion of letters that\nsound alike, thereby supporting the theory that information is stored acoustically in short-term memory. Of the letters listed in the responses,\nonly \"D\" sounds like \"T.\"\nAnswers A, C, and D: These responses provide the incorrect letter."}, {"question": "For Kohler and other Gestalt psychologists, learning is the result of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "The development of mental models", "B": "The formation of associations between stimuli and responses", "C": "The perception of relationships among elements of the problem", "D": "Observation and imitation of the behavior of others"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Kohler was one of the founders of Gestalt psychology, which is based on the assumption that \"the whole is greater than the sum of its parts.\"\nHe is probably best known for his work on insight. Kohler described insight as a type of relational thinking - i.e., the sudden understanding of\nrelationships among elements of the problem situation.\nAnswer A: A mental model is an internal representation of how something works in the external world. Although a mental model may represent\nwhat a person has learned, the learning process itself is not a result of the mental model.\nAnswer B: The formation of associations between stimuli and responses is best explained by classical and operant conditioning.\nAnswer D: Bandura's (1986) theory of observational learning proposes that most complex human behaviors are learned by observing another\nperson perform those behaviors. While learning can occur without reinforcement, performance (imitation) of the behavior requires motivation."}, {"question": "A psychologist determines that, for a 6-year child, Behavior A is more probable than Behavior B. The psychologist tells the parents to make the child's opportunity to engage in Behavior A contingent on Behavior B. The psychologist is using which of the following techniques?", "options": {"A": "Response cost", "B": "Shaping", "C": "Differential reinforcement of other behaviors", "D": "Premack principle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In this situation, the opportunity to engage in the more frequent behavior is being made contingent on performance of the less frequent\nbehavior in order to increase the less frequent behavior; this describes the Premack Principle. The Premack Principle is a type of positive\nreinforcement in which the reinforcer is a frequently occurring behavior. For example, if you allow yourself to talk on the phone to friends (or\nwatch TV) only after studying for at least one hour, you are using the Premack Principle to reinforce studying.\nAnswer A: Response cost is an application of negative punishment that is used to eliminate an undesirable behavior by imposing a penalty or\n\u0000ne (i.e., removing a speci\u0000c stimulus) whenever the undesirable behavior occurs.\nAnswer B: Shaping involves reinforcing successive approximations to the desired behavior (i.e., any behavior leading to the development of the\ntarget behavior is rewarded).\nAnswer C: Differential reinforcement of other behaviors (DRO) is an operant technique in which a positive reinforcement is delivered\nperiodically and only in cases for which the participant demonstrates a response other than the target response in order to reduce the\nfrequency of the target response."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Aaron Beck, automatic thoughts are:", "options": {"A": "more accessible and less stable than voluntary thoughts.", "B": "spontaneously triggered by a speci\u0000c circumstance and accompanied by an emotional reaction.", "C": "systematic errors in reasoning that are evident during times of psychological distress.", "D": "always irrational."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Automatic thoughts are \"surface level cognitions\" that intercede between an event and a person's emotional or behavioral reactions to that\nevent. Automatic thoughts spontaneously occur when triggered by a speci\u0000c circumstance or situation.\nAnswer A: Automatic thoughts are less accessible and more stable than voluntary thoughts.\nAnswer C: This describes cognitive distortions.\nAnswer D: Automatic thoughts may be rational or irrational in the sense that they may be accurate or inaccurate. Treatment focuses on\nirrational (inaccurate) automatic thoughts."}, {"question": "Dr. Agape uses a dismantling strategy to identify the \"active ingredient\" of systematic desensitization. He is most likely to \u0000nd that which of the following is the critical component?", "options": {"A": "Stimulus control", "B": "Reciprocal inhibition", "C": "Extinction", "D": "Behavior rehearsal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Research using a dismantling strategy involves separating and comparing the various components of a treatment to identify the component(s)\nmost responsible for the treatment's effects. Systematic desensitization was originally developed as an application of reciprocal inhibition.\nHowever, studies using the dismantling strategy have found that its effects are due to repeated exposure to the CS without the US (i.e., to\nclassical extinction).\nAnswer A: Stimulus control training involves reinforcing the target behavior in the presence of one stimulus but not others.\nAnswer B: Reciprocal inhibition is a form of counterconditioning used to replace an undesired response with a desired response.\nAnswer D: Behavior rehearsal is the practicing of behaviors, responses, and social skills in a therapeutic setting in preparation for use outside\nof therapy (i.e., \"the real world\")."}, {"question": "A \u0000rst-grade student frequently misbehaves in class. Whenever he does so, his teacher has him stand in the corner for ten minutes. During that time, he often turns around and makes faces at his classmates, and they respond by laughing and talking to him. The boy's misbehavior is being maintained by which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Habituation", "B": "Satiation", "C": "Positive reinforcement", "D": "Negative reinforcement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "In this situation, the boy's misbehavior is continuing because of the attention he receives from his classmates when he is punished by the\nteacher. Positive reinforcement increases the frequency or the likelihood that a behavior will be maintained and is used by applying an\nappealing stimulus following the behavior. In this situation, the boy's misbehavior is being maintained by the attention from his peers.\nAnswer A: Habituation occurs when a punishment loses its effectiveness. In this case, the punishment was never effective because it was\nactually serving as positive reinforcement.\nAnswer B: Satiation occurs when a reinforcer loses its effectiveness. The teacher was not trying to reinforce the boy's behavior.\nAnswer D: Negative reinforcement occurs when a behavior increases because of a stimulus that is removed following the behavior and does\nnot apply to this question."}, {"question": "According to Piaget, centration, or the tendency to focus on only one aspect of a situation or object while ignoring all other aspects, is characteristic of the __________ stage.", "options": {"A": "preconventional", "B": "conventional", "C": "preoperational", "D": "concrete operational"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "During Piaget's preoperational stage, several limitations (including centration) underlie the child's inability to conserve.\nAnswer A: Preconventional is not one of Piaget's stages of cognitive development.\nAnswer B: Conventional is not one of Piaget's stages of cognitive development.\nAnswer D: Decentration is the ability to consider more than one dimension or aspect at a time. This is characteristic of the concrete operational\nstage."}, {"question": "Prader-Willi syndrome is characterized by an intellectual disability and extreme obesity. It is caused by which of the following chromosomal abnormalities?", "options": {"A": "An extra chromosome", "B": "A missing chromosome", "C": "A chromosomal translocation", "D": "A chromosomal deletion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Approximately 1 in 200 babies is born with a chromosomal abnormality. Deletion occurs when part of a chromosome is missing. Prader-Willi\nsyndrome and cru-du-chat are two disorders caused by a chromosomal deletion.\nAnswer A: Down Syndrome and Klinefelter Syndrome are two disorders attributable to the presence of an extra chromosome.\nAnswer B: Turner Syndrome is an example of a disorder caused by the absence of a chromosome. It occurs only in females and involves the\nabsence of one X chromosome.\nAnswer C: A translocation is the transfer of a chromosome segment to another chromosome. Although most cases of Down syndrome are\ndue to the presence of an extra chromosome 21, some cases also involve a translocation in which the extra chromosome 21 is\nattached to another chromosome."}, {"question": "John M. Gottman (1994) has linked criticism, defensiveness, contempt, and stonewalling to a high risk for:", "options": {"A": "divorce in the \u0000rst seven years following marriage.", "B": "child abuse.", "C": "delinquency in girls in early adolescence.", "D": "acting out behaviors by boys following the divorce of their parents."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Gottman and his colleagues (1994) have conducted longitudinal research investigating the interactions between married couples that predict\ndivorce. They have found that the combination of criticism, defensiveness, contempt, and stonewalling (which they refer to as the \"four\nhorseman of the Apocalypse\") is associated with a high risk for divorce, while the suppression of both positive and negative affect predicts\nlater divorce.\nAnswer B: Child abuse was not the focus of Gottman's research on marriage.\nAnswer C: Delinquency was not the subject of Gottman's research on marriage.\nAnswer D: Although divorce was the subject of Gottman's research, factors in the marital relationship were examined rather than the behavior\nof children after divorce occurred."}, {"question": "The parent of a one-year-old says her daughter shows signs of understanding some of what is said to her. However, the girl is still babbling and the closest she has come to a word is \"dadada.\" You:", "options": {"A": "advise the parent that this is within the range of normal language development.", "B": "tell the parent that most children don't speak their \u0000rst words until 16 or 17 months of age.", "C": "refer the parent and her daughter to a speech pathologist for an evaluation of the child's language ability.", "D": "consider the possibility of a diagnosis of Expressive Language Disorder."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "At 12 months of age, children still babble, although their babbling consists primarily of identical sound sequences (\"dadada\"). While many\nchildren have spoken their \u0000rst words by this age, it is not uncommon for \u0000rst words to occur later. Therefore, the child's language development\nis \"on track.\"\nAnswer B: Most children utter their \u0000rst words prior to 17 or 18 months.\nAnswer C: The child's language skills do not indicate developmental delays, so this is unnecessary.\nAnswer D: The child's language abilities are not indicative of a disorder."}, {"question": "In Piaget's model of cognitive development, which stage is associated with the development of object permanence?", "options": {"A": "Sensorimotor stage", "B": "Preoperational stage", "C": "Concrete operational stage", "D": "Formal operational stage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Object permanence is the ability of a child to recognize that an object continues to exist when the object is no longer visible. This is an\naccomplishment associated with the sensorimotor stage.\nAnswer B: The preoperational stage begins the emergence of semiotic functions, and does not include the development of object permanence.\nAnswer C: Object permanence is not developed in the concrete operational stage, which includes the emergence of mental operations, which\nare logical rules for transforming and manipulating information.\nAnswer D: Object permanence is not \u0000rst seen in the formal operational stage. In this stage, a person is able to think abstractly, relativistically\nand hypothetically."}, {"question": "A mother is most likely to accurately recall which of the following about her child?", "options": {"A": "Birth weight", "B": "Ages when \u0000rst crawled and walked alone", "C": "Height at certain ages", "D": "Age when read \u0000rst word"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Certain facts about one's child seem to be recalled most easily and accurately. Although mothers may remember speci\u0000c information such as\ngrades, height, etc., they most consistently and accurately remember their children's birth weights.\nAnswer B: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "With regard to empathy in childhood and adolescence, the research has generally found that:", "options": {"A": "females exhibit higher levels of empathy than do males but that females and males both bene\u0000t from empathy modeling and training.", "B": "females exhibit higher levels of empathy than do males and that males do not bene\u0000t from empathy modeling and training.", "C": "females and males exhibit similar levels of empathy but that females bene\u0000t more from empathy modeling and training.", "D": "males exhibit higher levels of empathy than do females but that females bene\u0000t more from empathy modeling and training."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The research on gender differences in empathy is not entirely consistent. In general, females of all ages exhibit higher levels of empathy\n(especially affective empathy) than do males. There is evidence that the gender difference in empathy is due to differences in childrearing\npractices. When males and females are exposed to similar empathy modeling and training, the gender gap in empathy decreases.[See, e.g.,\nHaynes, L. A. and Avery, A. W. (1979), Training adolescents in self-disclosure and empathy skills, Journal of Community Psychology, 26(6), 526-\n530. ]\nAnswer B:Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Research investigating the impact of early attachment has most consistently linked the ________ attachment pattern to aggressive behavior problems in childhood.", "options": {"A": "ambivalent", "B": "organized avoidant", "C": "disorganized", "D": "disorganized secure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Early attachment patterns are associated with a number of behaviors, including aggression in childhood, which is linked to the disorganized\nattachment pattern. (e.g., Lyons-Ruth, K. (1996), Attachment relationships among children with aggressive behavior problems: The role of early\ndisorganized attachment patterns. Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology, 64(1), 64-73.)\nAnswer A: The ambivalent attachment pattern is relatively rare, and the research has not linked it to aggressiveness in childhood.\nAnswer B: Although some studies found a link between the avoidant attachment pattern and aggression for boys (but not for girls), other\nstudies have not found a consistent link between the avoidant pattern and aggression for boys or girls. In addition, while a disorganized\navoidant pattern (a pattern that combines behaviors associated with the disorganized and avoidant types) has been linked an increased risk for\naggression, an organized avoidant pattern has not.\nAnswer D: Disorganized secure is not one of the attachment patterns identi\u0000ed by the researchers."}, {"question": "Repeated exposure to alcohol during prenatal development is most likely to negatively impact the ___________, according to research using brain imaging techniques.", "options": {"A": "hypothalamus, thalamus, and parietal lobes", "B": "medulla, thalamus, and temporal lobes", "C": "basal ganglia, hippocampus, and frontal lobes", "D": "brainstem, amygdala, and frontal lobes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Fetal alcohol syndrome (FAS) is characterized by a combination of behavioral and cognitive symptoms including an intellectual disability,\nimpaired attention and memory, hyperactivity, impaired motor coordination, impulsivity, and poor judgment. The areas of the brain listed in this\nanswer mediate some of these functions and are often adversely affected by prenatal exposure to alcohol. Other areas that are commonly\naffected include the corpus callosum, cerebellum, and hypothalamus.\nAnswer A: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer B: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C."}, {"question": "Eric Erikson proposed that an adolescent who doesn't successfully resolve the psychosocial con\u0000ict of identity versus identity confusion due to uncertainty about his/her sexual identity is likely to exhibit which of the following maladaptive responses?", "options": {"A": "Compulsion", "B": "Inertia", "C": "Disdain", "D": "Repudiation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Erikson described the outcomes of each stage of psychosocial development in terms of basic strengths (virtues) and maladaptive responses\n(danger). Repudiation is the maladaptive outcome for inadequate resolution of the identity vs. identity confusion con\u0000ict.\nAnswer A: Compulsion is the maladaptive outcome of inadequate resolution of the autonomy vs. shame/doubt con\u0000ict.\nAnswer B: This is the maladaptive outcome for the industry vs. inferiority con\u0000ict.\nAnswer C: Disdain is the result of inadequate resolution of the integrity vs. despair con\u0000ict."}, {"question": "Research on the effects of divorce on the parent-child relationship has generally shown that, during the initial period following divorce, the relationship between the custodial parent and their child often changes. Speci\u0000cally, during this period, the custodial mother most often:", "options": {"A": "provides harsher but more inconsistent punishment.", "B": "becomes overindulgent and overpermissive.", "C": "spends more time with her child.", "D": "is more concerned about the effectiveness of her parenting skills."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "According to Wallerstein and Blakeslee (1990), the period following divorce is typically characterized by a \"diminished capacity to parent.\"\nMothers, immediately after divorce, are often more punitive but their punishments are commonly inconsistent.\nAnswer B: This is not what has been reported in the literature about custodial mothers.\nAnswer C: Both parents tend to become more self-involved following a divorce and, therefore, typically spend less time with their children.\nAnswer D:This is not what has been found in follow-up studies of divorced families."}, {"question": "Diana Baumrind (1971) and her colleagues describe parenting styles in terms of two dimensions - demandingness and responsiveness. For example, the authoritative style is characterized by:", "options": {"A": "high demandingness, high responsiveness.", "B": "high demandingness, low responsiveness.", "C": "low demandingness, high responsiveness.", "D": "low demandingness, low responsiveness."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Researchers who classify parenting styles in terms demandingness and responsiveness distinguish between four styles, which represent\ndifferent combinations of levels of these characteristics. A combination of high demandingness and high responsiveness is characteristic of\nauthoritative parents. Note, however, that the demands of authoritative parents are reasonable and involve setting and enforcing appropriate\nlimits.\nAnswer B: A combination of high demandingness and low responsiveness is characteristic of authoritarian parents.\nAnswer C: A combination of low demandingness and high responsiveness is characteristic of permissive parents.\nAnswer D: A combination of low demandingness and low responsiveness is characteristic of uninvolved parents."}, {"question": "Konrad Lorenz's (1957) research on \"imprinting\" in ducklings and goslings had the greatest impact on the study of ___________ in human infants.", "options": {"A": "fear and aggression", "B": "gender differences", "C": "sociability", "D": "attachment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Imprinting refers to the social bonding that occurs during a critical period following birth in some non-human species. This \u0000nding led to a\nsearch for a similar \"critical period\" for attachment in humans.\nAnswer A: Ideas about fear and aggression have not been in\u0000uenced by Lorenz's research.\nAnswer B: The study of gender differences has not been in\u0000uenced by Lorenz's research.\nAnswer C: While ideas about sociability may seem loosely related to the imprinting behavior of birds, the bonding of the birds at a \"critical\nperiod\" after birth is most similar to the attachment of the human infant to a caregiver, and this comparison has therefore been made in\ndevelopmental research."}, {"question": "A(n) __________ would be most likely to agree that the basic structure of memory, attention, and other aspects of cognition are similar throughout the lifespan and that increasing complexity of these abilities during childhood are due to increases in capacity and e\u0000ciency.", "options": {"A": "Piagetian", "B": "Vygotskian", "C": "information processing theorist", "D": "ecological systems theorist"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Information-processing theorists state that the cognitive changes that occur during childhood are due to increased capacity. For example,\nexecutive function develops due to increases in maximum complexity of rules children can create (Zelazo, P. et al (2003). The Development of\nExecutive Function in Early Childhood. Monographs of the Society for Research in Child Development, 68(3), I-151.) or increases in abstract\nreasoning during adolescence are due to increases in the underlying capacities to attend to information and to hold information in memory\n(e.g., Case, 1985; Fischer, 1980).\nAnswer A: Piaget viewed cognitive development as the result of qualitative changes that occur as the consequence of biological maturation\nand interactions with the environment.\nAnswer B: Vygotsky focused on the impact of sociocultural factors in cognitive development.\nAnswer D: Ecological systems theorists (e.g., Bronfenbrenner) emphasize the impact of external factors on development."}, {"question": "Vygotsky proposed that which of the following creates a \"zone of proximal development\" for young children?", "options": {"A": "Unconditional positive regard", "B": "Make-believe play", "C": "Attachment", "D": "Extended family"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The zone of proximal development is a key concept in Vygotsky's theory of cognitive development. It is de\u0000ned as a type of support that allows\nthe child to accomplish activities with assistance that he/she will eventually be able to perform independently. Although Vygotsky focused\nprimarily on adults and more competent peers as providers of support (\"scaffolding\"), he also viewed make-believe (symbolic) play as a\nsupport system that allows children to act and think in more cognitively complex ways.\nAnswer A: Unconditional positive regard is not part of Vygotsky's zone of proximal development.\nAnswer C: Attachment is not a concept related to Vygotsky's zone of proximal development.\nAnswer D: Extended family is not a speci\u0000c part of Vygotsky's zone of proximal development."}, {"question": "A 12-month old baby who has been classi\u0000ed as \"insecure/ambivalent\" is reunited with his mother in Ainsworth's \"Strange Situation.\" Most likely, this baby will:", "options": {"A": "hit or push his mother when she approaches and continue to cry after she picks him up.", "B": "run away from his mother when she approaches but then cling to her when she picks him up.", "C": "greet his mother but try to avoid her when she attempts to pick him up.", "D": "ignore his mother initially but welcome her attempts to pick him up."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Research using the Strange Situation has identi\u0000ed four distinct attachment patterns - secure, insecure/avoidant, insecure/ambivalent, and\ndisorganized-disoriented. The described behaviors are characteristic of children with a insecure/ambivalent pattern.\nAnswer B: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "Harry Harlow's research con\u0000rmed the importance of which of the following in the development of attachment in infant monkeys?", "options": {"A": "Oral grati\u0000cation", "B": "Tactile comfort", "C": "Interactional synchrony", "D": "Pheromones"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Harlow and his colleagues found that infant monkeys preferred physical contact with a soft terrycloth surrogate mother to contact with a hard\nwire surrogate that provided food. They concluded that these results indicate that contact (tactile) comfort is most critical for attachment.\nAnswer A: Incorrect. The experiment showed that tactile comfort was more important than oral grati\u0000cation and nutrition.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. Interactional synchrony was not the subject of Harlow's experiments.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. Pheromones were not the subject of Harlow's experiments."}, {"question": "In general, peer pressure reaches its peak in intensity during:", "options": {"A": "preadolescence (ages 10 to 13).", "B": "early adolescence (ages 13 to 15).", "C": "middle adolescence (ages 15 to 17).", "D": "late adolescence (ages 17 to 19)."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Peer pressure increases during preadolescence, peaks during early adolescence (ages 13 to 15), and then gradually declines.\nAnswer A: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B."}, {"question": "Research on parenting styles suggests that parents who are _____________ are most likely to have adolescents who are high in academic achievement.", "options": {"A": "high in both demandingness and warmth", "B": "high in demandingness but low in warmth", "C": "low in both demandingness and warmth", "D": "low in demandingness but high in warmth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Two dimensions are often used to categorize parenting styles - demandingness/control and warmth/support. Baumrind and others have\ncon\u0000rmed that an authoritative parenting style (i.e., a style characterized by a high degree of warmth and demandingness) is most predictive of\nacademic success in adolescence.\nAnswer B: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "The most effective efforts to prevent teen pregnancy have been:", "options": {"A": "abstinence-only programs.", "B": "multi-component programs that include community and clinical interventions.", "C": "school-based educational programs.", "D": "contraception distribution programs."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Although teen pregnancy rates have declined in the United States in recent years, the U.S. continues to have the highest teen pregnancy and\nbirth rates among comparable industrialized nations. Recent efforts through the Teenage Pregnancy Prevention Program started in 2010 have\ndemonstrated success when community-wide initiatives are combined with improved access to clinical interventions. (e.g. Mueller, T. et al.\n(2017). Teen pregnancy prevention: Implementation of a multi-component, community-wide approach. J. Adolescent Health, 60(3S), S9-S17\nand Oringanje, C. (2016). Interventions for preventing unintended pregnancies among adolescents. Cochrane Database of Systematic Review,\nIssue 2. Art. No. CD00525).\nAnswer A: Abstinence only programs do not have a substantial impact on pregnancy rates. (e.g. Chin, H.B., (2012). The effectiveness of group-\nbased comprehensive risk-reduction and abstinence education interventions to prevent or reduce the risk of adolescent pregnancy, HIV, and\nsexually transmitted infections: two systematic reviews for the Guide to Community Preventive Services. Amer. J. of Preventive Medicine,\n42(3), 272-294).\nAnswer C: Educational programs alone are not effective. See Oringanje (2016).\nAnswer D: Contraception access alone is not effective. See Oringanje (2016)."}, {"question": "A mother of a newborn is most likely to say that, based on the nature of her baby's cry, she can tell if her baby is:", "options": {"A": "hungry, tired, or uncomfortable.", "B": "hungry, thirsty, or tired.", "C": "hungry, angry, or in pain.", "D": "hungry, lonely, or irritable."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Researchers have distinguished between three types of cries in infants. Mothers and other caretakers have con\u0000rmed that each cry is\nassociated with a different meaning. Even very young infants seem to be able to communicate that they are either hungry, angry or in pain by\nthe nature of their cries.\nAnswer A: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer B: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C."}, {"question": "A woman is told that she has a serious illness for which there is no cure and that it is likely that she will die within six months. From the perspective of Kubler-Ross' stages of death and dying, you would expect the woman's \u0000rst reaction to \u0000nding out about her prognosis to be:", "options": {"A": "Why me?", "B": "The test results must be inaccurate.", "C": "I must be being punished for something I did.", "D": "I hate my life anyway, so what difference does it make?"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "This statement best re\u0000ects denial, the usual initial response to learning of one's impending death. Kubler-Ross' stages, in order, are: denial,\nanger, bargaining, depression, and acceptance.\nAnswer A: This is more characteristic of the second stage (anger).\nAnswer C: This statement best re\u0000ects the fourth stage (depression).\nAnswer D: This is also more characteristic of the fourth stage."}, {"question": "Studies comparing bilingual and monolingual children on measures of inhibitory control and cognitive \u0000exibility have generally found that:", "options": {"A": "monolingual children outperform bilingual children on both measures.", "B": "bilingual children outperform monolingual children on both measures.", "C": "monolingual children outperform bilingual children on a measure of inhibitory control but bilingual children outperform monolingual children on a measure of cognitive \u0000exibility.", "D": "bilingual children outperform monolingual children on a measure of inhibitory control but monolingual children outperform bilingual children on a measure of cognitive \u0000exibility."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Several studies have reported that bilingual children do better than monolingual children on measures of inhibitory control and cognitive\n\u0000exibility. [See, e.g., Akhtar, N. and Menjivar, J.A. (2012). Cognitive and linguistic correlates of early exposure to more than one language. in J.\nB. Benson (Ed.). Advances in child development and behavior (pp. 41-78). London: Academic Press.]\nAnswer A: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B."}, {"question": "Phallic is to initiative vs. guilt as latency is to:", "options": {"A": "autonomy vs. shame and doubt.", "B": "identity vs. role confusion.", "C": "industry vs. inferiority.", "D": "generativity vs. stagnation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The terms presented in this question represent stages in Freud's and Erikson's theories of development. Freud's phallic and Erikson's initiative\nvs. guilt stages occur at about ages 3 to 6, while Freud's latency and Erikson's industry vs. inferiority stages occur at about ages 6 to 12.\nAnswer A: Erikson's autonomy vs. shame and doubt stage aligns with Freud's anal stage.\nAnswer B: Erikson's identity vs. role confusion stage aligns with Freud's genital stage.\nAnswer D: Erikson's generativity vs. stagnation occurs in middle-aged adults. According to Freud, the genital stage lasts throughout adulthood."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT characteristic of Piaget's preoperational stage of development?", "options": {"A": "Magical thinking", "B": "Insight learning", "C": "Finalism", "D": "Decentration"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The preoperational stage of development spans the period from ages two through seven. In this stage, children exhibit centration, or an\ninability to mentally hold two dimensions at the same time. Decentration occurs in the concrete operational stage and contributes to the ability\nto conserve.\nAnswer A: Magical thinking, the belief that one has magical powers, is a result of egocentrism and precausal reasoning.\nAnswer B: Piaget believed that a major achievement of the preoperational stage is insight learning (learning based on intuition).\nAnswer C: Finalism is the belief that every event has a purpose and is characteristic of the preoperational stage."}, {"question": "Developmental research has most consistently linked prolonged emotional stress in an expectant mother to which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Sudden infant death syndrome", "B": "Suppressed immune system in the newborn", "C": "Increased crying and irritability in the newborn", "D": "Decreased activity levels in the newborn"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Of the responses given, increased crying and irritability in the newborn have been most consistently linked to severe or prolonged stress in the\nexpectant mother.\nAnswer A: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer B: Incorrect. See explanation for answer C.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. Prolonged stress in the expectant mother is associated with increased activity levels in the newborn."}, {"question": "At __________ months of age, infants begin to search for a hidden object but reach for the object in the last place they found it even when they have seen the object moved to another location.", "options": {"A": "4 to 8", "B": "8 to 12", "C": "12 to 18", "D": "18 to 24"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "At about eight months of age, children \u0000rst provide evidence that they know that objects exist when they are out of view. However, they commit\nthe A-not-B error, which continues to about 12 months of age.\nAnswer A: During this stage, infants reach for a partially hidden object but stop if the object disappears from view.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. See explanation for answer B."}, {"question": "The expression of some of our traits is restricted by our genetic make-up. In other words, for these traits, genotype restricts phenotype to a small number of outcomes. This is referred to as:", "options": {"A": "equilibration.", "B": "canalization.", "C": "niche-picking.", "D": "epigenesis."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Canalization occurs when phenotype is restricted by genotype to a small number of developmental outcomes.\nAnswer A: Equilibration is the term Piaget used to describe the drive toward cognitive equilibrium, which he considered to be the motivating\nforce for cognitive development.\nAnswer C: Niche-picking is an alternative term for active genotype-environment correlation and refers to the tendency of individuals to seek\nexperiences that are consistent with their genetic predispositions.\nAnswer D: Epigenesis refers to the bidirectional and continuous in\u0000uences of heredity and environment on developmental outcomes."}, {"question": "_________ refers to the \u0000ve spinal segments that make up the lower part of the lower back.", "options": {"A": "lumbar", "B": "sacral", "C": "thoracic", "D": "cervical"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Sacral (pelvic) refers to the \u0000ve spinal segments that make up the lower part of the lower back.\nAnswer A: Lumbar (lower back) refers to the \u0000ve spinal segments that make up the upper part of the lower back.\nAnswer C: Thoracic (trunk) refers to the 12 spinal segments below the cervical (neck) portion of the spinal cord, corresponding to the chest.\nAnswer D: Cervical (neck) refers to the topmost eight segments of the spinal cord in the neck region."}, {"question": "Which of the following describes the ability of the nervous system to change in response to experience or the environment?", "options": {"A": "neural plasticity", "B": "reductionism", "C": "neuropraxia", "D": "neuroscience"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The ability of the brain to be changed by the environment and by experience is called neural plasticity.\nAnswer B: Reductionism is the scienti\u0000c strategy of breaking a system down into increasingly smaller parts to understand it.\nAnswer C: Neuropraxia is a type of peripheral nerve injury that results in symptoms like burning, stinging, and pain. By de\u0000nition, neuropraxia\ndoes not involve damage to the axon.\nAnswer D: Neuroscience is the study of the nervous system."}, {"question": "The neurotransmitter that is most directly implicated with voluntary muscle movement is:", "options": {"A": "norepinephrine.", "B": "acetylcholine.", "C": "serotonin.", "D": "glutamate."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The neurotransmitter most directly implicated in voluntary muscle movement is acetylcholine, as it mediates neuromuscular transmission.\nAcetylcholine has also been implicated in REM sleep, the sleep-wake cycle, learning, and memory.\nAnswer A: Norepinephrine is involved in a number of functions including mood, attention, dreaming, learning, and certain autonomic functions.\nAnswer C: Serotonin has been implicated in mood, hunger, temperature regulation, sexual activity, arousal, sleep, aggression, and migraine\nheadache.\nAnswer D: Glutamate plays a role in learning and memory, especially the formation of long-term memories."}, {"question": "Which of the following drugs is most effective in treating enuresis?", "options": {"A": "Naltrexone", "B": "Methylphenidate", "C": "Imipramine", "D": "Propranolol"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Imipramine is a tricyclic antidepressant that has been found useful for treating enuresis, and although the mechanism of action is not\ncompletely understood, Imipramine may relax the bladder and lighten sleep.\nAnswer A: Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist used to treat alcohol use disorders, as Naltrexone blocks the reinforcing effects of alcohol.\nAnswer B: Methylphenidate is a psychostimulant used to treat ADHD in children in adults.\nAnswer D: Propranolol is a beta-blocker used to treat cardiovascular disease, tremor, migraine headache, and the physical symptoms of\nanxiety."}, {"question": "As a consequence of head trauma caused by a serious car accident, a middle-aged woman experiences a loss of sensation for touch, temperature, and pain in her left hand. Most likely the damage responsible for this de\u0000cit is in her right _____________lobe.", "options": {"A": "frontal", "B": "temporal", "C": "occipital", "D": "parietal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Cutaneous sensation, including pressure, warmth, cold, and pain, is mediated by the somatosensory cortex, which is located in the parietal\nlobe.\nAnswer A: The frontal lobe contains the primary motor cortex, supplementary motor area, premotor cortex, Broca's area, and prefrontal cortex.\nBroadly, these areas are involved in the planning and execution of movement, complex behaviors (such as emotion, attention, and self-\nawareness), and executive functioning.\nAnswer B: The temporal lobes contain the auditory cortex, Wernicke's area (important for the comprehension of language), and are involved in\nthe encoding, retrieval, and storage of long-term memories.\nAnswer C: The occipital lobes contain the visual cortex, which is responsible for visual perception, recognition, and memory."}, {"question": "As a result of head trauma, a young man experiences damage to the primary motor cortex in the left cerebral hemisphere. This is most likely to have which of the following effects?", "options": {"A": "Loss of voluntary movement in his right hand and \u0000ngers", "B": "Loss of voluntary movement in his left hand and \u0000ngers", "C": "Loss of involuntary movement on the right side of his body", "D": "Loss of involuntary movement on the left side of his body"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "For most functions, the right side of the body is controlled by the left hemisphere and the left side of the body is controlled by the right\nhemisphere. The motor cortex is involved in the regulation of voluntary movements. Damage to this area is most likely to affect voluntary\nmovements in distal parts (such as hands and \u0000ngers) of the opposite side of the body.\nAnswer B: Damage to the motor cortex in the right cerebral hemisphere would result in the loss of voluntary movements in the man's left hand\nand \u0000ngers.\nAnswer C: Damage to the basal ganglia and cortical myoclonus would impact involuntary movement.\nAnswer D: Damage to the basal ganglia and cortical myoclonus would impact involuntary, not voluntary, movement."}, {"question": "Which cortex uses working memory to guide thoughts and behavior, regulate attention, monitor the effects of our actions, and organize plans for future actions?", "options": {"A": "orbiotfrontal cortex", "B": "entorhinal cortex", "C": "cingulate cortex", "D": "prefrontal cortex"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The prefrontal cortex helps individuals set and achieve goals. It receives input from multiple regions of the brain to process information and\nadapts accordingly.\nAnswer A: The orbitofrontal cortex is involved in the conscious perception of odors.\nAnswer B: The entorhinal cortex is located in the medial temporal lobe and its functions are implicated in memory, navigation, and perception\nof time.\nAnswer C: The cingulate cortex surrounds the corpus callosum and is involved in attention, emotion, and the perception and experience of pain."}, {"question": "For children with ADHD, the most common side effects of methylphenidate is:", "options": {"A": "dizziness.", "B": "appetite loss.", "C": "pruritus.", "D": "headache."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Methylphenidate is associated with a number of adverse side effects, and the symptoms can often be alleviated by reducing the dosage of the\ndrug. In children, loss of appetite, abdominal pain, insomnia, and tachycardia are the most common side effects of methylphenidate.\nAnswer A: Dizziness is a less common side effect of methylphenidate.\nAnswer C: Pruritus is the term for itchy skin and makes individuals want to scratch. It is not a common side effect of methylphenidate.\nAnswer D: Headache is not a common side effect of methylphenidate."}, {"question": "For some patients with type 2 diabetes, acute hyperglycemia can produce:", "options": {"A": "substantial mood changes only.", "B": "substantial cognitive changes only.", "C": "substantial mood and cognitive changes.", "D": "neither substantial mood nor substantial cognitive changes."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Diabetes is associated with rapid changes in blood glucose levels. There is evidence that these \u0000uctuations are associated with both mood\nand cognitive changes. Hyperglycemia refers to elevated blood glucose levels. There is evidence that, for some patients with type 2 diabetes,\nacute hyperglycemia produces substantial impairments in speed of information processing, working memory, aspects of attention, decreased\nhappiness and alertness, and increased agitation.\nAnswers A, B, and D: Incorrect"}, {"question": "In adults, the early motor symptoms of Huntington's disease most often involve:", "options": {"A": "tingling, numbness, and weakness in one limb.", "B": "repetitive movements in the extremities and face.", "C": "slowness and poverty of movement.", "D": "di\u0000culty swallowing and speaking."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The early signs of Huntington's disease vary from person to person. For some, the initial symptoms are motor. For others, initial symptoms are\naffective or cognitive. For many adults, early motor symptoms include rapid, repetitive, involuntary movements in the extremities and face - e.g.,\n\"piano-playing\" movements of the \u0000ngers and facial grimaces.\nAnswer A: These symptoms are characteristic of multiple sclerosis.\nAnswer C: Slowness and poverty of movement are characteristic of Parkinson's disease.\nAnswer D: Di\u0000culty swallowing and speaking are characteristic of the later stages of Huntington's disease."}, {"question": "What part of the brain regulates the sleep-wake cycle?", "options": {"A": "cerebellum", "B": "thalamus", "C": "reticular formation", "D": "amygdala"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The reticular formation is a network of nerve pathways that extends from the spinal cord into the hypothalamus in the forebrain. It is involved in\nvarious functions including respiration, coughing, vomiting, posture, locomotion, and regulating the sleep-wake cycle.\nAnswer A: The cerebellum is important for balance, posture, and the performance of coordinated and re\u0000ned motor movements. The\ncerebellum has also been implicated in sensorimotor learning.\nAnswer B: The thalamus is involved in motor activity, language, and memory. It acts as a \"relay station\" for incoming sensory information,\nexcept olfaction.\nAnswer D: The amygdala is a substructure of the limbic system and is involved in the control of emotional activities."}, {"question": "A student stares at an object for an extended period of time and then looks at a neutral background, where he sees an image of the object in its complementary color. This phenomenon supports which of the following theories?", "options": {"A": "trichromatic theory", "B": "opponent-process theory", "C": "color blindness", "D": "retinal disparity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The opponent-process theory is supported by the phenomenon of negatives afterimages, which involves seeing the image of an object in its\ncomplementary color after staring at it and then looking at a neutral background. Hering's opponent-process theory postulates three types of\nbipolar cells in the retina: red-green, yellow-blue, and white-black.\nAnswer A: The Young-Helmholtz trichromatic theory posits there are three types of color receptors (cones) that are each receptive to a different\ncolor - red, blue, or green.\nAnswer C: Color blindness affects one or more of the three types of cones, and there are several types. The most common type is an inability to\ndistinguish between red and green.\nAnswer D: Retinal disparity refers to the fact that our two eyes see objects in the world from two different views."}, {"question": "Research by Baker and Green (2005) found that, when compared to older adults with chronic pain, younger adults with chronic pain tend to report:", "options": {"A": "more pain intensity but fewer symptoms of depression.", "B": "less pain intensity and fewer symptoms of depression.", "C": "more pain intensity and more symptoms of depression.", "D": "less pain intensity but more symptoms of depression."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Baker and Green (2005) looked at the effects of age and race on chronic pain. They found that among White and Black adults with chronic pain,\nadults under the age of 50 reported more depressive symptoms, greater pain intensity, and more problems coping with pain than did those age\n50 and over. [See Baker, T. A. and Green, C. R. (2005). Inter-race differences among black and white Americans presenting for chronic pain\nmanagement: The in\u0000uence of age, physical health, and psychosocial factors, Pain Medicine, 6, 29-38.]\nAnswers A, B, and D: Incorrect."}, {"question": "The diagnosis of a spinal cord injury generally relies on which of the following?", "options": {"A": "X-ray and MRI or CT", "B": "X-ray and CPAP", "C": "actigraphy and MRI or CT", "D": "actigraphy and PET or SPECT"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The procedure used to diagnose a spinal injury varies somewhat but ordinarily includes an X-ray and either an MRI or CT. An X-ray is often the\ninitial assessment and is used to identify fractures or other injuries to the spine. An MRI or CT is then used to obtain information about injury to\nthe soft tissues of the spine.\nAnswer B: CPAP (continuous positive airway pressure) is a treatment for sleep apnea.\nAnswer C: Actigraphy is used to monitor body movement and is sometimes used in the assessment of sleep disorders.\nAnswer D: Positron emission tomography (PET) and SPECT (single-photon emission computed tomography) may be used to gather\ninformation on brain activity and to examine how blood \u0000ows to tissues and organs. They are typically used to assess cerebrovascular disease,\nAlzheimer's disease, and other forms of dementia."}, {"question": "The most commonly-prescribed drugs for Tourette's syndrome are drugs that:", "options": {"A": "increase dopamine levels.", "B": "block dopamine transmission.", "C": "increase acetylcholine levels.", "D": "block acetylcholine transmission."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Haloperidol is an antipsychotic drug that exerts its effects by blocking the transmission of dopamine. It has been found effective for alleviation\nthe symptoms of Tourette's syndrome in about 70% of patients.\nAnswer A: Drugs that increase dopamine levels are not typically prescribed for Tourette's syndrome. Drugs that increase dopamine levels may\nused to treat Parkinson's disease and neuroleptic malignant syndrome.\nAnswer C: Drugs that increase acetylcholine are typically used in the treatment of Alzheimer's disease.\nAnswer D: Drugs that block transmission of acetylcholine are used in the treatment of conditions such as urinary incontinence, overactive\nbladder, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disorder."}, {"question": "The most consistent evidence for neurogenesis in human adults has been provided for which of the following areas of the brain?", "options": {"A": "Pineal gland", "B": "Hippocampus", "C": "Medulla oblangata", "D": "Pituitary gland"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Neurogenesis is the development of new neurons and until relatively recently, it was believed that humans do not experience neurogenesis\nfollowing the postnatal period. However, there is now consistent evidence that neurogenesis continues throughout the lifespan in the\nhippocampus and olfactory bulb. [See Eriksson et al. (1998), Neurogenesis in the adult human hippocampus, Nature Medicine, 4, 1313-1317.]\nWhile there have been some reports of postnatal neurogenesis in the caudate nucleus, amygdala, neocortex, and substantia nigra, these\n\u0000ndings have not been consistently replicated.\nAnswer A: The pineal gland is responsible for the production and secretion of melatonin.\nAnswer C: The medulla oblongata transmits signals between the spinal cord and the brain, and it regulates heartbeat and respiration.\nAnswer D: The pituitary gland produces gonadotropic hormones."}, {"question": "________ is a rare syndrome that presents with hyperthermia, muscular rigidity, severe autonomic dysfunction, and in some cases death.", "options": {"A": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "B": "Agranulocytosis", "C": "Tardive dyskinesia", "D": "Akathisia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare reaction to antipsychotic drugs that treat schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. It affects the nervous\nsystem and causes symptoms, including hyperthermia (high fever) and muscle stiffness.\nAnswer B: Agranulocytosis is a rare, drug-induced blood disorder that is characterized by a severe reduction in the number of white blood cells\n(granulocytes) in the circulating blood.\nAnswer C: Tardive Dyskinesia (TD) is a serious and often irreversible effect of antipsychotic medication. Symptoms include involuntary sucking\nand smacking movements of the mouth and lips and can include chorea in the trunk and extremities.\nAnswer D: Akathisia is an uncontrolled sense of inner restlessness."}, {"question": "Gerstmann's syndrome is most likely due to damage to which area of the brain?", "options": {"A": "frontal lobe", "B": "left parietal lobe", "C": "right occipital lobe", "D": "left temporal lobe"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Damage to the left parietal lobe can result in Gerstmann's syndrome which includes right-left confusion, di\u0000cultly with writing (agraphia), and\ndi\u0000culty with mathematics (acalculia). It can also produce \u0000nger agnosia and left-right disorientation.\nAnswer A: Damage to the neurons or tissue of the frontal lobe can lead to personality changes, di\u0000culty concentrating or planning, and\nimpulsivity.\nAnswer C: Damage to one side of the occipital lobe causes homonymous loss of vision with exactly the same \"\u0000eld cut\" in both eyes. Disorders\nof the occipital lobe can cause visual hallucinations and illusions.\nAnswer D: Left temporal lobe lesions result in impaired memory for verbal material, aphasia, and auditory agnosia."}, {"question": "Weber's law explains why:", "options": {"A": "you perceive objects that are close together as a single stimulus or unity.", "B": "you can be heard when you whisper in a very quiet room but must shout to be heard in a very loud, noisy room.", "C": "you perform better in the presence of others on simple tasks but worse in the presence of others on di\u0000cult or complex tasks.", "D": "you feel less pain after banging your elbow when you vigorously rub your elbow or apply heat to it."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Weber's law is one of the psychophysical laws that explains the relationship between physical stimuli and their psychological effects.\nAccording the Weber's law, the \"just-noticeable difference\" in a stimulus is proportion to the magnitude of the original stimulus. It explains why\na whisper is audible in a quiet room but not in a very noisy room.\nAnswer A: This is the prediction made by the Gestaltian \"law of proximity.\"\nAnswer C: This answer describes social facilitation (performing better in the presence of others on simple tasks) and social inhibition\n(performing worse in the presence of others on complex tasks).\nAnswer D: This answer illustrates the prediction made by the gate-control theory of pain."}, {"question": "What type of aphasia is characterized by di\u0000culties with producing written and spoken language, with minimal impairment in understanding language?", "options": {"A": "Receptive aphasia", "B": "Conduction aphasia", "C": "Expressive aphasia", "D": "Global aphasia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Expressive aphasia is also known as Broca's aphasia and is characterized by di\u0000culties with producing written and spoken language, although\nindividuals with expressive aphasia have minimal impairment in the comprehension of language.\nAnswer A: Receptive aphasia is also known as Wernicke's aphasia and people with this form of aphasia have di\u0000culty understanding written\nand spoken language and generating meaningful language.\nAnswer B: Conduction aphasia is caused by damage to the arcuate fasciculus, which connects Wernicke's area and Broca's area. This disorder\ndoes not affect comprehension of language but results in anomia (the inability to name a common or familiar object), paraphasia (the\nsubstitution of words related in sound or meaning to the intended words) and impaired repetition.\nAnswer D: Global aphasia is caused by widespread brain injury and is characterized by extensive disruption in the ability to produce and\nunderstand language."}, {"question": "A client taking tranylcypromine, an MAO-inhibitor, should avoid eating all of the following foods except ________.", "options": {"A": "salami", "B": "soy sauce", "C": "cottage cheese", "D": "home-brewed beer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Individuals taking MAOIs must avoid foods high in tyramine. Aged, fermented, pickled, and air-dried foods should be avoided. Tyramine levels\nincrease in foods when they are held at room temperatures. Although aged cheeses should be avoided, cottage cheese and cream cheese are\nacceptable.\nAnswer A: Salami is an air-dried meat that is considered to have high levels of tyramine.\nAnswer B: Asian \u0000sh sauce, soy sauce, and concentrated yeast extract are also not acceptable due to the high content of tyramine.\nAnswer D: Beer on tap and unpasteurized or home-brewed beers (including Vermouth and Korean beers) are known to be high in tyramine."}, {"question": "Anosognosia is best described as an impairment in __________.", "options": {"A": "coordination and balance.", "B": "speech.", "C": "self-awareness.", "D": "visual perception."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Anosognosia is de\u0000ned as a de\u0000cit in self-awareness and, more speci\u0000cally, awareness of one's own symptoms. Anosognosia is most often\ncaused by brain trauma that affects the parietal lobe.\nAnswer A: Ataxia describes a lack of muscle control or coordination of voluntary movements, and may cause di\u0000culty with walking and\nbalance, hand coordination, speech, and swallowing.\nAnswer B: Aphasia is a condition that affects the ability to understand and produce speech and typically occurs after a stroke or head injury.\nAnswer D: Prosopagnosia is a type of cognitive disorder of visual perception known as face blindness."}, {"question": "Di\u0000culties in concentration, forgetfulness, sensitivity to cold, unexplained weight gain, and constipation are symptoms of ____________.", "options": {"A": "pernicious anemia", "B": "hyperadrenalism", "C": "hypoglycemia", "D": "hypothyroidism"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Several physical conditions can mimic mental disorders. Symptoms associated with hypothyroidism can range from mild to severe. Early\nsymptoms can include weight gain and fatigue. As the thyroid slows, symptoms become more pronounced and include depression,\nconstipation, muscle weakness, and slowed heart rate.\nAnswer A: Pernicious anemia is a type of B12 anemia, and symptoms may include depression, feelings of guilt and worthlessness, anorexia\nand weight loss, and weakness.\nAnswer B: Hyperadrenalism occurs when levels of adrenal hormones in the blood are elevated. Symptoms include depression, anxiety, and\nsomatic delusions.\nAnswer C: Hypoglycemia is a condition where an individual's blood sugar level is lower than normal. Symptoms include weakness, headache,\nhunger, and anxiety."}, {"question": "The primary motor cortex in the left hemisphere controls movement on the right side of the body. The term for affecting the opposite side of the body is called __________.", "options": {"A": "rostral", "B": "caudal", "C": "contralateral", "D": "ipsilateral"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Contralateral means located on opposite sides of the body. Most functions of the body are controlled by the opposite side of the brain; that is,\nvoluntary motor movements on the right side of the body are controlled by motor centers in the left side of the brain.\nAnswer A: Rostral means \"towards the nose\" and therefore refers to the anterior (front) aspect of the head.\nAnswer B: The term caudal means \"toward the tail\" and refers to the posterior (back) of the head.\nAnswer D: Ipsilateral refers to structures on the same side of the body or brain."}, {"question": "The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) is responsible for controlling circadian rhythms. The SCN is located in which area of the brain?", "options": {"A": "thalamus", "B": "hypothalamus", "C": "cerebral cortex", "D": "caudate nucleus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The SCN is a bilateral structure located in the anterior part of the hypothalamus. It is the central pacemaker of the circadian timing system and\nregulates most circadian rhythms in the body.\nAnswer A: The thalamus is composed of different nuclei that each serve a unique role, ranging from relaying sensory and motor signals as well\nas the regulation of consciousness and alertness.\nAnswer C: The cerebral cortex is the outer layer of the brain that has a wrinkled appearance. It is divided into \u0000elds with speci\u0000c functions,\nincluding sight, hearing, smell, and sensation, and controls higher functions such as speech, thinking, and memory.\nAnswer D: The caudate nucleus functions in planning the execution of movement, but also in learning, memory, motivation, and emotion."}, {"question": "The results of the Seattle Longitudinal Study found that:", "options": {"A": "measures of crystallized and \u0000uid intelligence both begin to show substantial age-related declines in the mid- to late-40s.", "B": "measures of crystallized intelligence show earlier age-related declines than measures of \u0000uid intelligence.", "C": "measures of perceptual speed and numeric ability begin to show age-related declines sooner than measures of vocabulary and verbal memory.", "D": "measures of inductive reasoning, spatial orientation, and verbal memory begin to show age-related declines in the mid- to late-20s."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Seattle Longitudinal Study (which used a cross-sequential design that combined cross-sectional and longitudinal methodologies) found\nthat most factors of intelligence remain relatively stable until the late 60s. For most people, only perceptual speed declined substantially prior\nto age 60. Numeric ability did not show a signi\u0000cant decline until after age 60. Inductive reasoning, spatial orientation, vocabulary, and verbal\nmemory remained fairly stable until about age 70 or 75.\nAnswer A: As crystallized intelligence, which would include vocabulary and verbal memory, tends to remain fairly stable until about age 70 or 75\nand components of \u0000uid reasoning tend to remain stable until close to age 60, this response is incorrect.\nAnswer B: As crystallized intelligence tends to show age-related declines later than \u0000uid intelligence, this response is incorrect.\nAnswer D: This response is incorrect. For most people, only perceptual speed declines substantially prior to age 60. Numeric ability does not\nshow a signi\u0000cant decline until after age 60. Inductive reasoning, spatial orientation, vocabulary, and verbal memory tend to remain fairly stable\nuntil about age 70 or 75."}, {"question": "The Working Memory Index of the WAIS-IV consists of which of the following subtests?", "options": {"A": "Digit Span, Arithmetic, and Letter-Number Sequencing", "B": "Vocabulary, Similarities, Information, and Comprehension", "C": "Symbol Search, Coding, and Cancellation", "D": "Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles, Figure Weights, and Picture Completion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The WAIS-IV provides scores on four Indexes -- Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory, and Processing Speed. Digit\nSpan, Arithmetic, and Letter-Number Sequencing are the subtests of the Working Memory Index.\nAnswer B: These are the Verbal Comprehension subtests.\nAnswer C: These are the Processing Speed subtests.\nAnswer D: These are the Perceptual Reasoning subtests."}, {"question": "Arthur Jensen's (1998) research on sources of variability in IQ indicated that the average IQ difference within families (as measured by the difference in IQs of full siblings living together) is _____ points.", "options": {"A": "4", "B": "11", "C": "18", "D": "26"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Unfortunately, the licensing exam will include three or four \"distant galaxy\" questions like this one that require you to be familiar with the details\nof a particular research study. When you encounter these questions in practice exams, try to memorize the information presented in those\nquestions. When you encounter them on the licensing exam, take a deep breath and see if you have any knowledge that will help you make an\n\"educated guess.\" Jensen found that the average IQ difference between siblings in the same family was 11 points. Answers A, C, and D: The\naverage IQ difference between siblings in the same family was 11 points. The average difference in IQs of the same person tested on two\noccasions a week apart was 4 points."}, {"question": "Scoring and interpretation of the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) usually involves considering which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Form quality and content", "B": "Emotional control/lability", "C": "Needs and press", "D": "General and speci\u0000c attitudes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is a projective personality test that was derived from Henry Murray's theory of needs. Most scoring and\ninterpretation systems for the TAT rely on Murray's concepts of needs (internal determinants of behavior), press (environmental determinants\nof behavior), and thema (interactions between needs and press).\nAnswer A: Form quality and content are two of the categories of Exner's comprehensive scoring system for the Rorschach test.\nAnswer B: While an examiner may gather information regarding an examinee's emotional reactivity, this was not one of Murray's main focuses.\nThus, this is not the best answer.\nAnswer D: General and speci\u0000c attitudes are not factors considered when scoring and interpreting TAT responses."}, {"question": "When conducting a functional behavioral assessment, a behavior's function is described in terms of:", "options": {"A": "the skills required to perform it.", "B": "the criteria used to measure it.", "C": "its antecedents and consequences.", "D": "its physical characteristics."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "As its name suggests, a primary goal of a functional behavioral assessment (FBA) is to determine the function of the target behavior. A\nfunctional behavioral assessment involves determining the environmental factors (antecedents and consequences) that contribute to its\noccurrence.\nAnswers A, B, and D: The term functional behavioral assessment refers to a type of behavioral assessment that entails determining the\nfunction or purpose of a behavior by identifying its antecedents and consequences. As such, these responses are incorrect."}, {"question": "An adult with traumatic brain injury is likely to obtain the lowest scores on which of the following WAIS-IV subtests?", "options": {"A": "Symbol Search, Coding, and Cancellation", "B": "Block Design, Visual Puzzles, and Picture Completion", "C": "Digit Span, Arithmetic, and Letter-Number Sequencing", "D": "Vocabulary, Similarities, and Information"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The WAIS-IV Technical Manual provides score pro\u0000les for various clinical groups. It indicates that individuals in several clinical groups -- e.g.,\ntraumatic brain injury, mild Alzheimer's dementia, and ADHD -- obtained the lowest score on the Processing Speed Index (PSI), which consists\nof the Symbol Search, Coding, and Cancellation subtests.\nAnswer B: Adults with traumatic brain injuries are likely to obtain the lowest scores on subtests comprising the Processing Speed Index (PSI).\nThe Block Design, Visual Puzzles, and Picture Completion subtests comprise the Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI).\nAnswer C: Adults with traumatic brain injuries are likely to obtain the lowest scores on subtests comprising the Processing Speed Index (PSI).\nThe Digit Span, Arithmetic, and Letter-Number Sequencing subtests comprise the Working Memory Index (WMI).\nAnswer D: Adults with traumatic brain injuries are likely to obtain the lowest scores on subtests comprising the Processing Speed Index (PSI).\nThe Vocabulary, Similarities, and Information subtests comprise the Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI). Scores within this index are in\u0000uenced\nthe least by traumatic brain injury."}, {"question": "In the assessment of cognitive abilities with a standardized test, you would most likely \"test the limits\":", "options": {"A": "before administering the test using standardized procedures.", "B": "as an alternative to administering the test using standardized procedures.", "C": "after administering the test using standardized procedures.", "D": "whenever it seems appropriate to do so."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Testing the limits was one of the early methods used to obtain additional qualitative information about an examinee's abilities. Testing the\nlimits is done after the entire test has been administered under standardized conditions. It is used to obtain additional information about an\nexaminee and may involve providing cues, suggestions, or feedback.\nAnswer A: This response is incorrect, considering that providing information or cues prior to administration would invalidate the results of the\nassessment.\nAnswer B: This response is incorrect, considering the main goal of a standardized procedure is to compare the results to a larger group. An\nexaminer would be unable to do this if they do not use the standardized procedure.\nAnswer D: This response is incorrect, considering that the goal of the standardized administration is to maintain the e\u0000cacy of the\nassessment. The examiner would only be able to do this by only providing additional information and feedback after the standardized\nadministration is over."}, {"question": "In the 1979 case of Larry P. v. Riles, the judge ruled that IQ tests could no longer be used to:", "options": {"A": "make hiring and other employment decisions.", "B": "make college admission decisions.", "C": "determine if older African American adults should be assigned a legal guardian.", "D": "determine if African American children should be placed in special education classes."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In the Larry P. v. Riles case, expert testimony led the judge to conclude that \"IQ tests are racially and culturally biased, [and] have a\ndiscriminatory impact on Black children.\" The court determined that the use of standardized IQ tests resulted in the placement of a\ndisproportionate number of African American children in special education classes, which prompted the judge to state that these tests should\nnot be used to assess African American children.\nAnswers A, B, and C: These answers are incorrect, considering that the Larry P. v. Riles case was based on identi\u0000cation that African American\nchildren were overrepresented in special education classes in the San Francisco public school system."}, {"question": "Which of the following statements is true about the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scales, 5th edition (SB5)?", "options": {"A": "The Full Scale IQ, Verbal IQ, Nonverbal IQ, and Cognitive Indexes have a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 16.", "B": "The test is appropriate only for individuals ages 5 through 65.", "C": "It provides scores on four factor indexes -- Working Memory, Verbal Comprehension, Processing Speed, and Perceptual Reasoning.", "D": "Its development was based on a hierarchical model of intelligence that begins with a global \"g\" factor."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the major characteristics of the SB5, including its age range and the scores that it provides. This\nanswer accurately describes the model underlying the SB5. The development of the SB5 was based on a hierarchical g (general mental ability)\nmodel that incorporates \u0000ve cognitive factors derived from the Cattell-Horn-Carroll theory of cognitive abilities.\nAnswer A: For the scores listed in this answer, the mean is 100 and the standard deviation is 15.\nAnswer B: The age range is 2 to 85+.\nAnswer C: These are the Indexes for the WAIS-IV, not the SB5. The SB5 has \u0000ve Cognitive Indexes -- Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative\nReasoning, Visual-Spatial Processing, and Working Memory."}, {"question": "Which of the following are related to the psychology of oppression?", "options": {"A": "Strength, learning, knowledge, and cultural individuality", "B": "Perception, willpower, self-surrender, and power status", "C": "Apathy, individualism, loss of identity, and group think", "D": "Motivation, emotions, ambitions, and ideals"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The psychology of oppression consists of the motivation, agency, perception, emotions, ambitions, ideals, reasoning, memory, aesthetics, and\nmorals that accept the oppressive social system, desire it, identify with it, take it for granted as normal and even as ideal, take pleasure in it,\ndefend it, and reject alternatives to it.\nAnswer A: These are not typically associated with the psychology of oppression.\nAnswer B: These are not typically associated with the psychology of oppression, with the exception of perception.\nAnswer C: These are not typically associated with the psychology of oppression."}, {"question": "A manager engaged in long-range planning for his company is fairly con\u0000dent about the strategic plan he has developed for the future but has some reservations about its acceptability. In this situation, the approach-avoidance con\u0000ict model predicts that, as it gets closer to the time to implement the plan:", "options": {"A": "the positive aspects of the plan will increase in strength for the manager while the negative aspects decrease.", "B": "the positive aspects of the plan will decrease in strength, while the negative aspects increase.", "C": "the positive and negative aspects of the plan will both increase in strength for the manager, but the negative aspects will increase more.", "D": "the positive and negative aspects of the plan will both decrease in strength for the manager, but the positive aspects will decrease more."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The \"approach-avoidance con\u0000ict\" occurs when a goal or decision/alternative has both positive and negative qualities. It predicts that as\ndistance from the goal decreases, the strength of both the \"approach gradient\" and the \"avoidance gradient\" increases. However, the strength\nof the avoidance gradient increases more rapidly. This would mean that, as one gets closer to the goal, the more likely one will choose to avoid\nit.\nAnswers A, B, and D: In the scenario described, as the manager gets closer to the goal, both positives and negatives of it will increase, but the\nnegative aspects will increase more rapidly."}, {"question": "__________ predicts that the decision to remain or leave a relationship depends on the relative costs and rewards of that relationship.", "options": {"A": "Social comparison theory", "B": "Self-veri\u0000cation theory", "C": "Social exchange theory", "D": "Gain-loss theory"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "\"Social exchange theory\" predicts that we're more likely to stay in a relationship when the rewards of the relationship exceed its costs.\nAnswer A: \"Social comparison theory\" predicts that, when people do not have objective standards for evaluating their own opinions and\nbehaviors, they tend to do so by comparing their opinions and behaviors to those of others.\nAnswer B: \"Self-veri\u0000cation theory\" predicts that people prefer to interact with others who provide them with feedback that is consistent with\ntheir self-views.\nAnswer D: \"The gain-loss effect\" predicts that our liking for another person is maximized when that person's initial evaluation of us is negative\nbut subsequently becomes positive. (For the exam, you want to be familiar with the basic assumptions and predictions of these four theories.)"}, {"question": "According to Bem's (1972) \"self-perception theory\":", "options": {"A": "our attitudes and behaviors are unrelated.", "B": "our emotions shape (cause) our attitudes and behaviors.", "C": "we infer our attitudes from our behaviors.", "D": "we rely on our attitudes to choose a behavior."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "According to Bem (1972), people infer their own attitudes and emotions from their behaviors. Bem's theory contradicts the more traditional\nview that attitudes determine behaviors.\nAnswers A, B, and D: According to self-perception theory, we infer our attitudes from our behaviors."}, {"question": "Berkowitz (1971) added which of the following to the original \"frustration-aggression hypothesis\"?", "options": {"A": "The role of aggressive cues", "B": "The impact of the model's attractiveness", "C": "The role of the individual's level of frustration tolerance", "D": "The likelihood that aggressive behavior will be rewarded or punished"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The frustration-aggression hypothesis proposed that frustration triggers a readiness to act aggressively. Berkowitz (1971) extended the\noriginal theory by proposing that unjusti\u0000ed frustration leads to anger and that this anger is most likely to elicit aggression in the presence of\naggressive cues.\nAnswers B, C, and D: The theory was extended to include the role of aggressive cues in acting aggressively."}, {"question": "The impact of social roles on behavior was demonstrated by:", "options": {"A": "Berkowitz's research on the frustration-aggression hypothesis.", "B": "Latane and Darley's research on bystander apathy.", "C": "Zimbardo's prison study.", "D": "Sherif's Robber's Cave study."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Zimbardo's (1972) \"prison study\" demonstrated that, under certain conditions, people will behave in uncharacteristically irrational and\nunacceptable ways that are consistent with assigned social roles.\nAnswers A, B, and D: Zimbardo's prison study was famous for the information it yielded on social roles and behavior."}, {"question": "According to \"attribution theory,\" a happy, versus depressed, person, would likely attribute a negative circumstance to:", "options": {"A": "external, stable, and global factors.", "B": "internal, stable, and speci\u0000c factors.", "C": "external, unstable, and global factors.", "D": "external, unstable, and speci\u0000c factors."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Studies on attribution theory have found that depressed people tend to make attributions based on internal, stable, and global factors when\nevaluating negative events. A happy person would tend to make attributions based on external, unstable, and speci\u0000c factors.\nAnswers A, B, and C. According the this theory, happy people would tend to attribute a negative circumstance to external, unstable, and speci\u0000c\nfactors."}, {"question": "According to the \"overjusti\u0000cation hypothesis\":", "options": {"A": "external rewards increase intrinsic interest.", "B": "external rewards have no effect on intrinsic interest.", "C": "external rewards decrease intrinsic interest.", "D": "external rewards are essential to getting someone to perform a task."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The overjusti\u0000cation hypothesis predicts that, when an external reward is given to a person for performing an intrinsically rewarding activity, the\nperson's intrinsic motivation to perform the activity will decrease.\nAnswers A, B, and D: Offering an external reward for performing an intrinsically rewarding activity has been shown to decrease intrinsic\nmotivation to perform the task."}, {"question": "Lewin's \"\u0000eld theory\" predicts that:", "options": {"A": "human behavior is due more to physical than to psychological factors.", "B": "a leader's power is directly affected by the characteristics of the task and environment.", "C": "human behavior is a function of both the person and his/her environment.", "D": "a group can be no more effective than its least effective member."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "According to Lewin's \u0000eld theory, behavior is a function of the person and the environment. Lewin expressed this relationship with the following\nformula: B = f(P, E).\nAnswers A, B, and D: According to \u0000eld theory, behavior is a function of the person and his/her environment."}, {"question": "Sherif et al.'s (1961) \"Robber's Cave\" study is considered important because of the information it provided on:", "options": {"A": "social facilitation and inhibition.", "B": "competition and cooperation.", "C": "bystander apathy.", "D": "conformity to group norms."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Sherif et al.'s (1961) study was one of the \u0000rst that investigated factors associated with intergroup competition. Sherif and his colleagues\nfound that introducing a \"superordinate goal\" (one that could be accomplished only when members of two groups cooperated) was the most\neffective way for reducing intergroup competition and hostility.\nAnswers A, C, and D: This study was important because of the information it provided on competition and cooperation."}, {"question": "Dr. Chang evaluates the relationship between a child's behavior and peer rejection of the child by observing the behavior of children previously classi\u0000ed as rejected or non-rejected in free-play situations over several days. During each observation period, Dr. Chang observes the \u0000rst child on a list for 10 seconds and then records the child's behavior, observes the next child on the list for 10 seconds and records their behavior, and so on until all children have been observed. Which of the following strategies is Dr. Chang using?", "options": {"A": "Time sampling", "B": "Time series", "C": "Sequential act coding", "D": "Multiple baseline"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In the situation described in this question, Dr. Chang is observing and recording each child's behavior during speci\u0000c periods of time. Time\nsampling involves observing an individual or group of individuals during pre-speci\u0000ed periods of time and, at the end of each observation,\nrecording whether or not the target behavior(s) occurred at all during that observation period. Time sampling is usually categorized as a type of\ninterval recording.\nAnswer B: When using a time series design, a researcher would assess the dependent (outcome) variable several times at regular intervals\nbefore and after administration of the independent variable.\nAnswer C: Sequential act coding involves recording events or behaviors in the order in which they occur.\nAnswer D: A multiple baseline design is a single-case (single-subject) design that involves sequentially applying a treatment across baselines\n(i.e., across participants, behaviors, or settings)."}, {"question": "A researcher wants to compare the effects of four different prevention programs on willingness to use safe-sex practices for sexually active male and female adolescents. The researcher will randomly assign participants to one of the four programs and six months later, will ask participants if they engaged in safe sex all of the time, some of the time, or never. The researchers are also including gender as a variable. Which of the following is the appropriate statistical test for analyzing the data obtained?", "options": {"A": "One-way ANOVA", "B": "Two-way ANOVA", "C": "Multiple-sample chi-square test", "D": "Single-sample chi-square test"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This study has two independent variables (prevention program and gender) and one dependent variable (safe-sex practices). While the\ndependent variable is actually measured on an ordinal scale, no ordinal tests are listed in the responses. It is always possible to treat an ordinal\nvariable as a nominal one but it is not acceptable to treat it as an interval or ratio variable. Of the tests listed, the multiple-sample chi-square is\nthe best choice.\nAnswer A: The one-way ANOVA is used with one IV and one DV that is measured on an interval or ratio scale. Because the DV is measured on\nthe ordinal scale, the one-way ANOVA would not be an appropriate test for this type of data.\nAnswer B: The two-way ANOVA is used with two IVs and one DV that is measured on an interval or ratio scale. Because the DV is measured on\nthe ordinal scale, the two-way ANOVA would not be an appropriate test for this type of data.\nAnswer D: The single sample chi-square test is used when a study includes only one nominal variable and this study involves more than one\nnominal variable. For additional information on the use of the chi-square tests, see the Statistics and Research Design chapter of the written\nstudy materials."}, {"question": "With which of the following research designs is \"autocorrelation\" most likely to be a problem?", "options": {"A": "Solomon four-group", "B": "Repeated measures", "C": "Between groups", "D": "Double-blind"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Autocorrelation refers to the correlation between measurements of the dependent variable when the dependent variable is repeatedly\nadministered to the same subjects. Autocorrelation is a problem in repeated measures designs because it can arti\u0000cially in\u0000ate the value of\nthe inferential statistic and thereby increase the probability of making a Type I error.\nAnswer A: We use this design to evaluate the impact of pretesting and does not relate to in\u0000ated statistical values and the increased\nprobability of making a Type I error.\nAnswer C: Between groups designs do not relate directly to in\u0000ated values and the increased probability of making a Type I error.\nAnswer D: A double-blind study is where neither the researchers nor the participants know which participants get the treatment until after the\nstudy and is designed to control for bias. It does not relate to in\u0000ated values or the increased probability of making a Type I error."}, {"question": "Which of the following correlation coe\u0000cients would you use to determine the degree of association between two continuous variables that have been arti\u0000cially dichotomized?", "options": {"A": "Eta", "B": "Biserial", "C": "Tetrachoric", "D": "Contingency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "For the licensing exam, you should be familiar with the uses of the four correlation coe\u0000cients listed in the answers to this question. The\ntetrachoric correlation coe\u0000cient is used to determine the relationship between two normally distributed continuous variables that have been\narti\u0000cially dichotomized. For example, it would be used to assess the degree of association between treatment outcome and symptom severity,\nwhen both variables were originally measured on a continuous scale but were then dichotomized so that outcome is categorized as either\nsuccessful or unsuccessful and symptom severity is categorized as either mild or severe.\nAnswer A: Eta is used to determine the relationship between two continuous variables when the relationship is nonlinear.\nAnswer B: The biserial correlation coe\u0000cient is used to determine the relationship between one continuous variable and one arti\u0000cially\ndichotomized variable.\nAnswer D: The contingency correlation coe\u0000cient is used to determine the relationship between two nominal variables."}, {"question": "The Bonferroni test helps control the experiment-wise error rate by_____.", "options": {"A": "controlling the total number of comparisons that can be made", "B": "reducing the level of signi\u0000cance for each comparison", "C": "permitting individual comparisons only after the omnibus test has produced signi\u0000cant results", "D": "requiring that all comparisons be conducted as two-tailed tests"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to have the Bonferroni test associated with planned (a priori) comparisons. The greater the number of planned\ncomparisons, the greater the likelihood that a Type I error will be made. The Bonferroni test controls this possibility by reducing the level of\nalpha for each comparison.\nAnswer A: The Bonferroni test controls for the greater likelihood of making Type I errors when making multiple comparisons with the same\ndata by reducing the level of alpha for each comparison.\nAnswer C: The Bonferroni test takes the place of the omnibus test (ANOVA) and post-hoc comparisons.\nAnswer D: Conducting multiple two tailed tests on the same data would substantially increase the likelihood of making a Type I error."}, {"question": "Which of the following is true about the standard error of the mean?", "options": {"A": "It increases as the standard deviation decreases.", "B": "It is not affected by the size of the standard deviation.", "C": "It decreases as sample size increases.", "D": "It decreases as the number of samples increases."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The standard error of the mean is the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the mean. It is equal to the population standard\ndeviation divided by the square root of the sample size. As sample size increases, the standard error decreases.\nAnswer A: As the population standard deviation increases or decreases, the standard error of the mean changes in the same direction (i.e., as\none increases, the other increases).\nAnswer B: The standard error of the mean is affected by the standard deviation, so changes in the standard deviation will impact the standard\nerror.\nAnswer D: The standard error of the mean is affected by the size (not the number) of samples."}, {"question": "Dr. Stanley Stat conducts a meta-analysis of the research on the effects of a behavioral treatment for ADHD for adults and obtains an average effect size (Cohen's d) of .50. Based on these results, he concludes that the effect size for adults is twice the effect size previously reported in a meta-analysis of the research on the effects of this treatment for adolescents. Based on this information, what was the most likely effect size for adolescents?", "options": {"A": "0.25", "B": "0.7", "C": "1", "D": "-0.5"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Knowing that Cohen's d indicates the difference between groups (usually treatment and no treatment groups) in terms of standard deviation\nunits would have helped you identify the correct answer to this question. A standard deviation of .50 is twice the size of a standard deviation of\n.25.\nAnswer B: A standard deviation of .50 is twice the size of a standard deviation of .25.\nAnswer C: A standard deviation of .50 is twice the size of a standard deviation of .25.\nAnswer D: A standard deviation of .50 is twice the size of a standard deviation of .25. When calculating Cohen's d, if the second mean (M2) is\nlarger than the \u0000rst mean (M1) then your effect size will be negative."}, {"question": "A Type I error occurs when, on the basis of a study's results, a researcher_____.", "options": {"A": "retains a false null hypothesis", "B": "rejects a false null hypothesis", "C": "retains a true null hypothesis", "D": "rejects a true null hypothesis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "A Type I error is when a researcher rejects a true null hypothesis. For the exam, you want to be familiar with Type I and Type II errors. The\nStatistics and Research Design chapter of the written study materials includes a decision outcome table that might help you memorize the\ndifference between these errors.\nAnswer A: Retaining a false null hypothesis is referred to as a Type II error.\nAnswer B: The ability to reject a false null hypothesis is referred to as power.\nAnswer C: Retaining a true null hypothesis is a correct decision."}, {"question": "When using protocol analysis to assess problem-solving in adolescents, a psychologist would ask each adolescent to_____.", "options": {"A": "assemble a representative set of task assignments that the psychologist will review", "B": "report the \u0000rst answer he/she thinks of when presented with a problem", "C": "describe how he/she determined the solution after working on a problem", "D": "think aloud while solving a problem"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Having a person think-aloud while solving a problem, recording the person's verbalizations, and then analyzing the transcript of that recording\nare the procedures used in protocol analysis.\nAnswer A: This sounds like portfolio assessment.\nAnswer B: This sounds like free association.\nAnswer C: This is called \"retrospective debrie\u0000ng.\""}, {"question": "To determine if there is a statistically signi\u0000cant pattern in the effect of time on memory for a list of nonsense syllables, you have subjects memorize a list of 15 syllables and test their memory at 15-minute intervals for the next two hours. The best technique for analyzing the data collected is_____.", "options": {"A": "multiple regression", "B": "factorial ANOVA", "C": "orthogonal analysis", "D": "trend analysis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In this situation, you want to determine if there is a pattern (or \"trend\") in forgetting the list of nonsense syllables. Of the techniques listed, only\ntrend analysis (a type of analysis of variance that is used when the IV is quantitative) would be useful for determining if there is a pattern or\ntrend in subjects' memory for the list of nonsense syllables over time.\nAnswer A: Multiple regression is appropriate when we want to predict the value of one variable dependent (criterion) variable based on the\nvalue of two or more independent (predictor) variables.\nAnswer B: A factorial ANOVA design is where each level, of each factor, is paired up with each level of every other factor.\nAnswer C: Orthogonal analysis is used to determine the independence of variables by examining the assumption that there is no correlation\nbetween them."}, {"question": "When using structural equation modeling, the \"\u0000t\" between the proposed causal model and the obtained data can be evaluated using the chi-square test for goodness-of-\u0000t. However, one problem with using the chi-square test for this purpose is that_____.", "options": {"A": "it is very sensitive to the sample size", "B": "it is very insensitive to the sample size", "C": "it is di\u0000cult to interpret when there are more than three predictors", "D": "it is di\u0000cult to interpret when there are more than three criteria"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "This question addresses a very advanced topic from the area of statistics. Fortunately, you won't encounter very many questions of this\ndi\u0000culty level on the licensing exam. The chi-square test is very sensitive to sample size, with large samples producing statistically signi\u0000cant\nresults even when there is a small difference between the proposed model and the obtained data. Because the goal in using the chi-square test\nin this situation is to NOT obtain a signi\u0000cant difference (i.e., you want the data to \u0000t the model), a large sample can be problematic.\nAnswer B: This is the opposite of the correct answer. The chi-square test is very sensitive to sample size, with large samples producing\nstatistically signi\u0000cant results even when there is a small difference between the proposed model and the obtained data.\nAnswer C: The chi-square test is very sensitive to sample size, with large samples producing statistically signi\u0000cant results even when there is\na small difference between the proposed model and the obtained data.\nAnswer D: The chi-square test is very sensitive to sample size, with large samples producing statistically signi\u0000cant results even when there is\na small difference between the proposed model and the obtained data."}, {"question": "To use two or more categorical variables to predict status on a single categorical variable, you would use which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Path analysis", "B": "Logit analysis", "C": "Multiple regression analysis", "D": "Canonical correlation analysis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Logit analysis is used to explain the relationship between one binary dependent variable and one or more independent variables on a nominal,\nordinal or ratio scale. If you're familiar with the multivariate techniques described in the Statistics and Research Design chapter, you may have\nbeen able to identify the correct answer to this question through the process of elimination.\nAnswer A: Path analysis is used to test hypotheses about the causal relationships among three or more variables.\nAnswer C: Multiple regression requires the predictors and criterion to be measured on a continuous scale.\nAnswer D: Canonical correlation is used when there are multiple IVs and multiple DVs that are all measured on a continuous scale."}, {"question": "You conduct an item analysis of the items you plan to include in a new test of computer knowledge. You obtain a p value of .85 for item #47. This indicates that this item:", "options": {"A": "is di\u0000cult.", "B": "is easy.", "C": "has poor discrimination.", "D": "has good discrimination."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "In the context of test construction, \"p\" is an index of item di\u0000culty and is calculated by dividing the total number of examinees who answered\nthe item correctly by the total number of examinees. The closer the p value is to 1.0, the easier the item since this means that a large\nproportion of examinees answered the item correctly.\nAnswer A: A low p value indicates a di\u0000cult item.\nAnswer C: Discrimination is determined by calculating D (subtracting the proportion of people in the low-scoring group from the proportion in\nthe high-scoring group). Extremely di\u0000cult or extremely easy items typically have poor ability to discriminate.\nAnswer D: Items with moderate di\u0000culty levels (around .50) have the greatest potential for maximum discrimination."}, {"question": "A screening test designed to identify individuals with traumatic brain injury identi\u0000es a number of individuals as having brain injury when, in fact, they do not. These individuals are:", "options": {"A": "false positives.", "B": "true positives.", "C": "false negatives.", "D": "true negatives."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In the context of decision-making theory, individuals may be classi\u0000ed as false positives, true positives, false negatives, or true negatives based\non their performance on the predictor and the criterion. In the situation described in this question, the screening test is the predictor and the\nactual diagnosis/condition is the criterion. False positives are individuals identi\u0000ed by the screening test as having brain injury when, in fact,\nthey do not.\nAnswer B: True positives are individuals identi\u0000ed by the screening test as having brain injury when, in fact, they do.\nAnswer C: False negatives are individuals identi\u0000ed as not having brain injury when, in fact, they do.\nAnswer D: True negatives are individuals identi\u0000ed as not having brain injury when, in fact, they do not."}, {"question": "Which of the following would NOT be useful for increasing a test's reliability coe\u0000cient?", "options": {"A": "Increasing the sample variance", "B": "Ensuring that the average p value is close to .50", "C": "Increasing the heterogeneity of the content domain", "D": "Increasing the number of items from 50 to 100"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "A test's reliability coe\u0000cient, like any other correlation coe\u0000cient, is affected by a number of factors. Increasing the heterogeneity of the\ncontent domain will have the effect of decreasing the reliability coe\u0000cient, especially the coe\u0000cient of internal consistency.\nAnswer A: Increasing sample variability (heterogeneity) maximizes the magnitude of the reliability coe\u0000cient.\nAnswer B: To maximize the range of scores on a test, the items should vary with regard to di\u0000cultly level, with the average di\u0000culty level being\nabout .50.\nAnswer D: Longer tests provide more reliable information."}, {"question": "When a test has high sensitivity, this means that there is a:", "options": {"A": "low chance of false negatives and high chance of false positives.", "B": "low chance of false negatives and low chance of false positives.", "C": "high chance of false negatives and high chance of false positives.", "D": "high chance of false negatives and low chance of false positives."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Sensitivity refers to the proportion of people with the condition who are correctly identi\u0000ed by the test and is calculated by dividing the true\npositives by the true positives plus false negatives. When the sensitivity is high, this means that most of the people with the disorder will be\nidenti\u0000ed as having the disorder by the test (i.e., there will be few false negatives) but that there will be some people without the disorder who\nwill also be identi\u0000ed as having the disorder (i.e., there will be some false positives).\nAnswer B: This does not describe sensitivity.\nAnswer C: This does not describe sensitivity.\nAnswer D: This describes speci\u0000city, not sensitivity. Speci\u0000city refers to the proportion of people who do not have the condition who are\ncorrectly identi\u0000ed by the test. When a test has high speci\u0000city, there is a high chance of false negatives and low chance of false positives."}, {"question": "When using __________, examinees who obtain scores that fall within a speci\u0000ed range of scores are considered to have received identical scores.", "options": {"A": "top-down selection", "B": "within-group norming", "C": "separate cutoff scores", "D": "banding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Banding is used to treat examinees' scores within a speci\u0000ed range as identical. Banding is being used, for example, when a band is de\u0000ned as\n91 to 100 points and examinees who receive scores of 91, 95, and 99 are treated the same.\nAnswers A, B, and C are incorrect, as the procedures listed in these answers are methods of score adjustment that are used to take group\ndifferences into account when assigning or interpreting scores."}, {"question": "When factors in a factor matrix are \"oblique,\" this means that:", "options": {"A": "the correlation coe\u0000cient for any two factors is equal to zero.", "B": "the correlation coe\u0000cient for any two factors is greater than zero.", "C": "the identi\u0000ed factors explain a statistically signi\u0000cant amount of variability in test scores.", "D": "the identi\u0000ed factors do not explain a statistically signi\u0000cant amount of variability in test scores."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to know the difference between orthogonal and oblique factors. In the context of factor analysis, oblique means\n\"correlated,\" so the correlation coe\u0000cient for any two factors would be greater than zero.\nAnswer A: In the context of factor analysis, orthogonal means \"uncorrelated, \" so the correlation coe\u0000cient for any two factors would be equal\nto zero.\nAnswers C and D are incorrect. A factor matrix includes information about communalities, or the total amount of variability in test scores that\nis accounted for by all of the identi\u0000ed factors. A factor matrix is not designed to test statistical signi\u0000cance."}, {"question": "In the context of factor analysis, \"speci\u0000city\" refers to:", "options": {"A": "the proportion of variability in a test that has not been explained by the factor analysis.", "B": "the proportion of variability in a test that has been explained by a single factor.", "C": "the proportion of variability in a test that has been explained by all of the identi\u0000ed factors.", "D": "the proportion of variability in a test that is attributable to measurement error."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "From the perspective of factor analysis, variability in test scores is due to a combination of communality, speci\u0000city, and error. In factor\nanalysis, a test's speci\u0000city is the variability that is due to factors that are speci\u0000c to the test and not measured by any other test included in the\nanalysis -- i.e., variability that is not accounted for by the identi\u0000ed factors.\nAnswer B: This describes a factor loading.\nAnswer C: This describes a test's communality, or the amount of variability in test scores that is accounted for by all of the identi\u0000ed factors.\nAnswer D: This describes measurement error in factor analysis."}, {"question": "Which type of reliability is most appropriate to measure the reliability of a multiple-choice speed test?", "options": {"A": "Split-half reliability", "B": "Alternate forms reliability", "C": "Coe\u0000cient alpha", "D": "Kuder-Richardson Formula 20"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Speed tests are designed so that all items answered by an examinee are answered correctly and the examinee's total score depends primarily\non his/her speed of responding. Because of the nature of these tests, a measure of internal consistency will provide a spuriously high estimate\nof the test's reliability. Alternate-forms reliability is an appropriate method for establishing the reliability of speed tests. Internal consistency\nreliability is generally not used to assess the reliability of speed tests because it produces a spuriously high-reliability coe\u0000cient. For the exam,\nbe sure to remember that split-half and other forms of internal consistency reliability overestimate the reliability of a speed test.\nAnswer A: Split-half reliability is a type of internal consistency reliability and is not appropriate for speed tests.\nAnswer C: Coe\u0000cient alpha is a type of internal consistency reliability and is less appropriate for speed tests.\nAnswer D: The Kuder-Richardson Formula 20 (KR-20) is a method for assessing internal consistency reliability when test items are scored\ndichotomously and is not used to measure the reliability of speed tests."}, {"question": "When the results of a test developer's validity study con\u0000rm that her new measure of an attribute correlates highly with an established measure of the same attribute and does not correlate highly with established measures of unrelated attributes, the test developer has evidence of her test's:", "options": {"A": "content validity.", "B": "construct validity.", "C": "internal validity.", "D": "external validity."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A test has construct validity when it measures the construct (trait or attribute) it was designed to measure. It is established by gathering\nevidence by, for example, determining if the test has adequate convergent and divergent validity: A test has convergent validity when its scores\nhave high correlations with scores on measures of the same and related constructs, and it has divergent validity when its scores have low\ncorrelations with scores on measures of unrelated constructs.\nAnswer A: Content validity is established primarily by having subject matter experts review test items to make sure they are a representative\nsample of the content domain(s) assessed by the test.\nAnswer C: Internal and external validity refer to the validity of research studies, not tests. A research study has adequate internal validity when\nits results allow a researcher to conclude that there is a cause-effect relationship between the independent and dependent variables.\nAnswer D: Internal and external validity refer to the validity of research studies, not tests. A research study has adequate external validity when\nit allows the researcher to generalize conclusions about the cause-effect relationship to other people and conditions."}, {"question": "Item response theory is considered a useful technique for constructing all of the following types of tests except:", "options": {"A": "criterion-keyed tests.", "B": "tailored (computerized) tests.", "C": "diagnostic or employment screening tests.", "D": "teacher-made (classroom) achievement tests."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Item response theory is theoretically and mathematically very complex, which limits its use. Use of item response theory for test construction\nrequires constructing an \"item response curve\" for each item. To do so, a large sample of examinees is needed, which would probably not be\navailable for classroom tests.\nAnswers A, B, and C are incorrect. The types of tests have been identi\u0000ed as good candidates for item response theory."}]};
